(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("linux",{"linux/filesystem-and-navigation":{"html":"\u003cp>It is 02:10 and an alert says the checkout service is serving an old version of its config. You SSH into the server. Before you change anything you need to answer three questions: where am I, what is here, and what changed? This lesson gives you the map and the commands to answer them in under a minute.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The standard directories and what belongs in each\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to move around, list and search without guessing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to copy, move and delete without destroying data\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How symbolic links work, and how a stale link breaks a deployment\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"one-tree-many-purposes\">One tree, many purposes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Linux has a single directory tree that starts at \u003ccode>/\u003c/code> (root). There are no drive letters. Disks, USB sticks and network shares are \u003cem>mounted\u003c/em> into the tree at a directory you choose. Knowing the standard layout tells you where to look before you search.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Path\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>What lives there\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>You will touch it when\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>/etc\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>System-wide configuration files\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Changing service settings\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>/var/log\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Log files\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Investigating a failure\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>/var/lib\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Application state, such as databases\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Checking data size or restoring\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>/home\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>One directory per user\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Looking at SSH keys or shell history\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>/usr/bin\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>/usr/sbin\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Installed programs\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Checking which binary runs\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>/opt\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Self-contained third-party or in-house apps\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Finding deployed releases\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>/tmp\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Temporary files, often cleared on reboot\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Debugging scratch files\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>/proc\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>/sys\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Live kernel and process information, not real files\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Reading CPU, memory, process data\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>/dev\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Device files such as disks and terminals\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Working with disks\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-why-it-matters\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Why it matters\u003c/div>\u003cp>Configuration is almost always under \u003ccode>/etc\u003c/code>, logs under \u003ccode>/var/log\u003c/code>, and application data under \u003ccode>/var/lib\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>/opt\u003c/code>. When a vendor puts things elsewhere, that is the exception worth noting.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003ch2 id=\"move-around-and-look\">Move around and look\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"pwd\ncd /var/log\nls -lah\ncd -\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">pwd\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">/home/deploy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cd\u003c/span> /var/log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> -lah\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">total 4.2M\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">drwxrwxr-x  9 root   syslog 4.0K Oct 24 00:00 .\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">drwxr-xr-x 14 root   root   4.0K Sep  2 11:20 ..\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-rw-r-----  1 syslog adm    312K Oct 24 02:09 auth.log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-rw-r-----  1 syslog adm    1.1M Oct 24 02:10 syslog\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">drwxr-xr-x  2 root   root   4.0K Oct 23 06:25 nginx\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cd\u003c/span> -\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">/home/deploy\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Read the \u003ccode>ls -l\u003c/code> columns left to right: file type and permissions, link count, owner, group, size, modified time, name. The first character is the type: \u003ccode>-\u003c/code> file, \u003ccode>d\u003c/code> directory, \u003ccode>l\u003c/code> symbolic link. \u003ccode>-h\u003c/code> prints human-readable sizes and \u003ccode>-a\u003c/code> shows hidden files, which start with a dot. \u003ccode>cd -\u003c/code> jumps back to the previous directory.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The output above is sample output. Names, sizes and dates on your machine will differ.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"find-things\">Find things\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>You will often know a file name but not its location, or the reverse.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"find /etc -name &quot;nginx.conf&quot;\nfind /var/log -type f -mtime -1 -size +1M\ngrep -rn &quot;listen 443&quot; /etc/nginx\ntype -a python3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>find /etc -name \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;nginx.conf&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">/etc/nginx/nginx.conf\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>find /var/log -\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">type\u003c/span> f -mtime -1 -size +1M\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">/var/log/syslog\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -rn \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;listen 443&quot;\u003c/span> /etc/nginx\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">/etc/nginx/sites-enabled/shop.conf:4:    listen 443 ssl;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">type\u003c/span> -a python3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">python3 is /usr/bin/python3\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>find\u003c/code> walks the tree and filters by name, type, age or size. \u003ccode>grep -rn\u003c/code> searches \u003cem>inside\u003c/em> files recursively and prints line numbers. \u003ccode>type -a\u003c/code> shows which executable will actually run when you type a command, which settles most &quot;wrong version&quot; arguments.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"copy-move-and-delete-without-regret\">Copy, move and delete without regret\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"cp -a /etc/nginx /root/nginx.bak.2026-10-24   # -a keeps permissions, owners and links\nmv app.conf app.conf.old                       # rename in place\nrm -i notes.txt                                # ask before deleting\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cp\u003c/span> -a /etc/nginx /root/nginx.bak.2026-10-24   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># -a keeps permissions, owners and links\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">mv\u003c/span> app.conf app.conf.old                       \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># rename in place\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rm\u003c/span> -i notes.txt                                \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># ask before deleting\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-warning\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Warning\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>rm\u003c/code> does not use a recycle bin. \u003ccode>rm -rf\u003c/code> with a typo or an empty variable, such as \u003ccode>rm -rf &quot;$DIR&quot;/\u003c/code>, can erase far more than you meant. Before deleting, run the same pattern with \u003ccode>ls\u003c/code> and read the list. Always make a copy before editing a production config.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003ch2 id=\"links-one-name-pointing-at-another\">Links: one name pointing at another\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>symbolic link\u003c/strong> (symlink) is a small file that holds a path. A \u003cstrong>hard link\u003c/strong> is a second name for the same data on disk.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/shop.conf /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/shop.conf\nls -l /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ln\u003c/span> -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/shop.conf /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/shop.conf\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> -l /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">lrwxrwxrwx 1 root root 38 Oct 20 08:12 shop.conf -&gt; /etc/nginx/sites-available/shop.conf\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>If you delete the target of a symlink, the link keeps pointing at nothing and is called \u003cem>broken\u003c/em>. Hard links cannot cross filesystems, and deleting one name does not remove the data until every name is gone.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-new-release-is-deployed-but-the-old-config-is-live\">Incident: the new release is deployed but the old config is live\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The deploy script finished without errors, yet the service still behaves like last week&#39;s build. Symlinks are a common cause because many teams point \u003ccode>current\u003c/code> at the active release.\u003c/p>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A deployment tool creates \u003ccode>/opt/shop/releases/&lt;id&gt;\u003c/code> and then swaps a \u003ccode>current\u003c/code> link. The service reads \u003ccode>/opt/shop/current/config.yml\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ls -l /opt/shop\nls /opt/shop/releases\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> -l /opt/shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">total 8\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">lrwxrwxrwx 1 deploy deploy   26 Oct 14 16:02 current -&gt; releases/2026-10-14-1a2b3c\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">drwxr-xr-x 4 deploy deploy 4096 Oct 21 09:14 releases\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> /opt/shop/releases\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2026-10-14-1a2b3c  2026-10-21-9f8e7d\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The newest release, \u003ccode>2026-10-21-9f8e7d\u003c/code>, exists, but \u003ccode>current\u003c/code> still points at the older one. The link was never updated. Repoint it, then verify:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ln -sfn releases/2026-10-21-9f8e7d /opt/shop/current\nreadlink -f /opt/shop/current\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ln\u003c/span> -sfn releases/2026-10-21-9f8e7d /opt/shop/current\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">readlink\u003c/span> -f /opt/shop/current\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">/opt/shop/releases/2026-10-21-9f8e7d\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>-s\u003c/code> makes a symlink, \u003ccode>-f\u003c/code> replaces an existing one, and \u003ccode>-n\u003c/code> treats a link to a directory as a plain file. Without \u003ccode>-n\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>ln\u003c/code> would create a new link \u003cem>inside\u003c/em> the old release directory instead of replacing \u003ccode>current\u003c/code>. After the swap, restart the service so it re-reads its files.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>To prevent a repeat, make the deploy script verify the link with \u003ccode>readlink -f\u003c/code> and fail if it does not match the new release.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Editing a config in \u003ccode>/etc\u003c/code> without a backup, then forgetting what the original said\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using \u003ccode>rm -rf\u003c/code> with an unquoted or empty variable\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Searching with \u003ccode>find /\u003c/code> on a busy server instead of a narrower directory\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting \u003ccode>-n\u003c/code> when replacing a symlink that points to a directory\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"one-tree-many-purposes","text":"One tree, many purposes"},{"id":"move-around-and-look","text":"Move around and look"},{"id":"find-things","text":"Find things"},{"id":"copy-move-and-delete-without-regret","text":"Copy, move and delete without regret"},{"id":"links-one-name-pointing-at-another","text":"Links: one name pointing at another"},{"id":"incident-the-new-release-is-deployed-but-the-old-config-is-live","text":"Incident: the new release is deployed but the old config is live"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Filesystem Hierarchy Standard 3.0","u":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS_3.0/fhs/index.html"},{"t":"Linux man-pages (man7.org)","u":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/index.html"},{"t":"GNU Bash reference manual","u":"https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/bash.html"}],"text":"It is 02:10 and an alert says the checkout service is serving an old version of its config. You SSH into the server. Before you change anything you need to answer three questions: where am I, what is here, and what changed? This lesson gives you the map and the commands to answer them in under a minute. What you will learn The standard directories and what belongs in each How to move around, list and search without guessing How to copy, move and delete without destroying data How symbolic links work, and how a stale link breaks a deployment One tree, many purposes Linux has a single directory tree that starts at / (root). There are no drive letters. Disks, USB sticks and network shares are mounted into the tree at a directory you choose. Knowing the standard layout tells you where to look before you search. Path What lives there You will touch it when /etc System-wide configuration files Changing service settings /var/log Log files Investigating a failure /var/lib Application state, such as databases Checking data size or restoring /home One directory per user Looking at SSH keys or shell history /usr/bin , /usr/sbin Installed programs Checking which binary runs /opt Self-contained third-party or in-house apps Finding deployed releases /tmp Temporary files, often cleared on reboot Debugging scratch files /proc , /sys Live kernel and process information, not real files Reading CPU, memory, process data /dev Device files such as disks and terminals Working with disks Why it matters Configuration is almost always under /etc , logs under /var/log , and application data under /var/lib or /opt . When a vendor puts things elsewhere, that is the exception worth noting. Move around and look $ pwd /home/deploy $ cd /var/log $ ls -lah total 4.2M drwxrwxr-x 9 root syslog 4.0K Oct 24 00:00 . drwxr-xr-x 14 root root 4.0K Sep 2 11:20 .. -rw-r----- 1 syslog adm 312K Oct 24 02:09 auth.log -rw-r----- 1 syslog adm 1.1M Oct 24 02:10 syslog drwxr-xr-x 2 root root 4.0K Oct 23 06:25 nginx $ cd - /home/deploy Read the ls -l columns left to right: file type and permissions, link count, owner, group, size, modified time, name. The first character is the type: - file, d directory, l symbolic link. -h prints human-readable sizes and -a shows hidden files, which start with a dot. cd - jumps back to the previous directory. The output above is sample output. Names, sizes and dates on your machine will differ. Find things You will often know a file name but not its location, or the reverse. $ find /etc -name \"nginx.conf\" /etc/nginx/nginx.conf $ find /var/log - type f -mtime -1 -size +1M /var/log/syslog $ grep -rn \"listen 443\" /etc/nginx /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/shop.conf:4: listen 443 ssl; $ type -a python3 python3 is /usr/bin/python3 find walks the tree and filters by name, type, age or size. grep -rn searches inside files recursively and prints line numbers. type -a shows which executable will actually run when you type a command, which settles most \"wrong version\" arguments. Copy, move and delete without regret cp -a /etc/nginx /root/nginx.bak.2026-10-24 # -a keeps permissions, owners and links mv app.conf app.conf.old # rename in place rm -i notes.txt # ask before deleting Warning rm does not use a recycle bin. rm -rf with a typo or an empty variable, such as rm -rf \"$DIR\"/ , can erase far more than you meant. Before deleting, run the same pattern with ls and read the list. Always make a copy before editing a production config. Links: one name pointing at another A symbolic link (symlink) is a small file that holds a path. A hard link is a second name for the same data on disk. $ ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/shop.conf /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/shop.conf $ ls -l /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/ lrwxrwxrwx 1 root root 38 Oct 20 08:12 shop.conf -> /etc/nginx/sites-available/shop.conf If you delete the target of a symlink, the link keeps pointing at nothing and is called broken . Hard links cannot cross filesystems, and deleting one name does not remove the data"},"linux/users-groups-permissions":{"html":"\u003cp>A developer uploads a logo through your app. The file is on disk, the app can read it, but the web server returns \u003ccode>403 Forbidden\u003c/code>. Nothing is wrong with the code. The cause is almost always an ownership or permission problem, and you can diagnose it in two commands once you can read the permission bits.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How users, groups and the \u003ccode>root\u003c/code> account relate\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to read \u003ccode>rwx\u003c/code> and numeric modes such as \u003ccode>640\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why a directory needs the execute bit\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to use \u003ccode>sudo\u003c/code> with least privilege\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"users-and-groups\">Users and groups\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every process runs as a user, and every file has an owner user and an owner group. Account data is stored in plain files: \u003ccode>/etc/passwd\u003c/code> (users), \u003ccode>/etc/group\u003c/code> (groups) and \u003ccode>/etc/shadow\u003c/code> (password hashes, readable only by root).\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"id\ngetent passwd www-data\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">id\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">uid=1001(deploy) gid=1001(deploy) groups=1001(deploy),998(docker)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>getent passwd www-data\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">www-data:x:33:33:www-data:/var/www:/usr/sbin/nologin\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>id\u003c/code> shows who you are and which groups you belong to. A shell of \u003ccode>/usr/sbin/nologin\u003c/code> marks a \u003cstrong>service account\u003c/strong>: it can own files and run a daemon, but nobody can log in as it. Use dedicated service accounts for each application so a compromise stays contained.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reading-permission-bits\">Reading permission bits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ls -l /srv/app/uploads\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> -l /srv/app/uploads\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-rw-r--r-- 1 deploy deploy 48213 Oct 24 01:50 logo.png\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The first column is ten characters: one for the type, then three triplets for \u003cstrong>owner\u003c/strong>, \u003cstrong>group\u003c/strong> and \u003cstrong>others\u003c/strong>. Each triplet is read (\u003ccode>r\u003c/code>), write (\u003ccode>w\u003c/code>) and execute (\u003ccode>x\u003c/code>).\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Mode\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Typical use\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>644\u003c/code> (\u003ccode>rw-r--r--\u003c/code>)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Owner reads and writes, everyone else reads\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Config and web content\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>600\u003c/code> (\u003ccode>rw-------\u003c/code>)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Owner only\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>SSH private keys, secrets\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>755\u003c/code> (\u003ccode>rwxr-xr-x\u003c/code>)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Everyone can run or enter, only owner writes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Programs and directories\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>750\u003c/code> (\u003ccode>rwxr-x---\u003c/code>)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Owner and group only\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Shared app directories\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Numbers add up per triplet: read is 4, write is 2, execute is 1. So \u003ccode>7\u003c/code> is \u003ccode>rwx\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>6\u003c/code> is \u003ccode>rw-\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>5\u003c/code> is \u003ccode>r-x\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>4\u003c/code> is \u003ccode>r--\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"chmod 640 app.env              # owner rw, group r, others none\nchmod u+x deploy.sh            # add execute for the owner\nchown deploy:deploy app.env    # change owner and group\nchmod -R g+rX /srv/app/static  # capital X adds execute only on directories\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">chmod\u003c/span> 640 app.env              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># owner rw, group r, others none\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">chmod\u003c/span> u+x deploy.sh            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># add execute for the owner\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">chown\u003c/span> deploy:deploy app.env    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># change owner and group\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">chmod\u003c/span> -R g+rX /srv/app/static  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># capital X adds execute only on directories\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-note\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Note\u003c/div>\u003cp>On a \u003cstrong>directory\u003c/strong>, \u003ccode>r\u003c/code> lets you list names, \u003ccode>w\u003c/code> lets you create or delete entries, and \u003ccode>x\u003c/code> lets you enter it and reach things inside. Without \u003ccode>x\u003c/code> on every directory in a path, files below it are unreachable even if the file itself is world-readable.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003ch2 id=\"least-privilege-with-sudo\">Least privilege with sudo\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Do not work as root. Log in as yourself and elevate one command at a time.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo systemctl restart shop\nsudo -l\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> systemctl restart shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">User deploy may run the following commands on app01:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    (root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl restart shop\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>sudo -l\u003c/code> lists what you may run. Granting a specific command, as above, is safer than a blanket \u003ccode>ALL\u003c/code>. Edit rules with \u003ccode>visudo\u003c/code>, which checks syntax before saving so you cannot lock yourself out with a typo.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-uploads-return-403-from-nginx\">Incident: uploads return 403 from nginx\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>nginx runs as \u003ccode>www-data\u003c/code> and serves files from \u003ccode>/srv/app/uploads\u003c/code>. New uploads, written by the app as user \u003ccode>deploy\u003c/code>, return 403 although the files exist and look readable.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>namei -l\u003c/code> prints the permissions of every directory on the way to the file, which is the fastest way to find the broken link in the chain.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"namei -l /srv/app/uploads/logo.png\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>namei -l /srv/app/uploads/logo.png\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">f: /srv/app/uploads/logo.png\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">dr-xr-xr-x root   root   /\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">drwxr-xr-x root   root   srv\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">drwx------ deploy deploy app\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">drwxr-xr-x deploy deploy uploads\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-rw-r--r-- deploy deploy logo.png\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The directory \u003ccode>app\u003c/code> is \u003ccode>drwx------\u003c/code>: only \u003ccode>deploy\u003c/code> can enter it. \u003ccode>www-data\u003c/code> is &quot;others&quot; and has no \u003ccode>x\u003c/code>, so it never reaches \u003ccode>uploads\u003c/code>. The file&#39;s own permissions are fine. Grant traverse access on that one directory, then test as the web user:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo chmod o+x /srv/app\nsudo -u www-data cat /srv/app/uploads/logo.png &gt; /dev/null &amp;&amp; echo readable\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">chmod\u003c/span> o+x /srv/app\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> -u www-data \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> /srv/app/uploads/logo.png &gt; /dev/null &amp;&amp; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> readable\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">readable\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>o+x\u003c/code> lets others enter \u003ccode>app\u003c/code> but not list its contents, which keeps the exposure small. Testing as the affected user proves the fix instead of assuming it.\u003c/p>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-warning\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Warning\u003c/div>\u003cp>Do not &quot;fix&quot; permission errors with \u003ccode>chmod -R 777\u003c/code>. It gives every user on the machine write access, hides the real cause and is a common source of security findings.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003ch2 id=\"predict-the-permission\">Predict the permission\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A file has mode 640, owner alice, group dev. User bob is in group dev. Can bob read it, and can bob write it?\u003c/p>\u003cpre class=\"predict-code\" tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>ls -l report.txt\n-rw-r----- 1 alice dev 120 Oct 1 09:00 report.txt\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Bob can read it but cannot write it.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The group digit is 4, which is read only. Bob matches the group class, so he gets read. Others get nothing. Write would need 660.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A directory has mode 750. A user who is not the owner and not in the group runs ls on it. What happens?\u003c/p>\u003cpre class=\"predict-code\" tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>drwxr-x--- 2 alice dev 4096 Oct 1 09:00 private\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Permission denied.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The other class has 0, so no read and no execute. The user cannot list it or enter it.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"proc&gt;dir;proc&gt;cls;dir&gt;bits;cls&gt;bits;bits&gt;res\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Linux decides if you may open a file\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"proc\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Process\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dir\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Directory path\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cls\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Owner, group or other\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"bits\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Permission bits\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"res\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Allow or deny\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Process to Directory path; 2, Process to Owner, group or other; 3, Directory path to Permission bits; 4, Owner, group or other to Permission bits; 5, Permission bits to Allow or deny.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"proc\">\u003cdt>Process \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A running program carries a user id and group ids.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If it runs as the wrong user, every check below uses the wrong identity.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dir\">\u003cdt>Directory path \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Each directory on the path needs execute (x) for the caller.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A missing x on one parent directory blocks access even if the file itself is open.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cls\">\u003cdt>Owner, group or other \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The kernel picks one class: owner first, then group, then other. Only that class is used.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Being the owner with fewer rights than the group still gives the owner the smaller set.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"bits\">\u003cdt>Permission bits \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The r, w and x bits of the chosen class are compared with what is asked.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong bits give Permission denied.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"res\">\u003cdt>Allow or deny \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The call succeeds or returns an error. ACLs and SELinux can add more checks.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A deny from SELinux looks like a permission error but chmod does not fix it.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Forgetting that every parent directory needs the execute bit\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running applications as root because it &quot;just works&quot;\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving SSH private keys at \u003ccode>644\u003c/code>; \u003ccode>ssh\u003c/code> refuses keys that other users can read\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using \u003ccode>777\u003c/code> instead of finding which user or group needs access\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"users-and-groups","text":"Users and groups"},{"id":"reading-permission-bits","text":"Reading permission bits"},{"id":"least-privilege-with-sudo","text":"Least privilege with sudo"},{"id":"incident-uploads-return-403-from-nginx","text":"Incident: uploads return 403 from nginx"},{"id":"predict-the-permission","text":"Predict the permission"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Linux man-pages (man7.org)","u":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/index.html"},{"t":"Red Hat Enterprise Linux documentation","u":"https://docs.redhat.com/"},{"t":"Ubuntu Server documentation","u":"https://ubuntu.com/server/docs"}],"text":"A developer uploads a logo through your app. The file is on disk, the app can read it, but the web server returns 403 Forbidden . Nothing is wrong with the code. The cause is almost always an ownership or permission problem, and you can diagnose it in two commands once you can read the permission bits. What you will learn How users, groups and the root account relate How to read rwx and numeric modes such as 640 Why a directory needs the execute bit How to use sudo with least privilege Users and groups Every process runs as a user, and every file has an owner user and an owner group. Account data is stored in plain files: /etc/passwd (users), /etc/group (groups) and /etc/shadow (password hashes, readable only by root). $ id uid=1001(deploy) gid=1001(deploy) groups=1001(deploy),998(docker) $ getent passwd www-data www-data:x:33:33:www-data:/var/www:/usr/sbin/nologin id shows who you are and which groups you belong to. A shell of /usr/sbin/nologin marks a service account : it can own files and run a daemon, but nobody can log in as it. Use dedicated service accounts for each application so a compromise stays contained. Reading permission bits $ ls -l /srv/app/uploads -rw-r--r-- 1 deploy deploy 48213 Oct 24 01:50 logo.png The first column is ten characters: one for the type, then three triplets for owner , group and others . Each triplet is read ( r ), write ( w ) and execute ( x ). Mode Meaning Typical use 644 ( rw-r--r-- ) Owner reads and writes, everyone else reads Config and web content 600 ( rw------- ) Owner only SSH private keys, secrets 755 ( rwxr-xr-x ) Everyone can run or enter, only owner writes Programs and directories 750 ( rwxr-x--- ) Owner and group only Shared app directories Numbers add up per triplet: read is 4, write is 2, execute is 1. So 7 is rwx , 6 is rw- , 5 is r-x , and 4 is r-- . chmod 640 app.env # owner rw, group r, others none chmod u+x deploy.sh # add execute for the owner chown deploy:deploy app.env # change owner and group chmod -R g+rX /srv/app/static # capital X adds execute only on directories Note On a directory , r lets you list names, w lets you create or delete entries, and x lets you enter it and reach things inside. Without x on every directory in a path, files below it are unreachable even if the file itself is world-readable. Least privilege with sudo Do not work as root. Log in as yourself and elevate one command at a time. $ sudo systemctl restart shop $ sudo -l User deploy may run the following commands on app01: (root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl restart shop sudo -l lists what you may run. Granting a specific command, as above, is safer than a blanket ALL . Edit rules with visudo , which checks syntax before saving so you cannot lock yourself out with a typo. Incident: uploads return 403 from nginx Incident nginx runs as www-data and serves files from /srv/app/uploads . New uploads, written by the app as user deploy , return 403 although the files exist and look readable. namei -l prints the permissions of every directory on the way to the file, which is the fastest way to find the broken link in the chain. $ namei -l /srv/app/uploads/logo.png f: /srv/app/uploads/logo.png dr-xr-xr-x root root / drwxr-xr-x root root srv drwx------ deploy deploy app drwxr-xr-x deploy deploy uploads -rw-r--r-- deploy deploy logo.png The directory app is drwx------ : only deploy can enter it. www-data is \"others\" and has no x , so it never reaches uploads . The file&#39;s own permissions are fine. Grant traverse access on that one directory, then test as the web user: $ sudo chmod o+x /srv/app $ sudo -u www-data cat /srv/app/uploads/logo.png > /dev/null && echo readable readable o+x lets others enter app but not list its contents, which keeps the exposure small. Testing as the affected user proves the fix instead of assuming it. Warning Do not \"fix\" permission errors with chmod -R 777 . It gives every user on the machine write access, hides the real cause and is a common source of security findings. Pr"},"linux/processes-and-services":{"html":"\u003cp>After a deploy, the new service will not stay up. \u003ccode>systemctl status\u003c/code> says &quot;activating (auto-restart)&quot; over and over. You cannot fix a crash loop by restarting it harder. You need to read why it exits. This lesson covers the process basics and the systemd workflow you will use daily.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How to list and inspect processes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What signals are and why \u003ccode>kill -9\u003c/code> is a last resort\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How a systemd unit file describes a service\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to read a failing service&#39;s logs and exit codes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"processes-in-practice\">Processes in practice\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A process is a running program with a process ID (PID), an owner and a parent. The kernel starts one process, PID 1, and every other process descends from it. On most modern distributions PID 1 is \u003cstrong>systemd\u003c/strong>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ps -eo pid,ppid,user,%cpu,%mem,etime,cmd --sort=-%mem | head -4\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ps -eo pid,ppid,user,%cpu,%mem,etime,cmd --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span>=-%mem | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    PID    PPID USER     %CPU %MEM     ELAPSED CMD\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">   2211     1 deploy    3.1 18.4  3-02:11:09 /opt/shop/current/bin/server --port 8080\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    845     1 postgres  0.4  6.2 12-01:02:44 /usr/lib/postgresql/16/bin/postgres -D /var/lib/postgresql/16/main\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">   1032     1 root      0.0  0.9 12-01:03:10 /usr/sbin/nginx -g daemon on; master_process on;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This is sample output. \u003ccode>ps -eo\u003c/code> lets you choose columns, and \u003ccode>--sort=-%mem\u003c/code> puts the biggest memory users first. \u003ccode>pgrep -a shop\u003c/code> finds processes by name, and \u003ccode>top\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>htop\u003c/code> shows a live view.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"signals\">Signals\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Signals are messages sent to a process. The ones you will use:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Signal\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Number\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>SIGTERM\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>15\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Ask the process to shut down cleanly (the default for \u003ccode>kill\u003c/code>)\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>SIGINT\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>2\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Same as pressing Ctrl+C\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>SIGHUP\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>1\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Many daemons reload configuration on this\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>SIGKILL\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>9\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Force-stop immediately; the process cannot catch it or clean up\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kill 2211          # SIGTERM: lets the app finish requests and close files\nkill -HUP 1032     # nginx reloads its configuration\nkill -9 2211       # last resort: no cleanup, can leave locks and half-written data\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">kill\u003c/span> 2211          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># SIGTERM: lets the app finish requests and close files\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">kill\u003c/span> -HUP 1032     \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># nginx reloads its configuration\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">kill\u003c/span> -9 2211       \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># last resort: no cleanup, can leave locks and half-written data\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Try \u003ccode>SIGTERM\u003c/code> first and wait. Reach for \u003ccode>SIGKILL\u003c/code> only when a process ignores \u003ccode>SIGTERM\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"services-with-systemd\">Services with systemd\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>unit file\u003c/strong> tells systemd how to run a service. Custom units go in \u003ccode>/etc/systemd/system/\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ini\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"[Unit]\nDescription=Shop API\nAfter=network-online.target\nWants=network-online.target\n\n[Service]\nUser=deploy\nWorkingDirectory=/opt/shop/current\nExecStart=/opt/shop/current/bin/server --port 8080\nRestart=on-failure\nRestartSec=5\n\n[Install]\nWantedBy=multi-user.target\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">[Unit]\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Description\u003c/span>=Shop API\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">After\u003c/span>=network-\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">on\u003c/span>line.target\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Wants\u003c/span>=network-\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">on\u003c/span>line.target\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">[Service]\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">User\u003c/span>=deploy\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">WorkingDirectory\u003c/span>=/opt/shop/current\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ExecStart\u003c/span>=/opt/shop/current/bin/server --port \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8080\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Restart\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">on\u003c/span>-failure\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">RestartSec\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">[Install]\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">WantedBy\u003c/span>=multi-user.target\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>ExecStart\u003c/code> must be an absolute path. \u003ccode>Restart=on-failure\u003c/code> restarts after a crash but not after a normal stop. \u003ccode>WantedBy\u003c/code> decides when the service starts at boot.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo systemctl daemon-reload      # required after editing a unit file\nsudo systemctl enable --now shop  # start now and at every boot\nsudo systemctl status shop        # state, recent log lines, exit code\nsudo systemctl restart shop\njournalctl -u shop -f             # follow the service's logs\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> systemctl daemon-reload      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># required after editing a unit file\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> systemctl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">enable\u003c/span> --now shop  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># start now and at every boot\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> systemctl status shop        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># state, recent log lines, exit code\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> systemctl restart shop\njournalctl -u shop -f             \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># follow the service&#x27;s logs\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-tip\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Tip\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>systemctl status\u003c/code> shows the last few log lines, but \u003ccode>journalctl -u shop -n 50 --no-pager\u003c/code> shows more context. Combine \u003ccode>-p err\u003c/code> to show only errors.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-service-restarts-every-five-seconds\">Incident: the service restarts every five seconds\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After a release, \u003ccode>shop.service\u003c/code> never becomes active. Users get connection refused.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"systemctl status shop\njournalctl -u shop -n 3 --no-pager\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>systemctl status shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">● shop.service - Shop API\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">     Loaded: loaded (/etc/systemd/system/shop.service; enabled)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">     Active: activating (auto-restart) (Result: exit-code) since Sat 2026-10-24 02:31:18 UTC; 3s ago\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    Process: 4410 ExecStart=/opt/shop/current/bin/server --port 8080 (code=exited, status=203/EXEC)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>journalctl -u shop -n 3 --no-pager\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Oct 24 02:31:18 app01 systemd[1]: shop.service: Failed at step EXEC spawning /opt/shop/current/bin/server: No such file or directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Oct 24 02:31:18 app01 systemd[1]: shop.service: Main process exited, code=exited, status=203/EXEC\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Oct 24 02:31:18 app01 systemd[1]: shop.service: Failed with result 'exit-code'.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Exit status \u003ccode>203/EXEC\u003c/code> means systemd could not even start the binary. The message names the path, so check it:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ls -l /opt/shop/current/bin/\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> -l /opt/shop/current/bin/\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">total 4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-rwxr-xr-x 1 deploy deploy 1873 Oct 24 02:29 start.sh\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The new release ships \u003ccode>start.sh\u003c/code>, but the unit still points at \u003ccode>bin/server\u003c/code>. Update \u003ccode>ExecStart\u003c/code>, run \u003ccode>daemon-reload\u003c/code>, restart, and confirm:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo systemctl daemon-reload &amp;&amp; sudo systemctl restart shop\nsystemctl is-active shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> systemctl daemon-reload &amp;&amp; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> systemctl restart shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>systemctl is-active shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">active\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The pattern is general: read the \u003cstrong>exit code\u003c/strong> and the \u003cstrong>first error line\u003c/strong>, not the restart loop. Common codes are \u003ccode>203/EXEC\u003c/code> (cannot execute the binary), \u003ccode>200/CHDIR\u003c/code> (bad \u003ccode>WorkingDirectory\u003c/code>), and \u003ccode>217/USER\u003c/code> (the \u003ccode>User=\u003c/code> does not exist).\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"predict-the-signal\">Predict the signal\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You run kill 4321 on a process that has no special signal handler. Which signal is sent, and what usually happens?\u003c/p>\u003cpre class=\"predict-code\" tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>kill 4321\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> SIGTERM (15) is sent, and the process exits after it cleans up, if it handles the signal.\u003c/p>\u003cp>kill without a number sends SIGTERM, a polite request. SIGKILL (9) is the forced version and gives the program no chance to clean up.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A systemd unit has Restart=on-failure. The service exits with code 0 after finishing its work. Does systemd restart it?\u003c/p>\u003cpre class=\"predict-code\" tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>[Service]\nExecStart=/usr/local/bin/job\nRestart=on-failure\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> No. A clean exit with code 0 is not a failure.\u003c/p>\u003cp>on-failure restarts for non-zero exit codes, signals and timeouts. Use Restart=always if it must run again after any exit.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Editing a unit file and forgetting \u003ccode>systemctl daemon-reload\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using relative paths in \u003ccode>ExecStart\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using \u003ccode>kill -9\u003c/code> as the first step\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading only \u003ccode>systemctl status\u003c/code> and missing earlier errors in \u003ccode>journalctl\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"processes-in-practice","text":"Processes in practice"},{"id":"signals","text":"Signals"},{"id":"services-with-systemd","text":"Services with systemd"},{"id":"incident-the-service-restarts-every-five-seconds","text":"Incident: the service restarts every five seconds"},{"id":"predict-the-signal","text":"Predict the signal"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"systemd.service manual","u":"https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.service.html"},{"t":"systemd documentation","u":"https://systemd.io/"},{"t":"Linux man-pages (man7.org)","u":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/index.html"}],"text":"After a deploy, the new service will not stay up. systemctl status says \"activating (auto-restart)\" over and over. You cannot fix a crash loop by restarting it harder. You need to read why it exits. This lesson covers the process basics and the systemd workflow you will use daily. What you will learn How to list and inspect processes What signals are and why kill -9 is a last resort How a systemd unit file describes a service How to read a failing service&#39;s logs and exit codes Processes in practice A process is a running program with a process ID (PID), an owner and a parent. The kernel starts one process, PID 1, and every other process descends from it. On most modern distributions PID 1 is systemd . $ ps -eo pid,ppid,user,%cpu,%mem,etime,cmd -- sort =-%mem | head -4 PID PPID USER %CPU %MEM ELAPSED CMD 2211 1 deploy 3.1 18.4 3-02:11:09 /opt/shop/current/bin/server --port 8080 845 1 postgres 0.4 6.2 12-01:02:44 /usr/lib/postgresql/16/bin/postgres -D /var/lib/postgresql/16/main 1032 1 root 0.0 0.9 12-01:03:10 /usr/sbin/nginx -g daemon on; master_process on; This is sample output. ps -eo lets you choose columns, and --sort=-%mem puts the biggest memory users first. pgrep -a shop finds processes by name, and top or htop shows a live view. Signals Signals are messages sent to a process. The ones you will use: Signal Number Meaning SIGTERM 15 Ask the process to shut down cleanly (the default for kill ) SIGINT 2 Same as pressing Ctrl+C SIGHUP 1 Many daemons reload configuration on this SIGKILL 9 Force-stop immediately; the process cannot catch it or clean up kill 2211 # SIGTERM: lets the app finish requests and close files kill -HUP 1032 # nginx reloads its configuration kill -9 2211 # last resort: no cleanup, can leave locks and half-written data Try SIGTERM first and wait. Reach for SIGKILL only when a process ignores SIGTERM . Services with systemd A unit file tells systemd how to run a service. Custom units go in /etc/systemd/system/ . [Unit] Description =Shop API After =network- on line.target Wants =network- on line.target [Service] User =deploy WorkingDirectory =/opt/shop/current ExecStart =/opt/shop/current/bin/server --port 8080 Restart = on -failure RestartSec = 5 [Install] WantedBy =multi-user.target ExecStart must be an absolute path. Restart=on-failure restarts after a crash but not after a normal stop. WantedBy decides when the service starts at boot. sudo systemctl daemon-reload # required after editing a unit file sudo systemctl enable --now shop # start now and at every boot sudo systemctl status shop # state, recent log lines, exit code sudo systemctl restart shop journalctl -u shop -f # follow the service&#x27;s logs Tip systemctl status shows the last few log lines, but journalctl -u shop -n 50 --no-pager shows more context. Combine -p err to show only errors. Incident: the service restarts every five seconds Incident After a release, shop.service never becomes active. Users get connection refused. $ systemctl status shop ● shop.service - Shop API Loaded: loaded (/etc/systemd/system/shop.service; enabled) Active: activating (auto-restart) (Result: exit-code) since Sat 2026-10-24 02:31:18 UTC; 3s ago Process: 4410 ExecStart=/opt/shop/current/bin/server --port 8080 (code=exited, status=203/EXEC) $ journalctl -u shop -n 3 --no-pager Oct 24 02:31:18 app01 systemd[1]: shop.service: Failed at step EXEC spawning /opt/shop/current/bin/server: No such file or directory Oct 24 02:31:18 app01 systemd[1]: shop.service: Main process exited, code=exited, status=203/EXEC Oct 24 02:31:18 app01 systemd[1]: shop.service: Failed with result 'exit-code'. Exit status 203/EXEC means systemd could not even start the binary. The message names the path, so check it: $ ls -l /opt/shop/current/bin/ total 4 -rwxr-xr-x 1 deploy deploy 1873 Oct 24 02:29 start.sh The new release ships start.sh , but the unit still points at bin/server . Update ExecStart , run daemon-reload , restart, and confirm: $ sudo systemctl daemon-reload && sudo syst"},"linux/disks-and-filesystems":{"html":"\u003cp>At 03:40 your database refuses writes with \u003ccode>No space left on device\u003c/code>. You run \u003ccode>df -h\u003c/code> and the root filesystem is 100% full. You add up the large directories and they total less than half the disk. Somebody has deleted a file that is still using space. This lesson gives you the tools to find it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How block devices, partitions, filesystems and mount points relate\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to measure space and inodes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to find the largest directories quickly\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How a deleted-but-open file keeps consuming space\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"from-device-to-directory\">From device to directory\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A disk appears as a \u003cstrong>block device\u003c/strong>, such as \u003ccode>/dev/sda\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>/dev/nvme0n1\u003c/code>. You split it into partitions, put a \u003cstrong>filesystem\u003c/strong> (ext4 or XFS are common) on each, and \u003cstrong>mount\u003c/strong> it at a directory.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"lsblk\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>lsblk\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME   MAJ:MIN RM  SIZE RO TYPE MOUNTPOINTS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">sda      8:0    0   50G  0 disk\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">├─sda1   8:1    0   49G  0 part /\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">└─sda15  8:15   0  1.0G  0 part /boot/efi\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">sdb      8:16   0  200G  0 disk\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">└─sdb1   8:17   0  200G  0 part /var/lib/postgresql\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This is sample output. \u003ccode>lsblk\u003c/code> shows the device tree, and \u003ccode>findmnt\u003c/code> shows what is mounted where. Permanent mounts are listed in \u003ccode>/etc/fstab\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# /etc/fstab: device, mount point, type, options, dump, fsck order\nUUID=3f5a1c0e-9c1e-4a77-8e0a-0c5d7a1b2c11  /var/lib/postgresql  ext4  defaults,noatime  0  2\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># /etc/fstab: device, mount point, type, options, dump, fsck order\u003c/span>\nUUID=3f5a1c0e-9c1e-4a77-8e0a-0c5d7a1b2c11  /var/lib/postgresql  ext4  defaults,noatime  0  2\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-warning\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Warning\u003c/div>\u003cp>A typo in \u003ccode>/etc/fstab\u003c/code> can stop a server from booting. Test new lines with \u003ccode>sudo mount -a\u003c/code> before you reboot, and consider the \u003ccode>nofail\u003c/code> option for non-critical mounts.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003ch2 id=\"measuring-space\">Measuring space\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"df -h /\ndf -i /\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">df\u003c/span> -h /\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Filesystem      Size  Used Avail Use% Mounted on\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">/dev/sda1        49G   49G     0 100% /\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">df\u003c/span> -i /\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Filesystem      Inodes   IUsed   IFree IUse% Mounted on\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">/dev/sda1      3276800  412887 2863913   13% /\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>df -h\u003c/code> reports blocks used. \u003ccode>df -i\u003c/code> reports \u003cstrong>inodes\u003c/strong>, the records that describe each file. A filesystem can run out of inodes, for example from millions of tiny files, while having free blocks. Either one produces \u003ccode>No space left on device\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>To find what is large, limit \u003ccode>du\u003c/code> to one filesystem and sort:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo du -xh --max-depth=1 /var | sort -h | tail -4\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">du\u003c/span> -xh --max-depth=1 /var | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span> -h | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">312M\t/var/cache\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">1.2G\t/var/lib\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">3.4G\t/var/log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">5.1G\t/var\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>-x\u003c/code> stays on one filesystem, and \u003ccode>sort -h\u003c/code> understands sizes like \u003ccode>1.2G\u003c/code>. Drill down level by level with the same command.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-df-says-full-du-disagrees\">Incident: df says full, du disagrees\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>df\u003c/code> reports 49 GB used on \u003ccode>/\u003c/code>, but the largest directories add up to about 12 GB.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Compare the two numbers. \u003ccode>df\u003c/code> asks the filesystem how many blocks are allocated. \u003ccode>du\u003c/code> adds up files it can see. The gap is space held by files that were \u003cstrong>deleted while a process still has them open\u003c/strong>. The name is gone, but the data stays until the last process closes it. A common cause is deleting a log file instead of rotating it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo lsof +L1\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> lsof +L1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">COMMAND  PID   USER FD TYPE DEVICE  SIZE/OFF NLINK   NODE NAME\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">java    1432    app  5w  REG  253,0 37580963840     0 131075 /var/log/app/app.log (deleted)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>+L1\u003c/code> lists open files with fewer than one link, meaning deleted. Here process 1432 still holds a 35 GB log. Two ways to recover:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo systemctl restart app            # preferred: releases the file cleanly\nsudo sh -c ': &gt; /proc/1432/fd/5'      # truncate in place if you cannot restart yet\ndf -h /\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> systemctl restart app            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># preferred: releases the file cleanly\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> sh -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;: &gt; /proc/1432/fd/5&#x27;\u003c/span>      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># truncate in place if you cannot restart yet\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">df\u003c/span> -h /\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Filesystem      Size  Used Avail Use% Mounted on\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">/dev/sda1        49G   13G   34G  28% /\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The fix is half the job. Prevent a repeat by rotating logs with \u003ccode>logrotate\u003c/code> (using \u003ccode>copytruncate\u003c/code> or a reload signal) so nobody has to delete live logs by hand, and alert at 80% disk usage so you act before 100%.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"creating-and-growing-storage\">Creating and growing storage\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>For a new disk, the sequence is: partition, create a filesystem, mount, persist in \u003ccode>fstab\u003c/code>. Always check the device name twice, because formatting the wrong disk destroys data.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo parted /dev/sdc --script mklabel gpt mkpart primary ext4 0% 100%\nsudo mkfs.ext4 /dev/sdc1\nsudo mkdir -p /data &amp;&amp; sudo mount /dev/sdc1 /data\nsudo blkid /dev/sdc1          # copy the UUID into /etc/fstab\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> parted /dev/sdc --script mklabel gpt mkpart primary ext4 0% 100%\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> mkfs.ext4 /dev/sdc1\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">mkdir\u003c/span> -p /data &amp;&amp; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> mount /dev/sdc1 /data\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> blkid /dev/sdc1          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># copy the UUID into /etc/fstab\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Many servers use \u003cstrong>LVM\u003c/strong> so volumes can grow without repartitioning. Growing is a two-step process: extend the logical volume, then grow the filesystem (for ext4, \u003ccode>resize2fs\u003c/code>).\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Looking only at \u003ccode>df -h\u003c/code> and forgetting \u003ccode>df -i\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deleting a live log instead of truncating or rotating it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running \u003ccode>du\u003c/code> on \u003ccode>/\u003c/code> without \u003ccode>-x\u003c/code>, which crosses into other mounts and \u003ccode>/proc\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Editing \u003ccode>/etc/fstab\u003c/code> without testing \u003ccode>mount -a\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"from-device-to-directory","text":"From device to directory"},{"id":"measuring-space","text":"Measuring space"},{"id":"incident-df-says-full-du-disagrees","text":"Incident: df says full, du disagrees"},{"id":"creating-and-growing-storage","text":"Creating and growing storage"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"The Linux kernel documentation","u":"https://docs.kernel.org/"},{"t":"Linux man-pages (man7.org)","u":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/index.html"},{"t":"Red Hat Enterprise Linux documentation","u":"https://docs.redhat.com/"}],"text":"At 03:40 your database refuses writes with No space left on device . You run df -h and the root filesystem is 100% full. You add up the large directories and they total less than half the disk. Somebody has deleted a file that is still using space. This lesson gives you the tools to find it. What you will learn How block devices, partitions, filesystems and mount points relate How to measure space and inodes How to find the largest directories quickly How a deleted-but-open file keeps consuming space From device to directory A disk appears as a block device , such as /dev/sda or /dev/nvme0n1 . You split it into partitions, put a filesystem (ext4 or XFS are common) on each, and mount it at a directory. $ lsblk NAME MAJ:MIN RM SIZE RO TYPE MOUNTPOINTS sda 8:0 0 50G 0 disk ├─sda1 8:1 0 49G 0 part / └─sda15 8:15 0 1.0G 0 part /boot/efi sdb 8:16 0 200G 0 disk └─sdb1 8:17 0 200G 0 part /var/lib/postgresql This is sample output. lsblk shows the device tree, and findmnt shows what is mounted where. Permanent mounts are listed in /etc/fstab . # /etc/fstab: device, mount point, type, options, dump, fsck order UUID=3f5a1c0e-9c1e-4a77-8e0a-0c5d7a1b2c11 /var/lib/postgresql ext4 defaults,noatime 0 2 Warning A typo in /etc/fstab can stop a server from booting. Test new lines with sudo mount -a before you reboot, and consider the nofail option for non-critical mounts. Measuring space $ df -h / Filesystem Size Used Avail Use% Mounted on /dev/sda1 49G 49G 0 100% / $ df -i / Filesystem Inodes IUsed IFree IUse% Mounted on /dev/sda1 3276800 412887 2863913 13% / df -h reports blocks used. df -i reports inodes , the records that describe each file. A filesystem can run out of inodes, for example from millions of tiny files, while having free blocks. Either one produces No space left on device . To find what is large, limit du to one filesystem and sort: $ sudo du -xh --max-depth=1 /var | sort -h | tail -4 312M /var/cache 1.2G /var/lib 3.4G /var/log 5.1G /var -x stays on one filesystem, and sort -h understands sizes like 1.2G . Drill down level by level with the same command. Incident: df says full, du disagrees Incident df reports 49 GB used on / , but the largest directories add up to about 12 GB. Compare the two numbers. df asks the filesystem how many blocks are allocated. du adds up files it can see. The gap is space held by files that were deleted while a process still has them open . The name is gone, but the data stays until the last process closes it. A common cause is deleting a log file instead of rotating it. $ sudo lsof +L1 COMMAND PID USER FD TYPE DEVICE SIZE/OFF NLINK NODE NAME java 1432 app 5w REG 253,0 37580963840 0 131075 /var/log/app/app.log (deleted) +L1 lists open files with fewer than one link, meaning deleted. Here process 1432 still holds a 35 GB log. Two ways to recover: $ sudo systemctl restart app # preferred: releases the file cleanly $ sudo sh -c &#x27;: > /proc/1432/fd/5&#x27; # truncate in place if you cannot restart yet $ df -h / Filesystem Size Used Avail Use% Mounted on /dev/sda1 49G 13G 34G 28% / The fix is half the job. Prevent a repeat by rotating logs with logrotate (using copytruncate or a reload signal) so nobody has to delete live logs by hand, and alert at 80% disk usage so you act before 100%. Creating and growing storage For a new disk, the sequence is: partition, create a filesystem, mount, persist in fstab . Always check the device name twice, because formatting the wrong disk destroys data. sudo parted /dev/sdc --script mklabel gpt mkpart primary ext4 0% 100% sudo mkfs.ext4 /dev/sdc1 sudo mkdir -p /data && sudo mount /dev/sdc1 /data sudo blkid /dev/sdc1 # copy the UUID into /etc/fstab Many servers use LVM so volumes can grow without repartitioning. Growing is a two-step process: extend the logical volume, then grow the filesystem (for ext4, resize2fs ). Common mistakes Looking only at df -h and forgetting df -i Deleting a live log instead of truncating or rotating it Running du on / without -x , which cr"},"linux/lvm-and-raid":{"html":"\u003cp>Partitions are rigid. When /var fills up, a plain partition forces you to back up, repartition and restore. \u003cstrong>LVM\u003c/strong> (Logical Volume Manager) adds a flexible layer between disks and filesystems, and \u003cstrong>software RAID\u003c/strong> protects data against disk failure. Production Linux servers commonly use one or both.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The LVM layers: physical volumes, volume groups and logical volumes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Creating, extending and snapshotting a logical volume\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>RAID levels and when each is appropriate\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Replacing a failed disk in an mdadm array\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-three-lvm-layers\">The three LVM layers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Think of LVM as a pool of storage you carve up. A \u003cstrong>physical volume (PV)\u003c/strong> is a disk or partition handed to LVM. A \u003cstrong>volume group (VG)\u003c/strong> pools one or more PVs together. A \u003cstrong>logical volume (LV)\u003c/strong> is a slice of the pool that you format and mount like a partition, but that can later grow or shrink.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo pvcreate /dev/sdb /dev/sdc\nsudo vgcreate data_vg /dev/sdb /dev/sdc\nsudo lvcreate -n logs_lv -L 50G data_vg\nsudo mkfs.ext4 /dev/data_vg/logs_lv\nsudo mkdir -p /var/applogs\nsudo mount /dev/data_vg/logs_lv /var/applogs\nsudo pvs\nsudo vgs\nsudo lvs\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> pvcreate /dev/sdb /dev/sdc\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> vgcreate data_vg /dev/sdb /dev/sdc\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> lvcreate -n logs_lv -L 50G data_vg\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> mkfs.ext4 /dev/data_vg/logs_lv\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">mkdir\u003c/span> -p /var/applogs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> mount /dev/data_vg/logs_lv /var/applogs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> pvs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> vgs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> lvs\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Add a line to \u003ccode>/etc/fstab\u003c/code> using the device mapper path or the UUID from \u003ccode>blkid\u003c/code> so the volume mounts at boot. Use the \u003ccode>nofail\u003c/code> option for non-critical volumes so a missing disk does not stop the boot.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"growing-a-volume-online\">Growing a volume online\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>This is why LVM exists. If the volume group has free space, extend the logical volume and the filesystem in one command:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo vgs data_vg\nsudo lvextend -r -L +20G /dev/data_vg/logs_lv\ndf -h /var/applogs\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> vgs data_vg\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> lvextend -r -L +20G /dev/data_vg/logs_lv\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">df\u003c/span> -h /var/applogs\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The \u003ccode>-r\u003c/code> flag resizes the filesystem too. If the group is full, add a new disk first with \u003ccode>pvcreate\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>vgextend data_vg /dev/sdd\u003c/code>. Growing is safe on a mounted ext4 or XFS filesystem. \u003cstrong>Shrinking\u003c/strong> is riskier: XFS cannot shrink at all, and ext4 must be unmounted and checked first.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"snapshots\">Snapshots\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An LVM snapshot freezes a point-in-time view of a volume using copy-on-write, handy before a risky upgrade or for a consistent backup.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo lvcreate -s -n logs_snap -L 5G /dev/data_vg/logs_lv\nsudo mount -o ro /dev/data_vg/logs_snap /mnt/snap\nsudo umount /mnt/snap &amp;&amp; sudo lvremove -y /dev/data_vg/logs_snap\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> lvcreate -s -n logs_snap -L 5G /dev/data_vg/logs_lv\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> mount -o ro /dev/data_vg/logs_snap /mnt/snap\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> umount /mnt/snap &amp;&amp; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> lvremove -y /dev/data_vg/logs_snap\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Snapshots are not backups. They live on the same disks, and a snapshot that fills its allocated space becomes invalid. Remove them when you are done.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"raid-in-one-table\">RAID in one table\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Level\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Disks\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Survives\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Notes\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>RAID 0\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>2+\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Nothing\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Speed only, any disk failure loses everything\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>RAID 1\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>2\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>1 disk\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Mirror, simple and safe\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>RAID 5\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>3+\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>1 disk\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Capacity of n-1, slow rebuilds on big disks\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>RAID 6\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>4+\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>2 disks\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Safer for large disks\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>RAID 10\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>4+\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>1 per mirror\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Fast and resilient, half the capacity\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>RAID is about \u003cstrong>availability\u003c/strong>, not backup. It does not protect against deletion, corruption or ransomware.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo mdadm --create /dev/md0 --level=1 --raid-devices=2 /dev/sdb /dev/sdc\ncat /proc/mdstat\nsudo mdadm --detail /dev/md0\nsudo mdadm --detail --scan | sudo tee -a /etc/mdadm/mdadm.conf\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> mdadm --create /dev/md0 --level=1 --raid-devices=2 /dev/sdb /dev/sdc\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> /proc/mdstat\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> mdadm --detail /dev/md0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> mdadm --detail --scan | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tee\u003c/span> -a /etc/mdadm/mdadm.conf\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"replacing-a-failed-disk\">Replacing a failed disk\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo mdadm --manage /dev/md0 --fail /dev/sdc\nsudo mdadm --manage /dev/md0 --remove /dev/sdc\nsudo mdadm --manage /dev/md0 --add /dev/sdd\nwatch cat /proc/mdstat\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> mdadm --manage /dev/md0 --fail /dev/sdc\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> mdadm --manage /dev/md0 --remove /dev/sdc\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> mdadm --manage /dev/md0 --add /dev/sdd\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>watch \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> /proc/mdstat\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The array resynchronises in the background. Until it finishes you have no redundancy, so watch the progress and avoid heavy jobs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-var-is-full-and-the-disk-is-not\">Incident: /var is full and the disk is not\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>The database host stops writing because /var is 100% full. The server has a free 200 GB disk, but /var is a plain partition on a different disk, and the team plans a maintenance window to repartition.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>If /var had been an LVM volume, the fix would take a minute. The team can still reduce risk: stop the writer, move the data directory to a new LVM volume built on the free disk, and mount it in place. Going forward, put growth-prone paths such as /var, /var/lib/docker and /data on logical volumes and leave 10 to 20 percent of each volume group unallocated for emergencies.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo vgs\nsudo lvextend -r -L +50G /dev/data_vg/db_lv\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> vgs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  VG      #PV #LV #SN Attr   VSize   VFree\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  data_vg   2   2   0 wz--n- 400.00g 120.00g\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> lvextend -r -L +50G /dev/data_vg/db_lv\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating RAID as a backup\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using up all free extents so you cannot extend or snapshot later\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting to update fstab or mdadm.conf, so volumes vanish after a reboot\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Letting a snapshot fill up and become invalid\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Trying to shrink XFS\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-three-lvm-layers","text":"The three LVM layers"},{"id":"growing-a-volume-online","text":"Growing a volume online"},{"id":"snapshots","text":"Snapshots"},{"id":"raid-in-one-table","text":"RAID in one table"},{"id":"replacing-a-failed-disk","text":"Replacing a failed disk"},{"id":"incident-var-is-full-and-the-disk-is-not","text":"Incident: /var is full and the disk is not"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"LVM2 project","u":"https://sourceware.org/lvm2/"},{"t":"Linux kernel: md (software RAID) admin guide","u":"https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/md.html"},{"t":"Red Hat Enterprise Linux documentation","u":"https://docs.redhat.com/"}],"text":"Partitions are rigid. When /var fills up, a plain partition forces you to back up, repartition and restore. LVM (Logical Volume Manager) adds a flexible layer between disks and filesystems, and software RAID protects data against disk failure. Production Linux servers commonly use one or both. What you will learn The LVM layers: physical volumes, volume groups and logical volumes Creating, extending and snapshotting a logical volume RAID levels and when each is appropriate Replacing a failed disk in an mdadm array The three LVM layers Think of LVM as a pool of storage you carve up. A physical volume (PV) is a disk or partition handed to LVM. A volume group (VG) pools one or more PVs together. A logical volume (LV) is a slice of the pool that you format and mount like a partition, but that can later grow or shrink. $ sudo pvcreate /dev/sdb /dev/sdc $ sudo vgcreate data_vg /dev/sdb /dev/sdc $ sudo lvcreate -n logs_lv -L 50G data_vg $ sudo mkfs.ext4 /dev/data_vg/logs_lv $ sudo mkdir -p /var/applogs $ sudo mount /dev/data_vg/logs_lv /var/applogs $ sudo pvs $ sudo vgs $ sudo lvs Add a line to /etc/fstab using the device mapper path or the UUID from blkid so the volume mounts at boot. Use the nofail option for non-critical volumes so a missing disk does not stop the boot. Growing a volume online This is why LVM exists. If the volume group has free space, extend the logical volume and the filesystem in one command: $ sudo vgs data_vg $ sudo lvextend -r -L +20G /dev/data_vg/logs_lv $ df -h /var/applogs The -r flag resizes the filesystem too. If the group is full, add a new disk first with pvcreate and vgextend data_vg /dev/sdd . Growing is safe on a mounted ext4 or XFS filesystem. Shrinking is riskier: XFS cannot shrink at all, and ext4 must be unmounted and checked first. Snapshots An LVM snapshot freezes a point-in-time view of a volume using copy-on-write, handy before a risky upgrade or for a consistent backup. $ sudo lvcreate -s -n logs_snap -L 5G /dev/data_vg/logs_lv $ sudo mount -o ro /dev/data_vg/logs_snap /mnt/snap $ sudo umount /mnt/snap && sudo lvremove -y /dev/data_vg/logs_snap Snapshots are not backups. They live on the same disks, and a snapshot that fills its allocated space becomes invalid. Remove them when you are done. RAID in one table Level Disks Survives Notes RAID 0 2+ Nothing Speed only, any disk failure loses everything RAID 1 2 1 disk Mirror, simple and safe RAID 5 3+ 1 disk Capacity of n-1, slow rebuilds on big disks RAID 6 4+ 2 disks Safer for large disks RAID 10 4+ 1 per mirror Fast and resilient, half the capacity RAID is about availability , not backup. It does not protect against deletion, corruption or ransomware. $ sudo mdadm --create /dev/md0 --level=1 --raid-devices=2 /dev/sdb /dev/sdc $ cat /proc/mdstat $ sudo mdadm --detail /dev/md0 $ sudo mdadm --detail --scan | sudo tee -a /etc/mdadm/mdadm.conf Replacing a failed disk $ sudo mdadm --manage /dev/md0 --fail /dev/sdc $ sudo mdadm --manage /dev/md0 --remove /dev/sdc $ sudo mdadm --manage /dev/md0 --add /dev/sdd $ watch cat /proc/mdstat The array resynchronises in the background. Until it finishes you have no redundancy, so watch the progress and avoid heavy jobs. Incident: /var is full and the disk is not Incident The database host stops writing because /var is 100% full. The server has a free 200 GB disk, but /var is a plain partition on a different disk, and the team plans a maintenance window to repartition. If /var had been an LVM volume, the fix would take a minute. The team can still reduce risk: stop the writer, move the data directory to a new LVM volume built on the free disk, and mount it in place. Going forward, put growth-prone paths such as /var, /var/lib/docker and /data on logical volumes and leave 10 to 20 percent of each volume group unallocated for emergencies. $ sudo vgs VG #PV #LV #SN Attr VSize VFree data_vg 2 2 0 wz--n- 400.00g 120.00g $ sudo lvextend -r -L +50G /dev/data_vg/db_lv Common mistakes Treating RAID as a backup Using "},"linux/package-management":{"html":"\u003cp>Every package on a server is code that runs with privilege. Package managers install software, resolve dependencies, verify signatures and make upgrades repeatable. Knowing them well is the difference between controlled patching and hoping nothing breaks.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The apt and dnf workflows for install, search, update and remove\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How repositories, GPG keys and signatures protect you\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Holding a package at a version\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Patching safely and checking what changed\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"two-families\">Two families\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Debian and Ubuntu use \u003cstrong>apt\u003c/strong> with \u003ccode>.deb\u003c/code> packages. RHEL, Rocky, Alma, Fedora and Amazon Linux use \u003cstrong>dnf\u003c/strong> (older systems use \u003ccode>yum\u003c/code>) with \u003ccode>.rpm\u003c/code> packages. The concepts are the same.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo apt update\napt search nginx | head -5\napt show nginx | head -12\nsudo apt install -y nginx\ndpkg -L nginx | head\ndpkg -S /usr/sbin/nginx\nsudo apt remove nginx\nsudo apt purge nginx\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> apt update\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>apt search nginx | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -5\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>apt show nginx | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -12\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> apt install -y nginx\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>dpkg -L nginx | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>dpkg -S /usr/sbin/nginx\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> apt remove nginx\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> apt purge nginx\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>apt update\u003c/code> refreshes the package index, it does not upgrade anything. \u003ccode>remove\u003c/code> keeps configuration files, \u003ccode>purge\u003c/code> deletes them too. \u003ccode>dpkg -S\u003c/code> tells you which package owns a file.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo dnf makecache\ndnf search nginx\ndnf info nginx\nsudo dnf install -y nginx\nrpm -ql nginx | head\nrpm -qf /usr/sbin/nginx\ndnf history\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> dnf makecache\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>dnf search nginx\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>dnf info nginx\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> dnf install -y nginx\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>rpm -ql nginx | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>rpm -qf /usr/sbin/nginx\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>dnf \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">history\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"repositories-and-trust\">Repositories and trust\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A repository is a server of packages plus an index. Your system trusts a repository only if you installed its \u003cstrong>signing key\u003c/strong>, and every package or index is verified against that key. Never disable signature checks to &quot;make an install work&quot;.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ls /etc/apt/sources.list.d/\ncat /etc/yum.repos.d/*.repo | head -15\nsudo apt-key list 2&gt;/dev/null | head -3\napt-cache policy nginx\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> /etc/apt/sources.list.d/\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> /etc/yum.repos.d/*.repo | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -15\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> apt-key list 2&gt;/dev/null | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>apt-cache policy nginx\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Add third-party repositories deliberately: download the key over HTTPS, store it in \u003ccode>/etc/apt/keyrings/\u003c/code>, and reference it with \u003ccode>signed-by=\u003c/code> in the source entry. Every extra repository widens who can put code on your servers, so keep the list short and review it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pinning-and-holding\">Pinning and holding\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Production rarely wants the newest version of everything. Hold a package so routine upgrades skip it, or install an exact version.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apt-cache madison nginx | head -3\nsudo apt install -y nginx=1.24.0-2ubuntu7\nsudo apt-mark hold nginx\napt-mark showhold\nsudo dnf install -y nginx-1.24.0\nsudo dnf versionlock add nginx\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>apt-cache madison nginx | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> apt install -y nginx=1.24.0-2ubuntu7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> apt-mark hold nginx\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>apt-mark showhold\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> dnf install -y nginx-1.24.0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> dnf versionlock add nginx\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A hold must be temporary and tracked. A package held for two years is a security risk with a ticket nobody reads.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"patching-safely\">Patching safely\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apt list --upgradable\nsudo apt upgrade -y\nsudo apt autoremove -y\nls /var/run/reboot-required\ndnf check-update\nsudo dnf upgrade --security -y\nsudo needs-restarting -r\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>apt list --upgradable\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> apt upgrade -y\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> apt autoremove -y\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> /var/run/reboot-required\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>dnf check-update\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> dnf upgrade --security -y\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> needs-restarting -r\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A reasonable routine is: patch a staging host, run smoke tests, roll through production in small batches behind a load balancer, and reboot where the kernel or libc changed. Enable \u003cstrong>unattended security updates\u003c/strong> for low-risk hosts, with alerts when they fail.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-held-package-and-the-cve\">Incident: the held package and the CVE\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A security scanner flags an OpenSSL vulnerability on forty servers. Patch automation reports success, but the scanner still fails them. Investigation shows \u003ccode>apt-mark hold openssl\u003c/code> was set a year ago during a compatibility problem and never removed.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Find holds across the fleet and decide on each one:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apt-mark showhold\nsudo apt-mark unhold openssl\nsudo apt install --only-upgrade openssl\nopenssl version\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>apt-mark showhold\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">openssl\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> apt-mark unhold openssl\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> apt install --only-upgrade openssl\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>openssl version\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Then fix the real issue, which was an application depending on an old library, and add a quarterly review that lists every hold with an owner and a reason.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Running \u003ccode>apt upgrade\u003c/code> on production without testing first\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Disabling GPG checks or using \u003ccode>--allow-unauthenticated\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Adding random repositories copied from a blog post\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving holds in place forever\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting to restart services or reboot after library and kernel updates\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"two-families","text":"Two families"},{"id":"repositories-and-trust","text":"Repositories and trust"},{"id":"pinning-and-holding","text":"Pinning and holding"},{"id":"patching-safely","text":"Patching safely"},{"id":"incident-the-held-package-and-the-cve","text":"Incident: the held package and the CVE"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Debian Reference Manual","u":"https://www.debian.org/doc/manuals/debian-reference/"},{"t":"DNF command reference","u":"https://dnf.readthedocs.io/en/latest/command_ref.html"},{"t":"Ubuntu Server documentation","u":"https://ubuntu.com/server/docs"}],"text":"Every package on a server is code that runs with privilege. Package managers install software, resolve dependencies, verify signatures and make upgrades repeatable. Knowing them well is the difference between controlled patching and hoping nothing breaks. What you will learn The apt and dnf workflows for install, search, update and remove How repositories, GPG keys and signatures protect you Holding a package at a version Patching safely and checking what changed Two families Debian and Ubuntu use apt with .deb packages. RHEL, Rocky, Alma, Fedora and Amazon Linux use dnf (older systems use yum ) with .rpm packages. The concepts are the same. $ sudo apt update $ apt search nginx | head -5 $ apt show nginx | head -12 $ sudo apt install -y nginx $ dpkg -L nginx | head $ dpkg -S /usr/sbin/nginx $ sudo apt remove nginx $ sudo apt purge nginx apt update refreshes the package index, it does not upgrade anything. remove keeps configuration files, purge deletes them too. dpkg -S tells you which package owns a file. $ sudo dnf makecache $ dnf search nginx $ dnf info nginx $ sudo dnf install -y nginx $ rpm -ql nginx | head $ rpm -qf /usr/sbin/nginx $ dnf history Repositories and trust A repository is a server of packages plus an index. Your system trusts a repository only if you installed its signing key , and every package or index is verified against that key. Never disable signature checks to \"make an install work\". $ ls /etc/apt/sources.list.d/ $ cat /etc/yum.repos.d/*.repo | head -15 $ sudo apt-key list 2>/dev/null | head -3 $ apt-cache policy nginx Add third-party repositories deliberately: download the key over HTTPS, store it in /etc/apt/keyrings/ , and reference it with signed-by= in the source entry. Every extra repository widens who can put code on your servers, so keep the list short and review it. Pinning and holding Production rarely wants the newest version of everything. Hold a package so routine upgrades skip it, or install an exact version. $ apt-cache madison nginx | head -3 $ sudo apt install -y nginx=1.24.0-2ubuntu7 $ sudo apt-mark hold nginx $ apt-mark showhold $ sudo dnf install -y nginx-1.24.0 $ sudo dnf versionlock add nginx A hold must be temporary and tracked. A package held for two years is a security risk with a ticket nobody reads. Patching safely $ apt list --upgradable $ sudo apt upgrade -y $ sudo apt autoremove -y $ ls /var/run/reboot-required $ dnf check-update $ sudo dnf upgrade --security -y $ sudo needs-restarting -r A reasonable routine is: patch a staging host, run smoke tests, roll through production in small batches behind a load balancer, and reboot where the kernel or libc changed. Enable unattended security updates for low-risk hosts, with alerts when they fail. Incident: the held package and the CVE Incident A security scanner flags an OpenSSL vulnerability on forty servers. Patch automation reports success, but the scanner still fails them. Investigation shows apt-mark hold openssl was set a year ago during a compatibility problem and never removed. Find holds across the fleet and decide on each one: $ apt-mark showhold openssl $ sudo apt-mark unhold openssl $ sudo apt install --only-upgrade openssl $ openssl version Then fix the real issue, which was an application depending on an old library, and add a quarterly review that lists every hold with an owner and a reason. Common mistakes Running apt upgrade on production without testing first Disabling GPG checks or using --allow-unauthenticated Adding random repositories copied from a blog post Leaving holds in place forever Forgetting to restart services or reboot after library and kernel updates"},"linux/ssh-and-remote-access":{"html":"\u003cp>SSH is how you reach nearly every Linux server. It encrypts the session, authenticates with keys and can carry file transfers and network tunnels. A weak SSH setup is one of the most common ways servers get compromised.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Generating and using key pairs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writing \u003ccode>~/.ssh/config\u003c/code> so connections are short and consistent\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Hardening sshd and using bastion (jump) hosts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Port forwarding, scp and rsync\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keys-instead-of-passwords\">Keys instead of passwords\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A key pair has a private key that never leaves your machine and a public key placed on servers. Use the modern Ed25519 type and protect the private key with a passphrase.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C &quot;reddy@laptop&quot; -f ~/.ssh/id_ed25519\nssh-copy-id -i ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub deploy@10.0.1.20\nssh deploy@10.0.1.20\nssh-add ~/.ssh/id_ed25519\nssh-add -l\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;reddy@laptop&quot;\u003c/span> -f ~/.ssh/id_ed25519\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ssh-copy-id -i ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub deploy@10.0.1.20\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ssh deploy@10.0.1.20\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ssh-add ~/.ssh/id_ed25519\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ssh-add -l\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>ssh-agent\u003c/code> holds the unlocked key in memory so you type the passphrase once. On first connection SSH records the server&#39;s fingerprint in \u003ccode>~/.ssh/known_hosts\u003c/code>. If the fingerprint later changes, SSH warns loudly. Do not blindly delete the line: first confirm whether the server was rebuilt.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-client-config\">The client config\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"Host bastion\n    HostName 203.0.113.10\n    User ops\n    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519\n\nHost app-*\n    User deploy\n    ProxyJump bastion\n    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519\n    ServerAliveInterval 30\n\nHost *\n    AddKeysToAgent yes\n    IdentitiesOnly yes\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">Host bastion\n    HostName 203.0.113.10\n    User ops\n    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519\n\nHost app-*\n    User deploy\n    ProxyJump bastion\n    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519\n    ServerAliveInterval 30\n\nHost *\n    AddKeysToAgent yes\n    IdentitiesOnly yes\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>With this, \u003ccode>ssh app-1\u003c/code> connects through the bastion automatically. \u003cstrong>ProxyJump\u003c/strong> is the safe way to reach private servers: your private key stays on your laptop and never sits on the bastion, unlike agent forwarding to untrusted hosts.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"hardening-the-server\">Hardening the server\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Edit \u003ccode>/etc/ssh/sshd_config\u003c/code> (or a file in \u003ccode>sshd_config.d/\u003c/code>) and test before reloading.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"PermitRootLogin no\nPasswordAuthentication no\nPubkeyAuthentication yes\nAllowUsers deploy ops\nMaxAuthTries 3\nClientAliveInterval 300\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">PermitRootLogin no\nPasswordAuthentication no\nPubkeyAuthentication yes\nAllowUsers deploy ops\nMaxAuthTries 3\nClientAliveInterval 300\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo sshd -t\nsudo systemctl reload sshd\nsudo ss -tlnp | grep sshd\nsudo journalctl -u ssh --since &quot;1 hour ago&quot; | grep -i failed | tail\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> sshd -t\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> systemctl reload sshd\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> ss -tlnp | grep sshd\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> journalctl -u ssh --since \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;1 hour ago&quot;\u003c/span> | grep -i failed | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Always keep an existing session open while you test a new one, so a typo does not lock you out. Add rate limiting with \u003ccode>fail2ban\u003c/code> or a cloud security group that limits port 22 to known addresses.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tunnels-and-file-transfer\">Tunnels and file transfer\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ssh -L 5433:db.internal:5432 app-1\nssh -N -D 1080 bastion\nscp ./release.tar.gz app-1:/tmp/\nrsync -avz --delete ./site/ app-1:/var/www/site/\nrsync -avzn ./site/ app-1:/var/www/site/\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ssh -L 5433:db.internal:5432 app-1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ssh -N -D 1080 bastion\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>scp ./release.tar.gz app-1:/tmp/\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>rsync -avz --delete ./site/ app-1:/var/www/site/\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>rsync -avzn ./site/ app-1:/var/www/site/\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>-L\u003c/code> forwards a local port to a remote destination, useful for reaching a private database from your laptop. \u003ccode>rsync\u003c/code> copies only differences, and \u003ccode>-n\u003c/code> is a dry run, which you should use before any \u003ccode>--delete\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-leaked-key-on-a-shared-bastion\">Incident: the leaked key on a shared bastion\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Engineers copy their private keys to a shared bastion so they can hop to internal servers. A contractor&#39;s account on the bastion is compromised, and the attacker reads every key in every home directory.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Private keys were stored where other people and processes could reach them. Remediation and prevention:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo find /home -name &quot;id_*&quot; -not -name &quot;*.pub&quot; 2&gt;/dev/null\nsudo chmod 700 /home/*/.ssh\nsudo sed -i 's/^#\\?PasswordAuthentication.*/PasswordAuthentication no/' /etc/ssh/sshd_config\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> find /home -name \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;id_*&quot;\u003c/span> -not -name \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;*.pub&quot;\u003c/span> 2&gt;/dev/null\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">chmod\u003c/span> 700 /home/*/.ssh\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> sed -i \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;s/^#\\?PasswordAuthentication.*/PasswordAuthentication no/&#x27;\u003c/span> /etc/ssh/sshd_config\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Rotate every exposed key, remove old entries from \u003ccode>authorized_keys\u003c/code>, switch to \u003ccode>ProxyJump\u003c/code> so keys stay on laptops, and consider short-lived certificates or a cloud session manager so there are no long-lived keys at all.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using password authentication on internet-facing hosts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Permissions on \u003ccode>~/.ssh\u003c/code> or the private key that are too open (SSH refuses them)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forwarding the agent to hosts you do not fully trust\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Editing sshd_config and reloading without testing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deleting a \u003ccode>known_hosts\u003c/code> warning without investigating\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"keys-instead-of-passwords","text":"Keys instead of passwords"},{"id":"the-client-config","text":"The client config"},{"id":"hardening-the-server","text":"Hardening the server"},{"id":"tunnels-and-file-transfer","text":"Tunnels and file transfer"},{"id":"incident-the-leaked-key-on-a-shared-bastion","text":"Incident: the leaked key on a shared bastion"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"OpenSSH manual pages","u":"https://www.openssh.com/manual.html"},{"t":"Ubuntu Server documentation","u":"https://ubuntu.com/server/docs"},{"t":"Red Hat Enterprise Linux documentation","u":"https://docs.redhat.com/"}],"text":"SSH is how you reach nearly every Linux server. It encrypts the session, authenticates with keys and can carry file transfers and network tunnels. A weak SSH setup is one of the most common ways servers get compromised. What you will learn Generating and using key pairs Writing ~/.ssh/config so connections are short and consistent Hardening sshd and using bastion (jump) hosts Port forwarding, scp and rsync Keys instead of passwords A key pair has a private key that never leaves your machine and a public key placed on servers. Use the modern Ed25519 type and protect the private key with a passphrase. $ ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C \"reddy@laptop\" -f ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 $ ssh-copy-id -i ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub deploy@10.0.1.20 $ ssh deploy@10.0.1.20 $ ssh-add ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 $ ssh-add -l ssh-agent holds the unlocked key in memory so you type the passphrase once. On first connection SSH records the server&#39;s fingerprint in ~/.ssh/known_hosts . If the fingerprint later changes, SSH warns loudly. Do not blindly delete the line: first confirm whether the server was rebuilt. The client config Host bastion HostName 203.0.113.10 User ops IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 Host app-* User deploy ProxyJump bastion IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 ServerAliveInterval 30 Host * AddKeysToAgent yes IdentitiesOnly yes With this, ssh app-1 connects through the bastion automatically. ProxyJump is the safe way to reach private servers: your private key stays on your laptop and never sits on the bastion, unlike agent forwarding to untrusted hosts. Hardening the server Edit /etc/ssh/sshd_config (or a file in sshd_config.d/ ) and test before reloading. PermitRootLogin no PasswordAuthentication no PubkeyAuthentication yes AllowUsers deploy ops MaxAuthTries 3 ClientAliveInterval 300 $ sudo sshd -t $ sudo systemctl reload sshd $ sudo ss -tlnp | grep sshd $ sudo journalctl -u ssh --since \"1 hour ago\" | grep -i failed | tail Always keep an existing session open while you test a new one, so a typo does not lock you out. Add rate limiting with fail2ban or a cloud security group that limits port 22 to known addresses. Tunnels and file transfer $ ssh -L 5433:db.internal:5432 app-1 $ ssh -N -D 1080 bastion $ scp ./release.tar.gz app-1:/tmp/ $ rsync -avz --delete ./site/ app-1:/var/www/site/ $ rsync -avzn ./site/ app-1:/var/www/site/ -L forwards a local port to a remote destination, useful for reaching a private database from your laptop. rsync copies only differences, and -n is a dry run, which you should use before any --delete . Incident: the leaked key on a shared bastion Incident Engineers copy their private keys to a shared bastion so they can hop to internal servers. A contractor&#39;s account on the bastion is compromised, and the attacker reads every key in every home directory. Private keys were stored where other people and processes could reach them. Remediation and prevention: $ sudo find /home -name \"id_*\" -not -name \"*.pub\" 2>/dev/null $ sudo chmod 700 /home/*/.ssh $ sudo sed -i &#x27;s/^#\\?PasswordAuthentication.*/PasswordAuthentication no/&#x27; /etc/ssh/sshd_config Rotate every exposed key, remove old entries from authorized_keys , switch to ProxyJump so keys stay on laptops, and consider short-lived certificates or a cloud session manager so there are no long-lived keys at all. Common mistakes Using password authentication on internet-facing hosts Permissions on ~/.ssh or the private key that are too open (SSH refuses them) Forwarding the agent to hosts you do not fully trust Editing sshd_config and reloading without testing Deleting a known_hosts warning without investigating"},"linux/cron-and-timers":{"html":"\u003cp>Backups, certificate renewals, log cleanup and reports all run on a schedule. Scheduled jobs fail silently more often than any other kind of automation, so the way you set them up matters as much as what they do.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Cron syntax and where crontabs live\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why cron jobs fail with &quot;works in my shell&quot;\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>systemd timers and when they are better\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Locking, logging and alerting for scheduled jobs\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cron-syntax\">Cron syntax\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A crontab line has five time fields and a command: minute, hour, day of month, month, day of week.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# m  h  dom mon dow  command\n*/5  *  *   *   *    /usr/local/bin/check-queue.sh\n30   2  *   *   1-5  /usr/local/bin/backup.sh\n0    4  1   *   *    /usr/local/bin/monthly-report.sh\n@reboot               /usr/local/bin/warm-cache.sh\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\"># m  h  dom mon dow  command\n*/5  *  *   *   *    /usr/local/bin/check-queue.sh\n30   2  *   *   1-5  /usr/local/bin/backup.sh\n0    4  1   *   *    /usr/local/bin/monthly-report.sh\n@reboot               /usr/local/bin/warm-cache.sh\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"crontab -l\ncrontab -e\nsudo crontab -u deploy -l\nls /etc/cron.d /etc/cron.daily\nsudo grep CRON /var/log/syslog | tail -5\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>crontab -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>crontab -e\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> crontab -u deploy -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> /etc/cron.d /etc/cron.daily\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> grep CRON /var/log/syslog | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -5\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Per-user crontabs are edited with \u003ccode>crontab -e\u003c/code>. System-wide jobs live in \u003ccode>/etc/cron.d/\u003c/code> and have an extra \u003cstrong>user\u003c/strong> field after the time fields. Use a site like crontab.guru to double-check an unfamiliar expression, and remember that cron uses the server&#39;s timezone, which in the cloud is usually UTC.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"why-cron-jobs-fail\">Why cron jobs fail\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Cron starts jobs with a \u003cstrong>tiny environment\u003c/strong>: a minimal PATH, no profile files, no interactive variables, and the current directory is the home directory. A script that works when you run it by hand can fail under cron.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"SHELL=/bin/bash\nPATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin\nMAILTO=ops@example.com\n\n30 2 * * * /opt/backup/run.sh &gt;&gt; /var/log/backup.log 2&gt;&amp;1\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">SHELL=/bin/bash\nPATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin\nMAILTO=ops@example.com\n\n30 2 * * * /opt/backup/run.sh &gt;&gt; /var/log/backup.log 2&gt;&amp;1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Set \u003ccode>PATH\u003c/code> explicitly, use absolute paths for every command and file, and redirect output. Output that goes nowhere is lost, and output that goes to local mail is usually never read.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"preventing-overlap-with-locks\">Preventing overlap with locks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If a job takes longer than its interval, cron starts a second copy on top of the first. Use \u003ccode>flock\u003c/code> so only one runs at a time:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"flock -n /var/lock/backup.lock /opt/backup/run.sh || echo &quot;previous run still active&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>flock -n /var/lock/backup.lock /opt/backup/run.sh || \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;previous run still active&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>In a crontab: \u003ccode>*/10 * * * * flock -n /var/lock/sync.lock /opt/sync.sh\u003c/code>. The \u003ccode>-n\u003c/code> flag makes the second instance exit immediately instead of waiting.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"systemd-timers\">systemd timers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A timer is a unit that triggers a service unit. You get journal logging, dependency handling, resource limits and a status command for free.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ini\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"[Unit]\nDescription=Nightly backup\n\n[Service]\nType=oneshot\nExecStart=/opt/backup/run.sh\nUser=backup\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">[Unit]\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Description\u003c/span>=Nightly backup\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">[Service]\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Type\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">on\u003c/span>eshot\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ExecStart\u003c/span>=/opt/backup/run.sh\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">User\u003c/span>=backup\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ini\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"[Unit]\nDescription=Run backup nightly\n\n[Timer]\nOnCalendar=*-*-* 02:30:00\nPersistent=true\nRandomizedDelaySec=300\n\n[Install]\nWantedBy=timers.target\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">[Unit]\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Description\u003c/span>=Run backup nightly\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">[Timer]\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">OnCalendar\u003c/span>=*-*-* \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">02\u003c/span>:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">30\u003c/span>:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">00\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Persistent\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">RandomizedDelaySec\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">300\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">[Install]\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">WantedBy\u003c/span>=timers.target\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Save them as \u003ccode>backup.service\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>backup.timer\u003c/code> in \u003ccode>/etc/systemd/system/\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo systemctl daemon-reload\nsudo systemctl enable --now backup.timer\nsystemctl list-timers --all\nsystemctl status backup.service\njournalctl -u backup.service --since today\nsystemd-analyze calendar &quot;Mon..Fri 02:30&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> systemctl daemon-reload\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> systemctl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">enable\u003c/span> --now backup.timer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>systemctl list-timers --all\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>systemctl status backup.service\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>journalctl -u backup.service --since today\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>systemd-analyze calendar \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Mon..Fri 02:30&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>Persistent=true\u003c/code> runs a missed job after the machine comes back from downtime, which cron does not do. \u003ccode>RandomizedDelaySec\u003c/code> spreads load when many hosts share a schedule.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"know-when-it-did-not-run\">Know when it did not run\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A job that never ran is invisible. Make success observable: have the job ping a monitoring URL or write a metric on success, and alert when the signal is missing for longer than expected (a dead man&#39;s switch).\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"/opt/backup/run.sh &amp;&amp; curl -fsS -m 10 https://hc.example.com/ping/abc123\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>/opt/backup/run.sh &amp;&amp; curl -fsS -m 10 https://hc.example.com/ping/abc123\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-certificate-that-did-not-renew\">Incident: the certificate that did not renew\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>The certificate renewal cron job has been failing for two months. It worked when tested by hand. Now the production certificate expires at midnight and customers see browser warnings.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>The job used \u003ccode>certbot\u003c/code> from \u003ccode>/usr/local/bin\u003c/code>, which cron&#39;s default PATH did not include, and output was mailed to a root mailbox nobody reads. Fix it properly:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo grep -r certbot /etc/cron.d /var/spool/cron 2&gt;/dev/null\nsudo certbot renew --dry-run\nsudo systemctl list-timers | grep certbot\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> grep -r certbot /etc/cron.d /var/spool/cron 2&gt;/dev/null\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> certbot renew --dry-run\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> systemctl list-timers | grep certbot\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use an absolute path or set PATH, log to a file the monitoring agent watches, run \u003ccode>renew --dry-run\u003c/code> in CI or weekly, and add an alert that fires when any certificate has fewer than 21 days left. Renewal should never depend on a human noticing an email.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Relying on the interactive PATH and environment\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not redirecting output, so failures leave no trace\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Overlapping runs of a slow job\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming the cron timezone matches yours\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Having no alert when the job stops running\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"cron-syntax","text":"Cron syntax"},{"id":"why-cron-jobs-fail","text":"Why cron jobs fail"},{"id":"preventing-overlap-with-locks","text":"Preventing overlap with locks"},{"id":"systemd-timers","text":"systemd timers"},{"id":"know-when-it-did-not-run","text":"Know when it did not run"},{"id":"incident-the-certificate-that-did-not-renew","text":"Incident: the certificate that did not renew"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"systemd.timer manual","u":"https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.timer.html"},{"t":"systemd manual pages","u":"https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/"},{"t":"Linux man-pages (man7.org)","u":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/index.html"}],"text":"Backups, certificate renewals, log cleanup and reports all run on a schedule. Scheduled jobs fail silently more often than any other kind of automation, so the way you set them up matters as much as what they do. What you will learn Cron syntax and where crontabs live Why cron jobs fail with \"works in my shell\" systemd timers and when they are better Locking, logging and alerting for scheduled jobs Cron syntax A crontab line has five time fields and a command: minute, hour, day of month, month, day of week. # m h dom mon dow command */5 * * * * /usr/local/bin/check-queue.sh 30 2 * * 1-5 /usr/local/bin/backup.sh 0 4 1 * * /usr/local/bin/monthly-report.sh @reboot /usr/local/bin/warm-cache.sh $ crontab -l $ crontab -e $ sudo crontab -u deploy -l $ ls /etc/cron.d /etc/cron.daily $ sudo grep CRON /var/log/syslog | tail -5 Per-user crontabs are edited with crontab -e . System-wide jobs live in /etc/cron.d/ and have an extra user field after the time fields. Use a site like crontab.guru to double-check an unfamiliar expression, and remember that cron uses the server&#39;s timezone, which in the cloud is usually UTC. Why cron jobs fail Cron starts jobs with a tiny environment : a minimal PATH, no profile files, no interactive variables, and the current directory is the home directory. A script that works when you run it by hand can fail under cron. SHELL=/bin/bash PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin MAILTO=ops@example.com 30 2 * * * /opt/backup/run.sh >> /var/log/backup.log 2>&1 Set PATH explicitly, use absolute paths for every command and file, and redirect output. Output that goes nowhere is lost, and output that goes to local mail is usually never read. Preventing overlap with locks If a job takes longer than its interval, cron starts a second copy on top of the first. Use flock so only one runs at a time: $ flock -n /var/lock/backup.lock /opt/backup/run.sh || echo \"previous run still active\" In a crontab: */10 * * * * flock -n /var/lock/sync.lock /opt/sync.sh . The -n flag makes the second instance exit immediately instead of waiting. systemd timers A timer is a unit that triggers a service unit. You get journal logging, dependency handling, resource limits and a status command for free. [Unit] Description =Nightly backup [Service] Type = on eshot ExecStart =/opt/backup/run.sh User =backup [Unit] Description =Run backup nightly [Timer] OnCalendar =*-*-* 02 : 30 : 00 Persistent = true RandomizedDelaySec = 300 [Install] WantedBy =timers.target Save them as backup.service and backup.timer in /etc/systemd/system/ . $ sudo systemctl daemon-reload $ sudo systemctl enable --now backup.timer $ systemctl list-timers --all $ systemctl status backup.service $ journalctl -u backup.service --since today $ systemd-analyze calendar \"Mon..Fri 02:30\" Persistent=true runs a missed job after the machine comes back from downtime, which cron does not do. RandomizedDelaySec spreads load when many hosts share a schedule. Know when it did not run A job that never ran is invisible. Make success observable: have the job ping a monitoring URL or write a metric on success, and alert when the signal is missing for longer than expected (a dead man&#39;s switch). $ /opt/backup/run.sh && curl -fsS -m 10 https://hc.example.com/ping/abc123 Incident: the certificate that did not renew Incident The certificate renewal cron job has been failing for two months. It worked when tested by hand. Now the production certificate expires at midnight and customers see browser warnings. The job used certbot from /usr/local/bin , which cron&#39;s default PATH did not include, and output was mailed to a root mailbox nobody reads. Fix it properly: $ sudo grep -r certbot /etc/cron.d /var/spool/cron 2>/dev/null $ sudo certbot renew --dry-run $ sudo systemctl list-timers | grep certbot Use an absolute path or set PATH, log to a file the monitoring agent watches, run renew --dry-run in CI or weekly, and add an alert that fires when any certificate has fewer than 21 days left. Renewal sh"},"linux/logs-and-troubleshooting":{"html":"\u003cp>Users report random \u003ccode>502 Bad Gateway\u003c/code> errors around 14:03. The web server looks healthy and CPU is idle. The answer is in the logs, but only if you know which logs to read, how to narrow them to a time window, and how to connect two sources. This lesson builds that routine.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Where Linux keeps logs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to filter \u003ccode>journalctl\u003c/code> by unit, time and severity\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to correlate application, web server and kernel logs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to spot an out-of-memory kill\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"two-places-logs-live\">Two places logs live\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Modern systems use \u003cstrong>journald\u003c/strong>, queried with \u003ccode>journalctl\u003c/code>, for services run by systemd. Many programs also write plain files under \u003ccode>/var/log\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Source\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Look here\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>For\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>journald\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>journalctl -u SERVICE\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Anything started by systemd\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>/var/log/nginx/\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>access.log\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>error.log\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Web requests and proxy errors\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>/var/log/auth.log\u003c/code> (Debian family) or \u003ccode>/var/log/secure\u003c/code> (Red Hat family)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Logins and \u003ccode>sudo\u003c/code> use\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Security events\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Kernel ring buffer\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>dmesg -T\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Hardware, disk and out-of-memory events\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"journalctl-filters-you-will-use-constantly\">journalctl filters you will use constantly\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"journalctl -u shop --since &quot;14:00&quot; --until &quot;14:10&quot;   # one service, one window\njournalctl -u shop -p err -n 50 --no-pager           # errors and worse, last 50\njournalctl -b -1 -p err                              # errors from the previous boot\njournalctl -f                                        # follow everything live\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">journalctl -u shop --since \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;14:00&quot;\u003c/span> --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">until\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;14:10&quot;\u003c/span>   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># one service, one window\u003c/span>\njournalctl -u shop -p err -n 50 --no-pager           \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># errors and worse, last 50\u003c/span>\njournalctl -b -1 -p err                              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># errors from the previous boot\u003c/span>\njournalctl -f                                        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># follow everything live\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Severity runs from \u003ccode>emerg\u003c/code> (0) to \u003ccode>debug\u003c/code> (7). \u003ccode>-p err\u003c/code> shows \u003ccode>err\u003c/code> and anything more severe.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"searching-plain-log-files\">Searching plain log files\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep -c &quot; 502 &quot; /var/log/nginx/access.log\nawk '$9 == 502 {print $4}' /var/log/nginx/access.log | cut -c2-15 | sort | uniq -c\ntail -n 3 /var/log/nginx/error.log\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot; 502 &quot;\u003c/span> /var/log/nginx/access.log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">41\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>awk \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;$9 == 502 {print $4}&#x27;\u003c/span> /var/log/nginx/access.log | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cut\u003c/span> -c2-15 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">uniq\u003c/span> -c\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">     41 24/Oct/2026:14\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -n 3 /var/log/nginx/error.log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2026/10/24 14:03:11 [error] 1032#1032: *9021 connect() failed (111: Connection refused) while connecting to upstream, upstream: &quot;http://127.0.0.1:8080/&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The default nginx access log puts the status code in field 9, so \u003ccode>awk &#39;$9 == 502&#39;\u003c/code> filters on it. Always include the time field when you aggregate, because the pattern in time is the clue. \u003ccode>less +F file\u003c/code> follows a file like \u003ccode>tail -f\u003c/code> but lets you scroll back.\u003c/p>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-tip\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Tip\u003c/div>\u003cp>Make timestamps comparable before correlating. Check the time zone with \u003ccode>timedatectl\u003c/code>. Mixing UTC and local time is a classic way to look at the wrong minute.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-502-errors-every-few-hours\">Incident: 502 errors every few hours\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>502\u003c/code> responses cluster at 14:03. nginx reports \u003ccode>Connection refused\u003c/code> to the upstream on port 8080.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>&quot;Connection refused&quot; means nothing was listening on 8080 at that moment. Why not? Ask the service&#39;s journal for the same minute:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"journalctl -u shop --since &quot;14:00&quot; --until &quot;14:10&quot; --no-pager\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>journalctl -u shop --since \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;14:00&quot;\u003c/span> --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">until\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;14:10&quot;\u003c/span> --no-pager\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Oct 24 14:03:05 app01 systemd[1]: shop.service: Main process exited, code=killed, status=9/KILL\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Oct 24 14:03:05 app01 systemd[1]: shop.service: Failed with result 'signal'.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Oct 24 14:03:10 app01 systemd[1]: shop.service: Scheduled restart job, restart counter is at 1.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Oct 24 14:03:10 app01 systemd[1]: Started Shop API.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The process did not crash by itself. It was killed with signal 9 and systemd restarted it five seconds later, which matches the five-second window of refused connections. Nobody ran \u003ccode>kill -9\u003c/code>, so ask the kernel:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo dmesg -T | grep -i -E &quot;out of memory|killed process&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> dmesg -T | grep -i -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;out of memory|killed process&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">[Sat Oct 24 14:03:05 2026] Out of memory: Killed process 2211 (server) total-vm:6291456kB, anon-rss:3932160kB, file-rss:0kB, shmem-rss:0kB\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The kernel&#39;s \u003cstrong>OOM killer\u003c/strong> chose the application because the machine ran out of memory. Confirm the trend with \u003ccode>free -h\u003c/code> and check whether the service has a memory limit. Real fixes are to reduce the application&#39;s memory use, add memory, or set a cgroup limit (\u003ccode>MemoryMax=\u003c/code> in the unit) so only this service is affected. Restarting more often hides the problem.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The method is the point: \u003cstrong>symptom\u003c/strong> (502), \u003cstrong>proximate cause\u003c/strong> (connection refused), \u003cstrong>event\u003c/strong> (process killed), \u003cstrong>root cause\u003c/strong> (OOM). Each step used a different log.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"log-rotation\">Log rotation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Logs grow without bound unless rotated. \u003ccode>logrotate\u003c/code> renames, compresses and deletes old logs on a schedule, configured in \u003ccode>/etc/logrotate.d/\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"/var/log/app/*.log {\n    daily\n    rotate 14\n    compress\n    delaycompress\n    missingok\n    notifempty\n    copytruncate\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">/var/log/app/*.log {\n    daily\n    rotate 14\n    compress\n    delaycompress\n    missingok\n    notifempty\n    copytruncate\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>copytruncate\u003c/code> copies the log and empties the original so programs that keep the file open do not need a restart. Test a config with \u003ccode>logrotate -d /etc/logrotate.d/app\u003c/code>, which prints what would happen without doing it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"sym&gt;stat;sym&gt;jour;stat&gt;cause;jour&gt;cause;cause&gt;fix\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Follow a failing service through the logs\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sym\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Symptom\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"stat\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">systemctl status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"jour\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">journalctl -u\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cause\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Cause\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"fix\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Fix and verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Symptom to systemctl status; 2, Symptom to journalctl -u; 3, systemctl status to Cause; 4, journalctl -u to Cause; 5, Cause to Fix and verify.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sym\">\u003cdt>Symptom \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A user reports an error or a monitor fires.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Without a time and a name, you search blindly.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"stat\">\u003cdt>systemctl status \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Shows state, exit code and the last log lines of the unit.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A recent restart can hide the first failure; look further back.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"jour\">\u003cdt>journalctl -u \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Shows all unit logs with timestamps. Use --since to narrow.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If the journal is not persistent, logs from before a reboot are gone.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cause\">\u003cdt>Cause \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Compare the first error with the last change: a config, a package or a disk.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Fixing the later error instead of the first one leads you away.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"fix\">\u003cdt>Fix and verify \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Change one thing, restart, and watch the logs confirm the fix.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Changing several things at once makes it unclear what worked.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reading only the latest lines instead of the minute when the problem occurred\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Comparing logs without confirming they use the same time zone\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Stopping at &quot;the process died&quot; without asking what killed it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deleting log files by hand instead of configuring rotation\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"two-places-logs-live","text":"Two places logs live"},{"id":"journalctl-filters-you-will-use-constantly","text":"journalctl filters you will use constantly"},{"id":"searching-plain-log-files","text":"Searching plain log files"},{"id":"incident-502-errors-every-few-hours","text":"Incident: 502 errors every few hours"},{"id":"log-rotation","text":"Log rotation"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"journalctl manual","u":"https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/journalctl.html"},{"t":"systemd documentation","u":"https://systemd.io/"},{"t":"Linux man-pages (man7.org)","u":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/index.html"}],"text":"Users report random 502 Bad Gateway errors around 14:03. The web server looks healthy and CPU is idle. The answer is in the logs, but only if you know which logs to read, how to narrow them to a time window, and how to connect two sources. This lesson builds that routine. What you will learn Where Linux keeps logs How to filter journalctl by unit, time and severity How to correlate application, web server and kernel logs How to spot an out-of-memory kill Two places logs live Modern systems use journald , queried with journalctl , for services run by systemd. Many programs also write plain files under /var/log . Source Look here For journald journalctl -u SERVICE Anything started by systemd /var/log/nginx/ access.log , error.log Web requests and proxy errors /var/log/auth.log (Debian family) or /var/log/secure (Red Hat family) Logins and sudo use Security events Kernel ring buffer dmesg -T Hardware, disk and out-of-memory events journalctl filters you will use constantly journalctl -u shop --since \"14:00\" -- until \"14:10\" # one service, one window journalctl -u shop -p err -n 50 --no-pager # errors and worse, last 50 journalctl -b -1 -p err # errors from the previous boot journalctl -f # follow everything live Severity runs from emerg (0) to debug (7). -p err shows err and anything more severe. Searching plain log files $ grep -c \" 502 \" /var/log/nginx/access.log 41 $ awk &#x27;$9 == 502 {print $4}&#x27; /var/log/nginx/access.log | cut -c2-15 | sort | uniq -c 41 24/Oct/2026:14 $ tail -n 3 /var/log/nginx/error.log 2026/10/24 14:03:11 [error] 1032#1032: *9021 connect() failed (111: Connection refused) while connecting to upstream, upstream: \"http://127.0.0.1:8080/\" The default nginx access log puts the status code in field 9, so awk &#39;$9 == 502&#39; filters on it. Always include the time field when you aggregate, because the pattern in time is the clue. less +F file follows a file like tail -f but lets you scroll back. Tip Make timestamps comparable before correlating. Check the time zone with timedatectl . Mixing UTC and local time is a classic way to look at the wrong minute. Incident: 502 errors every few hours Incident 502 responses cluster at 14:03. nginx reports Connection refused to the upstream on port 8080. \"Connection refused\" means nothing was listening on 8080 at that moment. Why not? Ask the service&#39;s journal for the same minute: $ journalctl -u shop --since \"14:00\" -- until \"14:10\" --no-pager Oct 24 14:03:05 app01 systemd[1]: shop.service: Main process exited, code=killed, status=9/KILL Oct 24 14:03:05 app01 systemd[1]: shop.service: Failed with result 'signal'. Oct 24 14:03:10 app01 systemd[1]: shop.service: Scheduled restart job, restart counter is at 1. Oct 24 14:03:10 app01 systemd[1]: Started Shop API. The process did not crash by itself. It was killed with signal 9 and systemd restarted it five seconds later, which matches the five-second window of refused connections. Nobody ran kill -9 , so ask the kernel: $ sudo dmesg -T | grep -i -E \"out of memory|killed process\" [Sat Oct 24 14:03:05 2026] Out of memory: Killed process 2211 (server) total-vm:6291456kB, anon-rss:3932160kB, file-rss:0kB, shmem-rss:0kB The kernel&#39;s OOM killer chose the application because the machine ran out of memory. Confirm the trend with free -h and check whether the service has a memory limit. Real fixes are to reduce the application&#39;s memory use, add memory, or set a cgroup limit ( MemoryMax= in the unit) so only this service is affected. Restarting more often hides the problem. The method is the point: symptom (502), proximate cause (connection refused), event (process killed), root cause (OOM). Each step used a different log. Log rotation Logs grow without bound unless rotated. logrotate renames, compresses and deletes old logs on a schedule, configured in /etc/logrotate.d/ . /var/log/app/*.log { daily rotate 14 compress delaycompress missingok notifempty copytruncate } copytruncate copies the log and empties the origin"},"linux/performance-triage":{"html":"\u003cp>The server &quot;feels slow&quot;. Dashboards are not available yet, and you have a shell. A good engineer does not start guessing or restarting things. They run the same short checklist every time and let the numbers decide whether the bottleneck is CPU, memory, disk or network.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How to read load average correctly\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A five-command triage sequence\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to separate CPU saturation from disk waiting\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The USE method: utilization, saturation, errors\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"load-average-is-a-queue-length\">Load average is a queue length\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"uptime\nnproc\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">uptime\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 02:44:10 up 12 days,  3:02,  2 users,  load average: 11.86, 9.40, 6.12\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">nproc\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">4\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The three numbers are the average count of tasks that were running or waiting to run over the last 1, 5 and 15 minutes. On Linux this includes tasks blocked on disk I/O. Compare them with the number of CPU cores from \u003ccode>nproc\u003c/code>. A load of 4 on a 4-core machine is full but healthy. A load of 11.86 on 4 cores means work is queueing, and the 1-minute number being higher than the 15-minute one means it is getting worse.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Load alone does not say \u003cstrong>why\u003c/strong>. You need the next commands.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-use-method\">The USE method\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>For each resource (CPU, memory, disk, network) ask three questions:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Question\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Example evidence\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Utilization\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>How busy is it?\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>CPU 95% busy\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Saturation\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Is work waiting?\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Run queue longer than cores, swapping\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Errors\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Is it failing?\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Disk errors in \u003ccode>dmesg\u003c/code>, packet drops\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Checking all three for each resource stops you from fixating on the first thing that looks odd.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-five-command-triage\">A five-command triage\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"uptime              # 1. load trend\nvmstat 1 5          # 2. CPU, memory, swap and I/O wait together\nfree -h             # 3. memory, focusing on &quot;available&quot;\niostat -xz 1 3      # 4. per-disk utilization and latency (package: sysstat)\nss -s               # 5. socket totals; then top for the process view\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">uptime\u003c/span>              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># 1. load trend\u003c/span>\nvmstat 1 5          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># 2. CPU, memory, swap and I/O wait together\u003c/span>\nfree -h             \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># 3. memory, focusing on &quot;available&quot;\u003c/span>\niostat -xz 1 3      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># 4. per-disk utilization and latency (package: sysstat)\u003c/span>\nss -s               \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># 5. socket totals; then top for the process view\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>free -h\u003c/code> has a column named \u003ccode>available\u003c/code>. It estimates how much memory can be used without swapping, including reclaimable cache. Low \u003ccode>free\u003c/code> with a large \u003ccode>available\u003c/code> is normal, because Linux uses spare memory as file cache.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reading-vmstat\">Reading vmstat\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"vmstat 1 3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>vmstat 1 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">procs -----------memory---------- ---swap-- -----io---- -system-- ------cpu-----\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> r  b   swpd   free   buff  cache   si   so    bi    bo   in   cs us sy id wa st\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 1  7      0 213456  10240 812344    0    0  5120   980 1200 2400  4  3 12 81  0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 0  8      0 211320  10240 814012    0    0  4980  1010 1180 2350  3  2 14 81  0\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This is sample output. Focus on a few columns:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>r\u003c/code>: tasks waiting to run on the CPU. Persistently above the core count means CPU saturation.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>b\u003c/code>: tasks blocked, usually waiting on disk.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>si\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>so\u003c/code>: memory swapped in and out. Sustained non-zero values mean memory pressure.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>us\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>sy\u003c/code>: CPU time in user code and kernel code. \u003ccode>id\u003c/code> is idle.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>wa\u003c/code>: CPU time spent waiting for I/O to complete.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-load-is-12-but-the-cpus-are-idle\">Incident: load is 12 but the CPUs are idle\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>The reporting server has load 11.86 on 4 cores, yet users say only exports are slow.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>In the \u003ccode>vmstat\u003c/code> output above, \u003ccode>r\u003c/code> is 1 and \u003ccode>id\u003c/code> is 12, so the CPU is not the limit. But \u003ccode>b\u003c/code> is 7 and \u003ccode>wa\u003c/code> is 81. Seven tasks are blocked and 81% of CPU time is spent waiting for I/O. This is a \u003cstrong>disk\u003c/strong> problem. Find which device with \u003ccode>iostat\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"iostat -xz 1 2\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>iostat -xz 1 2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Device   r/s    w/s   rkB/s   wkB/s  r_await  w_await  aqu-sz  %util\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">sda     2.0    4.0    16.0    48.0     1.1      2.0    0.01    0.5\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">sdb   410.0   35.0 52480.0  4400.0    38.5     12.2   16.90   99.8\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>sdb\u003c/code> is 99.8% utilized with a 38 ms read wait and a deep queue (\u003ccode>aqu-sz\u003c/code>). That is the database volume. Now find the process doing the reading:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo iotop -oP -n 1\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> iotop -oP -n 1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  PID  PRIO  USER   DISK READ  DISK WRITE  COMMAND\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 3187  be/4  app    48.20 M/s     0.00 B/s  python3 /opt/report/export.py\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A nightly export is scanning large tables at the same time as users query. Options include scheduling the job off-peak, lowering its priority with \u003ccode>ionice -c3\u003c/code>, adding an index so it reads less, or moving it to a read replica. The triage did not require guessing, because each command removed a hypothesis.\u003c/p>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-why-it-matters\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Why it matters\u003c/div>\u003cp>Rebooting would have cleared the symptom for an hour and taught you nothing. A five-minute triage gives you a cause you can fix and explain.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-to-check-when-it-is-not-the-disk\">What to check when it is not the disk\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>CPU saturated:\u003c/strong> high \u003ccode>us\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>r\u003c/code> above core count. Use \u003ccode>top\u003c/code> sorted by CPU (\u003ccode>P\u003c/code>) and look for a runaway process.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Memory pressure:\u003c/strong> \u003ccode>si\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>so\u003c/code> non-zero, \u003ccode>available\u003c/code> low, OOM events in \u003ccode>dmesg\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Network:\u003c/strong> \u003ccode>ss -s\u003c/code> for socket counts, \u003ccode>ip -s link\u003c/code> for errors and drops.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reading load average without comparing it to the number of cores\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating low \u003ccode>free\u003c/code> memory as a problem when \u003ccode>available\u003c/code> is large\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Restarting the service before capturing evidence\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Looking at averages only, instead of watching \u003ccode>vmstat 1\u003c/code> for a few seconds\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"load-average-is-a-queue-length","text":"Load average is a queue length"},{"id":"the-use-method","text":"The USE method"},{"id":"a-five-command-triage","text":"A five-command triage"},{"id":"reading-vmstat","text":"Reading vmstat"},{"id":"incident-load-is-12-but-the-cpus-are-idle","text":"Incident: load is 12 but the CPUs are idle"},{"id":"what-to-check-when-it-is-not-the-disk","text":"What to check when it is not the disk"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Linux performance (Brendan Gregg)","u":"https://www.brendangregg.com/linuxperf.html"},{"t":"proc(5) manual","u":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/proc.5.html"},{"t":"The Linux kernel documentation","u":"https://docs.kernel.org/"}],"text":"The server \"feels slow\". Dashboards are not available yet, and you have a shell. A good engineer does not start guessing or restarting things. They run the same short checklist every time and let the numbers decide whether the bottleneck is CPU, memory, disk or network. What you will learn How to read load average correctly A five-command triage sequence How to separate CPU saturation from disk waiting The USE method: utilization, saturation, errors Load average is a queue length $ uptime 02:44:10 up 12 days, 3:02, 2 users, load average: 11.86, 9.40, 6.12 $ nproc 4 The three numbers are the average count of tasks that were running or waiting to run over the last 1, 5 and 15 minutes. On Linux this includes tasks blocked on disk I/O. Compare them with the number of CPU cores from nproc . A load of 4 on a 4-core machine is full but healthy. A load of 11.86 on 4 cores means work is queueing, and the 1-minute number being higher than the 15-minute one means it is getting worse. Load alone does not say why . You need the next commands. The USE method For each resource (CPU, memory, disk, network) ask three questions: Question Meaning Example evidence Utilization How busy is it? CPU 95% busy Saturation Is work waiting? Run queue longer than cores, swapping Errors Is it failing? Disk errors in dmesg , packet drops Checking all three for each resource stops you from fixating on the first thing that looks odd. A five-command triage uptime # 1. load trend vmstat 1 5 # 2. CPU, memory, swap and I/O wait together free -h # 3. memory, focusing on \"available\" iostat -xz 1 3 # 4. per-disk utilization and latency (package: sysstat) ss -s # 5. socket totals; then top for the process view free -h has a column named available . It estimates how much memory can be used without swapping, including reclaimable cache. Low free with a large available is normal, because Linux uses spare memory as file cache. Reading vmstat $ vmstat 1 3 procs -----------memory---------- ---swap-- -----io---- -system-- ------cpu----- r b swpd free buff cache si so bi bo in cs us sy id wa st 1 7 0 213456 10240 812344 0 0 5120 980 1200 2400 4 3 12 81 0 0 8 0 211320 10240 814012 0 0 4980 1010 1180 2350 3 2 14 81 0 This is sample output. Focus on a few columns: r : tasks waiting to run on the CPU. Persistently above the core count means CPU saturation. b : tasks blocked, usually waiting on disk. si / so : memory swapped in and out. Sustained non-zero values mean memory pressure. us / sy : CPU time in user code and kernel code. id is idle. wa : CPU time spent waiting for I/O to complete. Incident: load is 12 but the CPUs are idle Incident The reporting server has load 11.86 on 4 cores, yet users say only exports are slow. In the vmstat output above, r is 1 and id is 12, so the CPU is not the limit. But b is 7 and wa is 81. Seven tasks are blocked and 81% of CPU time is spent waiting for I/O. This is a disk problem. Find which device with iostat : $ iostat -xz 1 2 Device r/s w/s rkB/s wkB/s r_await w_await aqu-sz %util sda 2.0 4.0 16.0 48.0 1.1 2.0 0.01 0.5 sdb 410.0 35.0 52480.0 4400.0 38.5 12.2 16.90 99.8 sdb is 99.8% utilized with a 38 ms read wait and a deep queue ( aqu-sz ). That is the database volume. Now find the process doing the reading: $ sudo iotop -oP -n 1 PID PRIO USER DISK READ DISK WRITE COMMAND 3187 be/4 app 48.20 M/s 0.00 B/s python3 /opt/report/export.py A nightly export is scanning large tables at the same time as users query. Options include scheduling the job off-peak, lowering its priority with ionice -c3 , adding an index so it reads less, or moving it to a read replica. The triage did not require guessing, because each command removed a hypothesis. Why it matters Rebooting would have cleared the symptom for an hour and taught you nothing. A five-minute triage gives you a cause you can fix and explain. What to check when it is not the disk CPU saturated: high us and r above core count. Use top sorted by CPU ( P ) and look for a runaway process. Memory pr"},"linux/boot-and-kernel":{"html":"\u003cp>When a server does not come back after a reboot, you have no SSH session, no logs from the usual tools and a console that shows only a few lines. Knowing the boot sequence tells you where to look.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The stages of the Linux boot process\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading boot logs and unit failures\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Kernel parameters, modules and \u003ccode>sysctl\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Recovering with rescue mode and fixing fstab errors\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-sequence\">The sequence\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Firmware\u003c/strong> (UEFI or BIOS) runs hardware checks and finds a boot device.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The \u003cstrong>bootloader\u003c/strong> (GRUB) loads the kernel and an initial RAM filesystem (initramfs).\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The \u003cstrong>kernel\u003c/strong> initialises hardware, mounts the root filesystem and starts PID 1.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>systemd\u003c/strong> (PID 1) mounts filesystems from fstab, starts services in dependency order and reaches a target such as \u003ccode>multi-user.target\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Each stage has its own failure signature. A GRUB prompt means the bootloader or its config is broken. A kernel panic mentioning &quot;unable to mount root fs&quot; means the initramfs or root device is wrong. A hang with &quot;A start job is running for dev-disk-by-uuid&quot; means systemd is waiting for a filesystem in fstab that does not exist.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reading-the-boot\">Reading the boot\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"systemd-analyze\nsystemd-analyze blame | head\nsystemd-analyze critical-chain\njournalctl -b -p err\njournalctl -b -1 -p err | tail -20\nsystemctl --failed\ndmesg --level=err,warn | tail\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>systemd-analyze\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>systemd-analyze blame | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>systemd-analyze critical-chain\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>journalctl -b -p err\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>journalctl -b -1 -p err | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -20\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>systemctl --failed\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>dmesg --level=err,warn | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>journalctl -b -1\u003c/code> shows the previous boot, which is essential after a crash. If the journal is not persistent, create \u003ccode>/var/log/journal\u003c/code> and restart journald so logs survive reboots.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-kernel-and-its-tunables\">The kernel and its tunables\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"uname -r\nlsmod | head -5\nmodinfo overlay | head -5\nsudo modprobe br_netfilter\nsysctl net.ipv4.ip_forward\nsudo sysctl -w net.ipv4.ip_forward=1\ncat /proc/cmdline\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">uname\u003c/span> -r\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>lsmod | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -5\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>modinfo overlay | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -5\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> modprobe br_netfilter\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>sysctl net.ipv4.ip_forward\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> sysctl -w net.ipv4.ip_forward=1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> /proc/cmdline\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>sysctl -w\u003c/code> changes a value until reboot. To keep it, put it in a file under \u003ccode>/etc/sysctl.d/\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"net.ipv4.ip_forward = 1\nvm.swappiness = 10\nfs.inotify.max_user_watches = 524288\nnet.core.somaxconn = 4096\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">net.ipv4.ip_forward = 1\nvm.swappiness = 10\nfs.inotify.max_user_watches = 524288\nnet.core.somaxconn = 4096\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo sysctl --system\ncat /etc/modules-load.d/k8s.conf\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> sysctl --system\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> /etc/modules-load.d/k8s.conf\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Kubernetes nodes, for example, need \u003ccode>br_netfilter\u003c/code> loaded and IP forwarding enabled. Modules listed in \u003ccode>/etc/modules-load.d/\u003c/code> load automatically at boot.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"recovery\">Recovery\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If the system drops to an emergency shell, the root filesystem is usually mounted read-only. Check what failed, then fix it:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"journalctl -xb | tail -30\nmount -o remount,rw /\nblkid\nnano /etc/fstab\nmount -a\nsystemctl default\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>journalctl -xb | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -30\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>mount -o remount,rw /\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>blkid\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>nano /etc/fstab\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>mount -a\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>systemctl default\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>If you cannot get a shell at all, boot with a rescue ISO, mount the root volume at \u003ccode>/mnt\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>chroot\u003c/code> into it. In the cloud, detach the root volume, attach it to a healthy instance, fix the file and reattach it. At the GRUB menu, pressing \u003ccode>e\u003c/code> lets you edit the kernel line once, for example adding \u003ccode>systemd.unit=rescue.target\u003c/code> to reach single-user mode.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-reboot-that-never-came-back\">Incident: the reboot that never came back\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After patching, a database server does not respond. The cloud console shows it running, but the serial console is stuck on &quot;A start job is running for /dev/disk/by-uuid/...&quot; for 1m 30s repeated. Someone had replaced a data disk last week, and its old UUID is still in fstab.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>systemd waits for a device that will never appear. Boot to emergency or rescue mode and correct the entry:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"blkid | grep data\nsudo nano /etc/fstab\nsudo mount -a &amp;&amp; echo fstab ok\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>blkid | grep data\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> nano /etc/fstab\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> mount -a &amp;&amp; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> fstab ok\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Prevent a repeat by testing with \u003ccode>mount -a\u003c/code> after every fstab edit, using \u003ccode>nofail,x-systemd.device-timeout=10s\u003c/code> on non-critical mounts, and rebooting staging hosts after changes so problems show up before production.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Editing fstab without running \u003ccode>mount -a\u003c/code> to test it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving the journal non-persistent, so crash logs disappear\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Changing sysctl values with \u003ccode>-w\u003c/code> and forgetting to persist them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Updating the kernel without keeping a previous one to fall back to\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Panicking and rebooting repeatedly instead of reading the previous boot&#39;s log\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-sequence","text":"The sequence"},{"id":"reading-the-boot","text":"Reading the boot"},{"id":"the-kernel-and-its-tunables","text":"The kernel and its tunables"},{"id":"recovery","text":"Recovery"},{"id":"incident-the-reboot-that-never-came-back","text":"Incident: the reboot that never came back"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"GNU GRUB manual","u":"https://www.gnu.org/software/grub/manual/grub/grub.html"},{"t":"Kernel command-line parameters","u":"https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/kernel-parameters.html"},{"t":"Documentation for /proc/sys (sysctl)","u":"https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/sysctl/index.html"}],"text":"When a server does not come back after a reboot, you have no SSH session, no logs from the usual tools and a console that shows only a few lines. Knowing the boot sequence tells you where to look. What you will learn The stages of the Linux boot process Reading boot logs and unit failures Kernel parameters, modules and sysctl Recovering with rescue mode and fixing fstab errors The sequence Firmware (UEFI or BIOS) runs hardware checks and finds a boot device. The bootloader (GRUB) loads the kernel and an initial RAM filesystem (initramfs). The kernel initialises hardware, mounts the root filesystem and starts PID 1. systemd (PID 1) mounts filesystems from fstab, starts services in dependency order and reaches a target such as multi-user.target . Each stage has its own failure signature. A GRUB prompt means the bootloader or its config is broken. A kernel panic mentioning \"unable to mount root fs\" means the initramfs or root device is wrong. A hang with \"A start job is running for dev-disk-by-uuid\" means systemd is waiting for a filesystem in fstab that does not exist. Reading the boot $ systemd-analyze $ systemd-analyze blame | head $ systemd-analyze critical-chain $ journalctl -b -p err $ journalctl -b -1 -p err | tail -20 $ systemctl --failed $ dmesg --level=err,warn | tail journalctl -b -1 shows the previous boot, which is essential after a crash. If the journal is not persistent, create /var/log/journal and restart journald so logs survive reboots. The kernel and its tunables $ uname -r $ lsmod | head -5 $ modinfo overlay | head -5 $ sudo modprobe br_netfilter $ sysctl net.ipv4.ip_forward $ sudo sysctl -w net.ipv4.ip_forward=1 $ cat /proc/cmdline sysctl -w changes a value until reboot. To keep it, put it in a file under /etc/sysctl.d/ : net.ipv4.ip_forward = 1 vm.swappiness = 10 fs.inotify.max_user_watches = 524288 net.core.somaxconn = 4096 $ sudo sysctl --system $ cat /etc/modules-load.d/k8s.conf Kubernetes nodes, for example, need br_netfilter loaded and IP forwarding enabled. Modules listed in /etc/modules-load.d/ load automatically at boot. Recovery If the system drops to an emergency shell, the root filesystem is usually mounted read-only. Check what failed, then fix it: $ journalctl -xb | tail -30 $ mount -o remount,rw / $ blkid $ nano /etc/fstab $ mount -a $ systemctl default If you cannot get a shell at all, boot with a rescue ISO, mount the root volume at /mnt and chroot into it. In the cloud, detach the root volume, attach it to a healthy instance, fix the file and reattach it. At the GRUB menu, pressing e lets you edit the kernel line once, for example adding systemd.unit=rescue.target to reach single-user mode. Incident: the reboot that never came back Incident After patching, a database server does not respond. The cloud console shows it running, but the serial console is stuck on \"A start job is running for /dev/disk/by-uuid/...\" for 1m 30s repeated. Someone had replaced a data disk last week, and its old UUID is still in fstab. systemd waits for a device that will never appear. Boot to emergency or rescue mode and correct the entry: $ blkid | grep data $ sudo nano /etc/fstab $ sudo mount -a && echo fstab ok Prevent a repeat by testing with mount -a after every fstab edit, using nofail,x-systemd.device-timeout=10s on non-critical mounts, and rebooting staging hosts after changes so problems show up before production. Common mistakes Editing fstab without running mount -a to test it Leaving the journal non-persistent, so crash logs disappear Changing sysctl values with -w and forgetting to persist them Updating the kernel without keeping a previous one to fall back to Panicking and rebooting repeatedly instead of reading the previous boot&#39;s log"},"linux/security-hardening":{"html":"\u003cp>A new server is secure only if you make it so. Hardening means removing what you do not need, restricting what remains and watching for changes. Most breaches use known weaknesses: weak passwords, exposed services, unpatched software and excess privilege.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Finding and closing unnecessary listening services\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A host firewall with a default-deny stance\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mandatory access control with SELinux and AppArmor\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Auditing changes and scanning for weaknesses\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"shrink-the-attack-surface\">Shrink the attack surface\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Start by finding what is listening, then decide whether each service is needed on this host.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo ss -tulpn\nsystemctl list-unit-files --state=enabled --type=service | head -20\nsudo systemctl disable --now cups avahi-daemon\ndpkg -l | wc -l\nsudo apt purge -y telnet rsh-client\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> ss -tulpn\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>systemctl list-unit-files --state=enabled --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">type\u003c/span>=service | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -20\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> systemctl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">disable\u003c/span> --now cups avahi-daemon\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>dpkg -l | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> apt purge -y telnet rsh-client\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Every listening port is an entry point. If a database should only be reached from the app tier, it should bind to a private interface (\u003ccode>bind-address = 10.0.1.5\u003c/code>), not \u003ccode>0.0.0.0\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-default-deny-firewall\">A default-deny firewall\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo ufw default deny incoming\nsudo ufw default allow outgoing\nsudo ufw allow from 203.0.113.0/24 to any port 22 proto tcp\nsudo ufw allow 443/tcp\nsudo ufw enable\nsudo ufw status numbered\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> ufw default deny incoming\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> ufw default allow outgoing\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> ufw allow from 203.0.113.0/24 to any port 22 proto tcp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> ufw allow 443/tcp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> ufw \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">enable\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> ufw status numbered\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>On RHEL-family systems the same idea uses \u003ccode>firewalld\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=https\nsudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-rich-rule='rule family=&quot;ipv4&quot; source address=&quot;203.0.113.0/24&quot; service name=&quot;ssh&quot; accept'\nsudo firewall-cmd --reload\nsudo firewall-cmd --list-all\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=https\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> firewall-cmd --permanent --add-rich-rule=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;rule family=&quot;ipv4&quot; source address=&quot;203.0.113.0/24&quot; service name=&quot;ssh&quot; accept&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> firewall-cmd --reload\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> firewall-cmd --list-all\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Cloud security groups are the first layer and a host firewall the second. Do both.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"least-privilege\">Least privilege\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Give each application its own non-login user and keep files owned by it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use \u003ccode>sudo\u003c/code> rules for specific commands, never blanket \u003ccode>ALL\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Remove the setuid bit from binaries that do not need it\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo visudo -f /etc/sudoers.d/deploy\nsudo find / -xdev -perm -4000 -type f 2&gt;/dev/null\nsudo find / -xdev -type f -perm -0002 2&gt;/dev/null | head\ngetent passwd | awk -F: '$7 !~ /nologin|false/ {print $1}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> visudo -f /etc/sudoers.d/deploy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> find / -xdev -perm -4000 -\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">type\u003c/span> f 2&gt;/dev/null\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> find / -xdev -\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">type\u003c/span> f -perm -0002 2&gt;/dev/null | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>getent passwd | awk -F: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;$7 !~ /nologin|false/ {print $1}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A drop-in sudoers line such as \u003ccode>deploy ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /bin/systemctl restart myapp\u003c/code> lets the deploy user restart one service and nothing else.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"selinux-and-apparmor\">SELinux and AppArmor\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Standard permissions answer &quot;does this user own the file&quot;. \u003cstrong>Mandatory access control\u003c/strong> also asks &quot;should this program touch this resource at all&quot;. RHEL-family systems use SELinux, Ubuntu uses AppArmor.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"getenforce\nsudo ausearch -m avc -ts recent | tail -5\nsudo semanage port -a -t http_port_t -p tcp 8081\nsudo restorecon -Rv /srv/www\nsudo aa-status | head\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>getenforce\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> ausearch -m avc -ts recent | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -5\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> semanage port -a -t http_port_t -p tcp 8081\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> restorecon -Rv /srv/www\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> aa-status | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>When SELinux blocks something, \u003cstrong>do not disable it\u003c/strong>. Read the denial, then fix the file label or add the specific port or boolean the application legitimately needs. Disabling the control removes a safety net that has stopped real attacks.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"audit-and-verify\">Audit and verify\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo apt install -y auditd lynis\nsudo auditctl -w /etc/passwd -p wa -k identity\nsudo ausearch -k identity | tail -3\nsudo lynis audit system | tail -25\nlast -n 10\nsudo lastb | head\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> apt install -y auditd lynis\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> auditctl -w /etc/passwd -p wa -k identity\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> ausearch -k identity | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> lynis audit system | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -25\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>last -n 10\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> lastb | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>auditd\u003c/code> records who changed watched files. \u003ccode>lynis\u003c/code> and CIS benchmarks give a prioritised checklist. Ship audit logs off the host, because an attacker&#39;s first move is to edit local logs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-forgotten-test-database\">Incident: the forgotten test database\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A security scan finds MongoDB answering on a public IP with no authentication. It was started for a quick test months ago with \u003ccode>--bind_ip_all\u003c/code>, and the security group allowed all traffic. Customer records are exposed.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Contain first: close the exposure, preserve evidence, then investigate.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo ss -tlnp | grep 27017\nsudo ufw deny 27017\nsudo systemctl stop mongod\nsudo journalctl -u mongod --since &quot;30 days ago&quot; | grep -i &quot;connection accepted&quot; | head\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> ss -tlnp | grep 27017\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> ufw deny 27017\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> systemctl stop mongod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> journalctl -u mongod --since \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;30 days ago&quot;\u003c/span> | grep -i \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;connection accepted&quot;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Review connection logs for unknown sources, rotate any credentials stored in the database, and notify according to your policy. Prevent a repeat with an inventory of listening services, default-deny security groups, and automated scans that alert on any port open to the internet.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Disabling SELinux or the firewall to &quot;fix&quot; a problem\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Binding databases and caches to all interfaces\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Shared accounts, and sudo rules that grant everything\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Hardening a server once, then never re-checking after changes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keeping audit logs only on the server being attacked\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"shrink-the-attack-surface","text":"Shrink the attack surface"},{"id":"a-default-deny-firewall","text":"A default-deny firewall"},{"id":"least-privilege","text":"Least privilege"},{"id":"selinux-and-apparmor","text":"SELinux and AppArmor"},{"id":"audit-and-verify","text":"Audit and verify"},{"id":"incident-the-forgotten-test-database","text":"Incident: the forgotten test database"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"CIS Benchmarks","u":"https://www.cisecurity.org/cis-benchmarks"},{"t":"SELinux Project","u":"https://selinuxproject.org/page/Main_Page"},{"t":"Ubuntu Server documentation: security and AppArmor","u":"https://ubuntu.com/server/docs"}],"text":"A new server is secure only if you make it so. Hardening means removing what you do not need, restricting what remains and watching for changes. Most breaches use known weaknesses: weak passwords, exposed services, unpatched software and excess privilege. What you will learn Finding and closing unnecessary listening services A host firewall with a default-deny stance Mandatory access control with SELinux and AppArmor Auditing changes and scanning for weaknesses Shrink the attack surface Start by finding what is listening, then decide whether each service is needed on this host. $ sudo ss -tulpn $ systemctl list-unit-files --state=enabled -- type =service | head -20 $ sudo systemctl disable --now cups avahi-daemon $ dpkg -l | wc -l $ sudo apt purge -y telnet rsh-client Every listening port is an entry point. If a database should only be reached from the app tier, it should bind to a private interface ( bind-address = 10.0.1.5 ), not 0.0.0.0 . A default-deny firewall $ sudo ufw default deny incoming $ sudo ufw default allow outgoing $ sudo ufw allow from 203.0.113.0/24 to any port 22 proto tcp $ sudo ufw allow 443/tcp $ sudo ufw enable $ sudo ufw status numbered On RHEL-family systems the same idea uses firewalld : $ sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=https $ sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-rich-rule= &#x27;rule family=\"ipv4\" source address=\"203.0.113.0/24\" service name=\"ssh\" accept&#x27; $ sudo firewall-cmd --reload $ sudo firewall-cmd --list-all Cloud security groups are the first layer and a host firewall the second. Do both. Least privilege Give each application its own non-login user and keep files owned by it Use sudo rules for specific commands, never blanket ALL Remove the setuid bit from binaries that do not need it $ sudo visudo -f /etc/sudoers.d/deploy $ sudo find / -xdev -perm -4000 - type f 2>/dev/null $ sudo find / -xdev - type f -perm -0002 2>/dev/null | head $ getent passwd | awk -F: &#x27;$7 !~ /nologin|false/ {print $1}&#x27; A drop-in sudoers line such as deploy ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /bin/systemctl restart myapp lets the deploy user restart one service and nothing else. SELinux and AppArmor Standard permissions answer \"does this user own the file\". Mandatory access control also asks \"should this program touch this resource at all\". RHEL-family systems use SELinux, Ubuntu uses AppArmor. $ getenforce $ sudo ausearch -m avc -ts recent | tail -5 $ sudo semanage port -a -t http_port_t -p tcp 8081 $ sudo restorecon -Rv /srv/www $ sudo aa-status | head When SELinux blocks something, do not disable it . Read the denial, then fix the file label or add the specific port or boolean the application legitimately needs. Disabling the control removes a safety net that has stopped real attacks. Audit and verify $ sudo apt install -y auditd lynis $ sudo auditctl -w /etc/passwd -p wa -k identity $ sudo ausearch -k identity | tail -3 $ sudo lynis audit system | tail -25 $ last -n 10 $ sudo lastb | head auditd records who changed watched files. lynis and CIS benchmarks give a prioritised checklist. Ship audit logs off the host, because an attacker&#39;s first move is to edit local logs. Incident: the forgotten test database Incident A security scan finds MongoDB answering on a public IP with no authentication. It was started for a quick test months ago with --bind_ip_all , and the security group allowed all traffic. Customer records are exposed. Contain first: close the exposure, preserve evidence, then investigate. $ sudo ss -tlnp | grep 27017 $ sudo ufw deny 27017 $ sudo systemctl stop mongod $ sudo journalctl -u mongod --since \"30 days ago\" | grep -i \"connection accepted\" | head Review connection logs for unknown sources, rotate any credentials stored in the database, and notify according to your policy. Prevent a repeat with an inventory of listening services, default-deny security groups, and automated scans that alert on any port open to the internet. Common mistakes Disabling SELinux or the firewall to \"fix\" a "},"linux/namespaces-and-cgroups":{"html":"\u003cp>A container is not a small virtual machine. It is an ordinary Linux process that the kernel has been told to treat in two special ways: it sees a restricted view of the system, and it may use only a limited amount of resources. Both features are in the kernel itself, and you can use them without Docker. Knowing this makes container behaviour, limits and escapes much easier to reason about.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The main namespace types and what each isolates\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How cgroups limit CPU, memory and process counts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to look at both for any running process\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why memory limits kill processes, and why &quot;container root&quot; is still root\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"namespaces-a-restricted-view\">Namespaces: a restricted view\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Namespace\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Isolates\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Effect\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>pid\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Process ids\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The first process inside is pid 1 and cannot see host processes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>net\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Network devices, addresses, routes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Its own interfaces and ports\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>mnt\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Mount points\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Its own filesystem view\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>uts\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Hostname\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Its own hostname\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>ipc\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Shared memory and queues\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>No sharing with other groups\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>user\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>User and group ids\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Root inside can be a normal user outside\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>cgroup\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The cgroup tree\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Hides the host&#39;s resource layout\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo unshare --pid --fork --mount-proc --uts bash\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> unshare --pid --fork --mount-proc --uts bash\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"># hostname lab &amp;&amp; ps aux\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">USER  PID %CPU %MEM COMMAND\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">root    1  0.0  0.1 bash\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">root    8  0.0  0.0 ps aux\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Inside the new shell only two processes exist, because the pid namespace hides the rest. Every process has links to its namespaces in \u003ccode>/proc\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ls -l /proc/$$/ns            # namespace ids of the current shell\nsudo nsenter -t 4321 -n ip a # run `ip a` inside the network namespace of process 4321\nlsns                         # list namespaces on the host\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> -l /proc/$$/ns            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># namespace ids of the current shell\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> nsenter -t 4321 -n ip a \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># run `ip a` inside the network namespace of process 4321\u003c/span>\nlsns                         \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># list namespaces on the host\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>nsenter\u003c/code> is the way to debug a container that has no tools: run the host&#39;s tools inside the container&#39;s namespaces.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cgroups-a-limited-budget\">cgroups: a limited budget\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Control groups account for and limit resources of a set of processes. On modern systems (cgroup v2) they appear as files:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"cat /sys/fs/cgroup/system.slice/shop.service/memory.max     # memory limit in bytes\ncat /sys/fs/cgroup/system.slice/shop.service/memory.current # use right now\ncat /sys/fs/cgroup/system.slice/shop.service/cpu.max        # quota and period, e.g. 50000 100000 = half a CPU\ncat /sys/fs/cgroup/system.slice/shop.service/pids.max\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> /sys/fs/cgroup/system.slice/shop.service/memory.max     \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># memory limit in bytes\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> /sys/fs/cgroup/system.slice/shop.service/memory.current \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># use right now\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> /sys/fs/cgroup/system.slice/shop.service/cpu.max        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># quota and period, e.g. 50000 100000 = half a CPU\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> /sys/fs/cgroup/system.slice/shop.service/pids.max\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Memory is a hard limit: when a group goes over \u003ccode>memory.max\u003c/code> and cannot free memory, the kernel kills a process in it (an OOM kill, exit code 137). CPU is a throttle: a group over its quota is paused until the next period, which shows up as latency, not as a crash. Check \u003ccode>cpu.stat\u003c/code> for \u003ccode>nr_throttled\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>systemd uses cgroups too: \u003ccode>systemctl set-property shop.service MemoryMax=512M CPUQuota=50%\u003c/code> sets limits on a service with no containers at all.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-this-means-in-practice\">What this means in practice\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A container shares the host kernel, so a kernel bug affects every container. This is the main difference from a virtual machine.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Root in a container is real root unless a user namespace maps it. Drop capabilities, run as non-root, and use seccomp profiles.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tools inside a container may show the host&#39;s memory or CPU count, not the limit, so applications must read cgroup values to size themselves.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A container has a CPU limit of 0.5 CPU and an application that wants a full core. What does the application experience?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> It is throttled: it runs for part of each scheduling period and waits for the rest, so requests become slower, but it is not killed.\u003c/p>\u003cp>CPU is a rate limit. Memory is the resource that causes kills. Look at nr_throttled in cpu.stat to confirm throttling.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-java-service-that-kept-dying\">Incident: the Java service that kept dying\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A service in a container with a 1 GB limit is killed every few hours with exit code 137, while its own heap setting looks safe.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"cat /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.max\ncat /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.events\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.max\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">1073741824\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.events\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">oom_kill 7\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The runtime saw the host&#39;s 64 GB and sized its heap and thread stacks for that, so total process memory (heap plus native memory) went past 1 GB. The team set the heap as a percentage of the container limit, left room for native memory, and alerted on \u003ccode>memory.current\u003c/code> approaching \u003ccode>memory.max\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Thinking a container is a virtual machine\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading \u003ccode>free\u003c/code> inside a container and trusting it as the limit\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Setting a memory limit equal to the heap size\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Giving containers capabilities &quot;to make it work&quot;\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"namespaces-a-restricted-view","text":"Namespaces: a restricted view"},{"id":"cgroups-a-limited-budget","text":"cgroups: a limited budget"},{"id":"what-this-means-in-practice","text":"What this means in practice"},{"id":"incident-the-java-service-that-kept-dying","text":"Incident: the Java service that kept dying"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"namespaces(7) manual page","u":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/namespaces.7.html"},{"t":"cgroups(7) manual page","u":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/cgroups.7.html"}],"text":"A container is not a small virtual machine. It is an ordinary Linux process that the kernel has been told to treat in two special ways: it sees a restricted view of the system, and it may use only a limited amount of resources. Both features are in the kernel itself, and you can use them without Docker. Knowing this makes container behaviour, limits and escapes much easier to reason about. What you will learn The main namespace types and what each isolates How cgroups limit CPU, memory and process counts How to look at both for any running process Why memory limits kill processes, and why \"container root\" is still root Namespaces: a restricted view Namespace Isolates Effect pid Process ids The first process inside is pid 1 and cannot see host processes net Network devices, addresses, routes Its own interfaces and ports mnt Mount points Its own filesystem view uts Hostname Its own hostname ipc Shared memory and queues No sharing with other groups user User and group ids Root inside can be a normal user outside cgroup The cgroup tree Hides the host&#39;s resource layout $ sudo unshare --pid --fork --mount-proc --uts bash # hostname lab && ps aux USER PID %CPU %MEM COMMAND root 1 0.0 0.1 bash root 8 0.0 0.0 ps aux Inside the new shell only two processes exist, because the pid namespace hides the rest. Every process has links to its namespaces in /proc : ls -l /proc/$$/ns # namespace ids of the current shell sudo nsenter -t 4321 -n ip a # run `ip a` inside the network namespace of process 4321 lsns # list namespaces on the host nsenter is the way to debug a container that has no tools: run the host&#39;s tools inside the container&#39;s namespaces. cgroups: a limited budget Control groups account for and limit resources of a set of processes. On modern systems (cgroup v2) they appear as files: cat /sys/fs/cgroup/system.slice/shop.service/memory.max # memory limit in bytes cat /sys/fs/cgroup/system.slice/shop.service/memory.current # use right now cat /sys/fs/cgroup/system.slice/shop.service/cpu.max # quota and period, e.g. 50000 100000 = half a CPU cat /sys/fs/cgroup/system.slice/shop.service/pids.max Memory is a hard limit: when a group goes over memory.max and cannot free memory, the kernel kills a process in it (an OOM kill, exit code 137). CPU is a throttle: a group over its quota is paused until the next period, which shows up as latency, not as a crash. Check cpu.stat for nr_throttled . systemd uses cgroups too: systemctl set-property shop.service MemoryMax=512M CPUQuota=50% sets limits on a service with no containers at all. What this means in practice A container shares the host kernel, so a kernel bug affects every container. This is the main difference from a virtual machine. Root in a container is real root unless a user namespace maps it. Drop capabilities, run as non-root, and use seccomp profiles. Tools inside a container may show the host&#39;s memory or CPU count, not the limit, so applications must read cgroup values to size themselves. Predict first. A container has a CPU limit of 0.5 CPU and an application that wants a full core. What does the application experience? Your guess Answer: It is throttled: it runs for part of each scheduling period and waits for the rest, so requests become slower, but it is not killed. CPU is a rate limit. Memory is the resource that causes kills. Look at nr_throttled in cpu.stat to confirm throttling. Incident: the Java service that kept dying Incident A service in a container with a 1 GB limit is killed every few hours with exit code 137, while its own heap setting looks safe. $ cat /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.max 1073741824 $ cat /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.events oom_kill 7 The runtime saw the host&#39;s 64 GB and sized its heap and thread stacks for that, so total process memory (heap plus native memory) went past 1 GB. The team set the heap as a percentage of the container limit, left room for native memory, and alerted on memory.current approaching memory.max . Common mistakes Thinking "},"linux/working-efficiently-in-the-terminal":{"html":"\u003cp>Operations work happens in a terminal, often over a slow connection and often under stress. Small habits decide whether you type a command twice or ten times, lose a session when the network drops, or run a destructive command in the wrong place. This lesson collects the habits that pay off most.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Shell history and line-editing shortcuts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Persistent sessions with \u003ccode>tmux\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Basic editing in \u003ccode>vim\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>nano\u003c/code> over SSH\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Aliases, functions and a prompt that prevents mistakes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keeping your setup in dotfiles\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"history-and-shortcuts\">History and shortcuts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Keys\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Action\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>Ctrl+R\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Search history backwards while typing\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>Ctrl+A\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>Ctrl+E\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Start or end of the line\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>Ctrl+W\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Delete the previous word\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>Alt+.\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Insert the last argument of the previous command\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>!!\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Repeat the previous command (\u003ccode>sudo !!\u003c/code> runs it with sudo)\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>Ctrl+L\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Clear the screen\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"HISTSIZE=50000 HISTFILESIZE=100000\nHISTCONTROL=ignoreboth:erasedups   # skip duplicates and commands starting with a space\nshopt -s histappend                # keep history from all open shells\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">HISTSIZE=50000 HISTFILESIZE=100000\nHISTCONTROL=ignoreboth:erasedups   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># skip duplicates and commands starting with a space\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">shopt\u003c/span> -s histappend                \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># keep history from all open shells\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A command starting with a space is not saved, which is useful when a secret appears on the command line (better still: avoid secrets on command lines).\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"sessions-that-survive-tmux\">Sessions that survive: tmux\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"tmux new -s fix          # start a named session\n# Ctrl+B then d          # detach, the session keeps running\ntmux attach -t fix       # come back later, even from another connection\n# Ctrl+B then %          # split left and right\n# Ctrl+B then &quot;          # split top and bottom\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">tmux new -s fix          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># start a named session\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># Ctrl+B then d          # detach, the session keeps running\u003c/span>\ntmux attach -t fix       \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># come back later, even from another connection\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># Ctrl+B then %          # split left and right\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># Ctrl+B then &quot;          # split top and bottom\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A long job started inside \u003ccode>tmux\u003c/code> is not lost if your connection drops, and a colleague can attach to the same session to watch or help.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"editing-on-a-server\">Editing on a server\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Learn the few keys of one editor that exists everywhere. In \u003ccode>vim\u003c/code>: \u003ccode>i\u003c/code> to insert, \u003ccode>Esc\u003c/code> to leave insert mode, \u003ccode>:w\u003c/code> save, \u003ccode>:q\u003c/code> quit, \u003ccode>:q!\u003c/code> quit without saving, \u003ccode>/word\u003c/code> search, \u003ccode>dd\u003c/code> delete a line, \u003ccode>u\u003c/code> undo. If you prefer, \u003ccode>nano\u003c/code> shows its keys at the bottom. Edit with \u003ccode>sudoedit /etc/file\u003c/code> rather than \u003ccode>sudo vim\u003c/code>, so the editor runs as you and the change is saved safely.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"aliases-functions-and-a-safer-prompt\">Aliases, functions and a safer prompt\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"alias ll='ls -lah'\nalias k=kubectl\nmkcd() { mkdir -p &quot;$1&quot; &amp;&amp; cd &quot;$1&quot;; }\nexport PS1='\\u@\\h:\\w\\$ '    # show user, host and directory\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">alias\u003c/span> ll=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;ls -lah&#x27;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">alias\u003c/span> k=kubectl\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-function\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-title\">mkcd\u003c/span>\u003c/span>() { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">mkdir\u003c/span> -p \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$1\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> &amp;&amp; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cd\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$1\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>; }\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">export\u003c/span> PS1=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;\\u@\\h:\\w\\$ &#x27;\u003c/span>    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># show user, host and directory\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Show the host name clearly, and use a different colour for production hosts, so you notice before running a destructive command. For Kubernetes, show the current context in the prompt for the same reason.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"dotfiles\">Dotfiles\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Keep \u003ccode>.bashrc\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>.vimrc\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>.tmux.conf\u003c/code> and similar files in one directory under version control, and install them with a small script that creates symbolic links. A new machine then feels familiar in minutes. Keep secrets out of dotfiles.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You typed a long command and notice a typo near the start. What is the fastest safe edit?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Use Ctrl+A or Alt+B to move to the typo and fix it, or press Ctrl+X Ctrl+E to open the line in your editor.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Editing the line avoids retyping and avoids running a half-typed command. Ctrl+X Ctrl+E opens the command in $EDITOR in bash.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-wrong-host\">Incident: the wrong host\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An engineer runs \u003ccode>rm -rf /var/lib/app/cache\u003c/code> to free space, in a terminal that was connected to production instead of staging.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"hostname\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>hostname\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">prod-web-03\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Both terminals looked the same. The team set a red background and a clear host name in the prompt for production, added a confirmation wrapper for destructive commands on production hosts, and used \u003ccode>tmux\u003c/code> window names such as &quot;STAGING&quot; and &quot;PROD&quot;.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Long jobs run without \u003ccode>tmux\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>nohup\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Identical-looking prompts for every environment\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Editing system files as root in a full editor session\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Secrets typed on the command line and kept in history\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"history-and-shortcuts","text":"History and shortcuts"},{"id":"sessions-that-survive-tmux","text":"Sessions that survive: tmux"},{"id":"editing-on-a-server","text":"Editing on a server"},{"id":"aliases-functions-and-a-safer-prompt","text":"Aliases, functions and a safer prompt"},{"id":"dotfiles","text":"Dotfiles"},{"id":"incident-the-wrong-host","text":"Incident: the wrong host"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"GNU Bash manual: Command line editing","u":"https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/Command-Line-Editing.html"},{"t":"Bash history facilities","u":"https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/Bash-History-Facilities.html"}],"text":"Operations work happens in a terminal, often over a slow connection and often under stress. Small habits decide whether you type a command twice or ten times, lose a session when the network drops, or run a destructive command in the wrong place. This lesson collects the habits that pay off most. What you will learn Shell history and line-editing shortcuts Persistent sessions with tmux Basic editing in vim or nano over SSH Aliases, functions and a prompt that prevents mistakes Keeping your setup in dotfiles History and shortcuts Keys Action Ctrl+R Search history backwards while typing Ctrl+A / Ctrl+E Start or end of the line Ctrl+W Delete the previous word Alt+. Insert the last argument of the previous command !! Repeat the previous command ( sudo !! runs it with sudo) Ctrl+L Clear the screen HISTSIZE=50000 HISTFILESIZE=100000 HISTCONTROL=ignoreboth:erasedups # skip duplicates and commands starting with a space shopt -s histappend # keep history from all open shells A command starting with a space is not saved, which is useful when a secret appears on the command line (better still: avoid secrets on command lines). Sessions that survive: tmux tmux new -s fix # start a named session # Ctrl+B then d # detach, the session keeps running tmux attach -t fix # come back later, even from another connection # Ctrl+B then % # split left and right # Ctrl+B then \" # split top and bottom A long job started inside tmux is not lost if your connection drops, and a colleague can attach to the same session to watch or help. Editing on a server Learn the few keys of one editor that exists everywhere. In vim : i to insert, Esc to leave insert mode, :w save, :q quit, :q! quit without saving, /word search, dd delete a line, u undo. If you prefer, nano shows its keys at the bottom. Edit with sudoedit /etc/file rather than sudo vim , so the editor runs as you and the change is saved safely. Aliases, functions and a safer prompt alias ll= &#x27;ls -lah&#x27; alias k=kubectl mkcd () { mkdir -p \" $1 \" && cd \" $1 \" ; } export PS1= &#x27;\\u@\\h:\\w\\$ &#x27; # show user, host and directory Show the host name clearly, and use a different colour for production hosts, so you notice before running a destructive command. For Kubernetes, show the current context in the prompt for the same reason. Dotfiles Keep .bashrc , .vimrc , .tmux.conf and similar files in one directory under version control, and install them with a small script that creates symbolic links. A new machine then feels familiar in minutes. Keep secrets out of dotfiles. Predict first. You typed a long command and notice a typo near the start. What is the fastest safe edit? Your guess Answer: Use Ctrl+A or Alt+B to move to the typo and fix it, or press Ctrl+X Ctrl+E to open the line in your editor. Editing the line avoids retyping and avoids running a half-typed command. Ctrl+X Ctrl+E opens the command in $EDITOR in bash. Incident: the wrong host Incident An engineer runs rm -rf /var/lib/app/cache to free space, in a terminal that was connected to production instead of staging. $ hostname prod-web-03 Both terminals looked the same. The team set a red background and a clear host name in the prompt for production, added a confirmation wrapper for destructive commands on production hosts, and used tmux window names such as \"STAGING\" and \"PROD\". Common mistakes Long jobs run without tmux or nohup Identical-looking prompts for every environment Editing system files as root in a full editor session Secrets typed on the command line and kept in history"},"linux/time-ntp-and-timezones":{"html":"\u003cp>A login token is rejected as &quot;not yet valid&quot;, a certificate seems to expire a day early, and logs from two servers cannot be lined up. All three problems have the same cause: the servers do not agree on the time. Time looks like a small topic until it breaks authentication, replication and every investigation that relies on timestamps.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The difference between the hardware clock, the system clock and a time zone\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How NTP keeps clocks in step and how to check that it works\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why servers should log and run in UTC\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How clock drift breaks tokens, certificates and logs\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"clocks-and-zones\">Clocks and zones\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Linux keeps a \u003cstrong>system clock\u003c/strong> that counts time since 1970 in UTC. A \u003cstrong>time zone\u003c/strong> only decides how that number is shown to people. The \u003cstrong>hardware clock\u003c/strong> (RTC) keeps time while the machine is off, and is read at boot.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"date\ntimedatectl\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">date\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Tue Jan  6 10:02:11 UTC 2026\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>timedatectl\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">               Local time: Tue 2026-01-06 10:02:11 UTC\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">           Universal time: Tue 2026-01-06 10:02:11 UTC\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">                 Time zone: Etc/UTC (UTC, +0000)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">System clock synchronized: yes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">              NTP service: active\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The two lines to watch are \u003ccode>System clock synchronized\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>NTP service\u003c/code>. Set the zone with \u003ccode>sudo timedatectl set-timezone Etc/UTC\u003c/code>. For servers, UTC is the best choice: no daylight saving changes, and logs from every region sort in one order.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-ntp-keeps-time\">How NTP keeps time\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>Network Time Protocol\u003c/strong> asks several time servers for the time, estimates the network delay, and adjusts the local clock. Small errors are corrected by \u003cem>slewing\u003c/em>, which runs the clock slightly faster or slower. Large errors may cause a \u003cem>step\u003c/em>, a sudden jump, which can upset software that measures durations.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>On most distributions the client is \u003ccode>systemd-timesyncd\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>chrony\u003c/code>. Chrony is the usual choice on servers because it recovers quickly and works well on virtual machines.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"chronyc tracking\nchronyc sources -v\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>chronyc tracking\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Reference ID    : A9FEA97B (169.254.169.123)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Stratum         : 4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">System time     : 0.000012345 seconds fast of NTP time\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Last offset     : +0.000008123 seconds\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>chronyc sources -v\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The \u003ccode>System time\u003c/code> line is the current error. Milliseconds are normal; seconds are a problem. In a cloud, prefer the provider&#39;s own time source (for example the link-local address shown above), which is close and reliable.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-breaks-when-time-is-wrong\">What breaks when time is wrong\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Many systems trust the clock more than you might expect:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Feature\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>What goes wrong with a wrong clock\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>TLS certificates\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A certificate looks &quot;not yet valid&quot; or &quot;expired&quot;\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Signed tokens (JWT, cloud API signatures)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Requests rejected, often with a clock skew message\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Kerberos\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Logins fail when skew is more than a few minutes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Databases and clusters\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Replication ordering and leases misbehave\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Logs and metrics\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Events from two hosts appear in the wrong order\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A cloud API that says the signature has expired, when the key is correct, is a classic sign of drift of more than five minutes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"fixing-and-checking-drift\">Fixing and checking drift\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Check first, then fix the cause, not only the symptom.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"timedatectl status\nchronyc tracking\nsudo systemctl enable --now chrony\nsudo chronyc makestep      # step the clock once, now\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">timedatectl status\nchronyc tracking\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> systemctl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">enable\u003c/span> --now chrony\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> chronyc makestep      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># step the clock once, now\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>If a virtual machine drifts after it was paused or migrated, enable the guest time-sync feature or point chrony at the hypervisor or cloud time source. Never set the clock by hand on a production server as a lasting fix. It hides the problem and the drift returns. Add a monitoring alert on the offset (for example, over 100 ms) so that you hear about drift before users do.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A server shows `System clock synchronized: no`. Name two things you check before changing anything.\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Check that the time service is running (systemctl status chrony) and that it can reach a time source (chronyc sources). Then look at firewall rules for UDP port 123.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Synchronization needs a running client and a reachable source. NTP uses UDP port 123, which strict outbound firewalls often block.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-tokens-rejected-after-a-restore\">Incident: Tokens rejected after a restore\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After a virtual machine was restored from a snapshot, every call from it to the cloud API fails with &quot;signature expired&quot;, although the credentials are valid.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"date -u\ncurl -sI https://api.example.com | grep -i ^date\nchronyc tracking | grep &quot;System time&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">date\u003c/span> -u\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Mon Jan  5 08:15:40 UTC 2026\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -sI https://api.example.com | grep -i ^\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">date\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">date: Tue, 06 Jan 2026 10:02:05 GMT\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>chronyc tracking | grep \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;System time&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">System time     : 93815.2 seconds slow of NTP time\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The restored machine woke up with the time of the snapshot, a day behind, and the time client was disabled in the image. Cloud APIs reject signatures that differ from their own clock by more than a few minutes. The fix was \u003ccode>chronyc makestep\u003c/code> and enabling the chrony service, and the team added a start-up check that waits for synchronization before the application starts, plus an alert on clock offset.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Setting the clock by hand and not fixing the time service\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using local time with daylight saving on servers, so logs repeat or skip an hour\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting that a restored or paused virtual machine may start with an old clock\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Blocking UDP port 123 on a firewall and not noticing for months\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"clocks-and-zones","text":"Clocks and zones"},{"id":"how-ntp-keeps-time","text":"How NTP keeps time"},{"id":"what-breaks-when-time-is-wrong","text":"What breaks when time is wrong"},{"id":"fixing-and-checking-drift","text":"Fixing and checking drift"},{"id":"incident-tokens-rejected-after-a-restore","text":"Incident: Tokens rejected after a restore"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"systemd-timesyncd manual","u":"https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd-timesyncd.service.html"},{"t":"timedatectl manual","u":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/timedatectl.1.html"}],"text":"A login token is rejected as \"not yet valid\", a certificate seems to expire a day early, and logs from two servers cannot be lined up. All three problems have the same cause: the servers do not agree on the time. Time looks like a small topic until it breaks authentication, replication and every investigation that relies on timestamps. What you will learn The difference between the hardware clock, the system clock and a time zone How NTP keeps clocks in step and how to check that it works Why servers should log and run in UTC How clock drift breaks tokens, certificates and logs Clocks and zones Linux keeps a system clock that counts time since 1970 in UTC. A time zone only decides how that number is shown to people. The hardware clock (RTC) keeps time while the machine is off, and is read at boot. $ date Tue Jan 6 10:02:11 UTC 2026 $ timedatectl Local time: Tue 2026-01-06 10:02:11 UTC Universal time: Tue 2026-01-06 10:02:11 UTC Time zone: Etc/UTC (UTC, +0000) System clock synchronized: yes NTP service: active The two lines to watch are System clock synchronized and NTP service . Set the zone with sudo timedatectl set-timezone Etc/UTC . For servers, UTC is the best choice: no daylight saving changes, and logs from every region sort in one order. How NTP keeps time The Network Time Protocol asks several time servers for the time, estimates the network delay, and adjusts the local clock. Small errors are corrected by slewing , which runs the clock slightly faster or slower. Large errors may cause a step , a sudden jump, which can upset software that measures durations. On most distributions the client is systemd-timesyncd or chrony . Chrony is the usual choice on servers because it recovers quickly and works well on virtual machines. $ chronyc tracking Reference ID : A9FEA97B (169.254.169.123) Stratum : 4 System time : 0.000012345 seconds fast of NTP time Last offset : +0.000008123 seconds $ chronyc sources -v The System time line is the current error. Milliseconds are normal; seconds are a problem. In a cloud, prefer the provider&#39;s own time source (for example the link-local address shown above), which is close and reliable. What breaks when time is wrong Many systems trust the clock more than you might expect: Feature What goes wrong with a wrong clock TLS certificates A certificate looks \"not yet valid\" or \"expired\" Signed tokens (JWT, cloud API signatures) Requests rejected, often with a clock skew message Kerberos Logins fail when skew is more than a few minutes Databases and clusters Replication ordering and leases misbehave Logs and metrics Events from two hosts appear in the wrong order A cloud API that says the signature has expired, when the key is correct, is a classic sign of drift of more than five minutes. Fixing and checking drift Check first, then fix the cause, not only the symptom. timedatectl status chronyc tracking sudo systemctl enable --now chrony sudo chronyc makestep # step the clock once, now If a virtual machine drifts after it was paused or migrated, enable the guest time-sync feature or point chrony at the hypervisor or cloud time source. Never set the clock by hand on a production server as a lasting fix. It hides the problem and the drift returns. Add a monitoring alert on the offset (for example, over 100 ms) so that you hear about drift before users do. Predict first. A server shows `System clock synchronized: no`. Name two things you check before changing anything. Your guess Answer: Check that the time service is running (systemctl status chrony) and that it can reach a time source (chronyc sources). Then look at firewall rules for UDP port 123. Synchronization needs a running client and a reachable source. NTP uses UDP port 123, which strict outbound firewalls often block. Incident: Tokens rejected after a restore Incident After a virtual machine was restored from a snapshot, every call from it to the cloud API fails with \"signature expired\", although the credentials are valid. $ date -u Mon"},"linux/systemd-overrides-targets-and-dependencies":{"html":"\u003cp>You need one extra environment variable for a packaged service. Editing the file under /usr/lib works until the next package update replaces it, and then the service quietly loses the setting. systemd has a clean answer, the drop-in override, and a few concepts that explain why services start in the order they do.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Edit a unit safely with \u003ccode>systemctl edit\u003c/code> and drop-in files\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Order and dependencies: After, Requires, Wants\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Targets and what happens at boot\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Restart policies and resource limits in a unit\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"where-unit-files-live-and-who-wins\">Where unit files live and who wins\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Packages install units in \u003ccode>/usr/lib/systemd/system/\u003c/code>. Your changes belong in \u003ccode>/etc/systemd/system/\u003c/code>, which has priority and is never touched by package updates.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"systemctl cat shop.service\nsudo systemctl edit shop.service\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>systemctl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> shop.service\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"># /usr/lib/systemd/system/shop.service\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">[Service]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ExecStart=/usr/bin/shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> systemctl edit shop.service\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>systemctl edit\u003c/code> opens an empty file at \u003ccode>/etc/systemd/system/shop.service.d/override.conf\u003c/code>. Put only the lines you want to change there:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ini\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"[Service]\nEnvironment=SHOP_MODE=production\nRestart=on-failure\nRestartSec=5\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">[Service]\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Environment\u003c/span>=SHOP_MODE=production\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Restart\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">on\u003c/span>-failure\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">RestartSec\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>After saving, systemd reloads the configuration for you. To clear a list setting such as \u003ccode>ExecStart\u003c/code>, set it to empty first, then set the new value. \u003ccode>systemctl cat\u003c/code> shows the original and all drop-ins together, which is the first thing to read when a service behaves unexpectedly.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ordering-is-not-the-same-as-dependency\">Ordering is not the same as dependency\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Two ideas are often confused:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Ordering\u003c/strong> (\u003ccode>After=\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Before=\u003c/code>) says which unit starts first if both are being started.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Dependency\u003c/strong> (\u003ccode>Requires=\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Wants=\u003c/code>) says that starting this unit pulls in another.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>Wants=\u003c/code> is a soft dependency: if the other unit fails, this one still starts. \u003ccode>Requires=\u003c/code> is hard: failure of the other stops this unit. A common, correct pair is \u003ccode>Wants=network-online.target\u003c/code> together with \u003ccode>After=network-online.target\u003c/code>, so the service starts after the network is really up.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ini\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"[Unit]\nDescription=Shop API\nWants=network-online.target postgresql.service\nAfter=network-online.target postgresql.service\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">[Unit]\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Description\u003c/span>=Shop API\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Wants\u003c/span>=network-\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">on\u003c/span>line.target postgresql.service\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">After\u003c/span>=network-\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">on\u003c/span>line.target postgresql.service\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>If you write only \u003ccode>After=\u003c/code> the other unit is not started for you, and if you write only \u003ccode>Wants=\u003c/code> the two may start at the same moment. You usually need both.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"targets-and-boot\">Targets and boot\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>target\u003c/strong> is a named group of units, a goal for boot. \u003ccode>multi-user.target\u003c/code> is the usual server state (no graphical session), and \u003ccode>graphical.target\u003c/code> adds a desktop. \u003ccode>systemctl enable\u003c/code> makes a symlink so that a unit is pulled in by a target, usually \u003ccode>multi-user.target\u003c/code>, through the \u003ccode>WantedBy=\u003c/code> line in \u003ccode>[Install]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"systemctl get-default\nsystemctl list-dependencies multi-user.target | head\nsystemd-analyze blame | head -5\nsystemd-analyze critical-chain\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>systemctl get-default\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">multi-user.target\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>systemctl list-dependencies multi-user.target | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>systemd-analyze blame | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -5\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>systemd-analyze critical-chain\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>systemd-analyze blame\u003c/code> shows which units took the most time to start, and \u003ccode>critical-chain\u003c/code> shows the chain that decided the total boot time. Use them when a server boots slowly.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"restarts-and-limits\">Restarts and limits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A unit can describe how it should behave when things go wrong, so that you do not need a separate watchdog script.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ini\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"[Service]\nRestart=on-failure\nRestartSec=5\nStartLimitIntervalSec=300\nStartLimitBurst=5\nMemoryMax=1G\nCPUQuota=150%\nLimitNOFILE=65536\nProtectSystem=strict\nNoNewPrivileges=true\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">[Service]\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Restart\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">on\u003c/span>-failure\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">RestartSec\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">StartLimitIntervalSec\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">300\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">StartLimitBurst\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">MemoryMax\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>G\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">CPUQuota\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">150\u003c/span>%\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">LimitNOFILE\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">65536\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ProtectSystem\u003c/span>=strict\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">NoNewPrivileges\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>StartLimit*\u003c/code> stops a crash loop after five tries in five minutes, and the unit then shows \u003ccode>failed\u003c/code> instead of hiding the problem. \u003ccode>MemoryMax\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>CPUQuota\u003c/code> use cgroups, so one service cannot starve the others. The \u003ccode>Protect*\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>NoNewPrivileges\u003c/code> lines reduce what a compromised service can do, and \u003ccode>systemd-analyze security shop.service\u003c/code> scores them.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You change a drop-in file by hand with a text editor and the service ignores it. What did you forget?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Run `sudo systemctl daemon-reload` so systemd re-reads the files, then restart the service.\u003c/p>\u003cp>systemctl edit reloads for you, but a manual edit does not. Check the result with systemctl cat and systemctl show.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-setting-that-vanished\">Incident: The setting that vanished\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After a routine package update, the shop service started failing at 03:00 because a database password variable was missing.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"systemctl cat shop.service | head -4\nls /etc/systemd/system/shop.service.d/\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>systemctl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> shop.service | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"># /usr/lib/systemd/system/shop.service\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">[Service]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ExecStart=/usr/bin/shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> /etc/systemd/system/shop.service.d/\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ls: cannot access ...: No such file or directory\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Someone had added \u003ccode>Environment=\u003c/code> lines straight into the packaged file under /usr/lib. The package update replaced the file, and the variable was gone. The fix was a drop-in override in /etc, plus an \u003ccode>EnvironmentFile=\u003c/code> with strict permissions for the secret. The team also added a check to the weekly review that lists services with local changes by running systemd-delta.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Editing files in /usr/lib/systemd/system instead of using an override\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writing After= without Wants=, so the dependency never starts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting daemon-reload after a manual edit\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using Restart=always with no start limit, which hides crash loops\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"where-unit-files-live-and-who-wins","text":"Where unit files live and who wins"},{"id":"ordering-is-not-the-same-as-dependency","text":"Ordering is not the same as dependency"},{"id":"targets-and-boot","text":"Targets and boot"},{"id":"restarts-and-limits","text":"Restarts and limits"},{"id":"incident-the-setting-that-vanished","text":"Incident: The setting that vanished"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"systemd.unit manual","u":"https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.unit.html"},{"t":"systemd.service manual","u":"https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.service.html"}],"text":"You need one extra environment variable for a packaged service. Editing the file under /usr/lib works until the next package update replaces it, and then the service quietly loses the setting. systemd has a clean answer, the drop-in override, and a few concepts that explain why services start in the order they do. What you will learn Edit a unit safely with systemctl edit and drop-in files Order and dependencies: After, Requires, Wants Targets and what happens at boot Restart policies and resource limits in a unit Where unit files live and who wins Packages install units in /usr/lib/systemd/system/ . Your changes belong in /etc/systemd/system/ , which has priority and is never touched by package updates. $ systemctl cat shop.service # /usr/lib/systemd/system/shop.service [Service] ExecStart=/usr/bin/shop $ sudo systemctl edit shop.service systemctl edit opens an empty file at /etc/systemd/system/shop.service.d/override.conf . Put only the lines you want to change there: [Service] Environment =SHOP_MODE=production Restart = on -failure RestartSec = 5 After saving, systemd reloads the configuration for you. To clear a list setting such as ExecStart , set it to empty first, then set the new value. systemctl cat shows the original and all drop-ins together, which is the first thing to read when a service behaves unexpectedly. Ordering is not the same as dependency Two ideas are often confused: Ordering ( After= , Before= ) says which unit starts first if both are being started. Dependency ( Requires= , Wants= ) says that starting this unit pulls in another. Wants= is a soft dependency: if the other unit fails, this one still starts. Requires= is hard: failure of the other stops this unit. A common, correct pair is Wants=network-online.target together with After=network-online.target , so the service starts after the network is really up. [Unit] Description =Shop API Wants =network- on line.target postgresql.service After =network- on line.target postgresql.service If you write only After= the other unit is not started for you, and if you write only Wants= the two may start at the same moment. You usually need both. Targets and boot A target is a named group of units, a goal for boot. multi-user.target is the usual server state (no graphical session), and graphical.target adds a desktop. systemctl enable makes a symlink so that a unit is pulled in by a target, usually multi-user.target , through the WantedBy= line in [Install] . $ systemctl get-default multi-user.target $ systemctl list-dependencies multi-user.target | head $ systemd-analyze blame | head -5 $ systemd-analyze critical-chain systemd-analyze blame shows which units took the most time to start, and critical-chain shows the chain that decided the total boot time. Use them when a server boots slowly. Restarts and limits A unit can describe how it should behave when things go wrong, so that you do not need a separate watchdog script. [Service] Restart = on -failure RestartSec = 5 StartLimitIntervalSec = 300 StartLimitBurst = 5 MemoryMax = 1 G CPUQuota = 150 % LimitNOFILE = 65536 ProtectSystem =strict NoNewPrivileges = true StartLimit* stops a crash loop after five tries in five minutes, and the unit then shows failed instead of hiding the problem. MemoryMax and CPUQuota use cgroups, so one service cannot starve the others. The Protect* and NoNewPrivileges lines reduce what a compromised service can do, and systemd-analyze security shop.service scores them. Predict first. You change a drop-in file by hand with a text editor and the service ignores it. What did you forget? Your guess Answer: Run `sudo systemctl daemon-reload` so systemd re-reads the files, then restart the service. systemctl edit reloads for you, but a manual edit does not. Check the result with systemctl cat and systemctl show. Incident: The setting that vanished Incident After a routine package update, the shop service started failing at 03:00 because a database password variable was missing. $ systemctl "},"linux/kernel-modules-sysctl-and-boot-parameters":{"html":"\u003cp>The kernel is not a single fixed program. Drivers and features can be loaded as modules, behaviour is tuned through thousands of runtime parameters, and some settings can only be given on the boot command line. Engineers who can read and change these safely solve problems that no application setting can touch, such as a connection table that fills up or a driver that misbehaves after an update.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>List, load and block kernel modules\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Read and change runtime parameters with sysctl\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Persist settings and understand the boot command line\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Read kernel messages with dmesg and journalctl -k\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"modules\">Modules\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A module is a piece of kernel code loaded on demand, such as a filesystem, a network driver or the module that implements overlay mounts for containers.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"lsmod | head\nmodinfo overlay\nsudo modprobe overlay\nsudo modprobe -r overlay\nlsmod | grep -E &quot;overlay|br_netfilter&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">lsmod | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\nmodinfo overlay\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> modprobe overlay\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> modprobe -r overlay\nlsmod | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;overlay|br_netfilter&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>lsmod\u003c/code> lists what is loaded, \u003ccode>modinfo\u003c/code> shows the module description and parameters, and \u003ccode>modprobe\u003c/code> loads a module with its dependencies (unlike \u003ccode>insmod\u003c/code>, which loads one file). To load a module at every boot, list it in a file under \u003ccode>/etc/modules-load.d/\u003c/code>. To prevent one from loading, add \u003ccode>blacklist name\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>install name /bin/false\u003c/code> under \u003ccode>/etc/modprobe.d/\u003c/code>. Remember that modules run with full kernel privileges: load only what you trust. With Secure Boot, modules must be signed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"sysctl-runtime-tuning\">sysctl: runtime tuning\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Kernel parameters appear as files under \u003ccode>/proc/sys\u003c/code> and are read and written with \u003ccode>sysctl\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sysctl net.ipv4.ip_forward\nsudo sysctl -w net.ipv4.ip_forward=1\nsysctl -a 2&gt;/dev/null | grep -c .\nsudo sysctl --system\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">sysctl net.ipv4.ip_forward\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> sysctl -w net.ipv4.ip_forward=1\nsysctl -a 2&gt;/dev/null | grep -c .\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> sysctl --system\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>-w\u003c/code> changes the running kernel only, and the change disappears at reboot. To keep it, put the line in a file such as \u003ccode>/etc/sysctl.d/90-shop.conf\u003c/code> and run \u003ccode>sysctl --system\u003c/code>. Parameters you will meet often: \u003ccode>net.ipv4.ip_forward\u003c/code> (routing and container networking), \u003ccode>vm.swappiness\u003c/code> (how readily memory is swapped), \u003ccode>vm.max_map_count\u003c/code> (needed by some search engines), \u003ccode>fs.file-max\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>fs.inotify.max_user_watches\u003c/code> (many files or watchers), \u003ccode>net.core.somaxconn\u003c/code> (listen queue length) and \u003ccode>net.ipv4.tcp_*\u003c/code> settings. Change one at a time, record the old value, and measure before and after.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"boot-parameters\">Boot parameters\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Some settings are read once, at boot, from the kernel command line. Look at the current one:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"cat /proc/cmdline\nBOOT_IMAGE=/vmlinuz-6.8.0 root=UUID=7a1c... ro quiet splash console=ttyS0 transparent_hugepage=madvise\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> /proc/cmdline\nBOOT_IMAGE=/vmlinuz-6.8.0 root=UUID=7a1c... ro quiet splash console=ttyS0 transparent_hugepage=madvise\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Typical parameters are \u003ccode>root=\u003c/code> (the root filesystem), \u003ccode>console=\u003c/code> (where messages go), \u003ccode>quiet\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>nomodeset\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>systemd.unit=rescue.target\u003c/code> (boot into rescue mode), \u003ccode>init=/bin/bash\u003c/code> (emergency shell) and \u003ccode>mem=\u003c/code>. On most distributions you change defaults in \u003ccode>/etc/default/grub\u003c/code> (the \u003ccode>GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX\u003c/code> line) and regenerate the config with \u003ccode>update-grub\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>grub2-mkconfig\u003c/code>. For a one-time test, press \u003ccode>e\u003c/code> in the boot menu and edit the line; nothing is saved. Keep a known good entry available before changing anything.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"kernel-messages\">Kernel messages\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"dmesg -T --level=err,warn | tail\njournalctl -k -b -1 | tail -50      # kernel messages of the previous boot\njournalctl -k -p err -b\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">dmesg -T --level=err,warn | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span>\njournalctl -k -b -1 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -50      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># kernel messages of the previous boot\u003c/span>\njournalctl -k -p err -b\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The kernel keeps a ring buffer of messages: hardware detection, driver errors, filesystem problems, out-of-memory kills and network warnings such as \u003ccode>nf_conntrack: table full, dropping packet\u003c/code>. Read it first when behaviour looks impossible from user space. \u003ccode>-T\u003c/code> prints readable times; the previous boot&#39;s messages require persistent journals. Messages from before a crash are often the only evidence, so make sure \u003ccode>/var/log/journal\u003c/code> exists on servers.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You run sysctl -w to raise net.core.somaxconn and the problem is solved. Next week, after a reboot, it returns. Why?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The change applied only to the running kernel. It must be saved in /etc/sysctl.d/ and loaded with sysctl --system.\u003c/p>\u003cp>sysctl -w is temporary.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-packets-dropped-nothing-in-the-application-logs\">Incident: Packets dropped, nothing in the application logs\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A gateway dropped new connections during peak hours while CPU was low.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"dmesg -T | tail -3\nsysctl net.netfilter.nf_conntrack_max net.netfilter.nf_conntrack_count\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>dmesg -T | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">[Tue Jan  6 18:03:11 2026] nf_conntrack: nf_conntrack: table full, dropping packet\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">[Tue Jan  6 18:03:12 2026] nf_conntrack: nf_conntrack: table full, dropping packet\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>sysctl net.netfilter.nf_conntrack_max net.netfilter.nf_conntrack_count\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">net.netfilter.nf_conntrack_max = 65536\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">net.netfilter.nf_conntrack_count = 65531\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The connection tracking table was full, so the kernel dropped new connections before the application saw them. The team raised nf_conntrack_max in a file under /etc/sysctl.d/ after checking memory use, shortened some timeouts, and added an alert on the count to max ratio. The lesson: some limits exist only in the kernel, and dmesg is where they speak.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using sysctl -w and forgetting to persist it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Changing many parameters at once\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Editing the boot command line without a fallback entry\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Loading unsigned or unknown modules on production servers\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"modules","text":"Modules"},{"id":"sysctl-runtime-tuning","text":"sysctl: runtime tuning"},{"id":"boot-parameters","text":"Boot parameters"},{"id":"kernel-messages","text":"Kernel messages"},{"id":"incident-packets-dropped-nothing-in-the-application-logs","text":"Incident: Packets dropped, nothing in the application logs"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kernel parameters","u":"https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/kernel-parameters.html"},{"t":"sysctl(8) man page","u":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/sysctl.8.html"}],"text":"The kernel is not a single fixed program. Drivers and features can be loaded as modules, behaviour is tuned through thousands of runtime parameters, and some settings can only be given on the boot command line. Engineers who can read and change these safely solve problems that no application setting can touch, such as a connection table that fills up or a driver that misbehaves after an update. What you will learn List, load and block kernel modules Read and change runtime parameters with sysctl Persist settings and understand the boot command line Read kernel messages with dmesg and journalctl -k Modules A module is a piece of kernel code loaded on demand, such as a filesystem, a network driver or the module that implements overlay mounts for containers. lsmod | head modinfo overlay sudo modprobe overlay sudo modprobe -r overlay lsmod | grep -E \"overlay|br_netfilter\" lsmod lists what is loaded, modinfo shows the module description and parameters, and modprobe loads a module with its dependencies (unlike insmod , which loads one file). To load a module at every boot, list it in a file under /etc/modules-load.d/ . To prevent one from loading, add blacklist name or install name /bin/false under /etc/modprobe.d/ . Remember that modules run with full kernel privileges: load only what you trust. With Secure Boot, modules must be signed. sysctl: runtime tuning Kernel parameters appear as files under /proc/sys and are read and written with sysctl . sysctl net.ipv4.ip_forward sudo sysctl -w net.ipv4.ip_forward=1 sysctl -a 2>/dev/null | grep -c . sudo sysctl --system -w changes the running kernel only, and the change disappears at reboot. To keep it, put the line in a file such as /etc/sysctl.d/90-shop.conf and run sysctl --system . Parameters you will meet often: net.ipv4.ip_forward (routing and container networking), vm.swappiness (how readily memory is swapped), vm.max_map_count (needed by some search engines), fs.file-max and fs.inotify.max_user_watches (many files or watchers), net.core.somaxconn (listen queue length) and net.ipv4.tcp_* settings. Change one at a time, record the old value, and measure before and after. Boot parameters Some settings are read once, at boot, from the kernel command line. Look at the current one: cat /proc/cmdline BOOT_IMAGE=/vmlinuz-6.8.0 root=UUID=7a1c... ro quiet splash console=ttyS0 transparent_hugepage=madvise Typical parameters are root= (the root filesystem), console= (where messages go), quiet , nomodeset , systemd.unit=rescue.target (boot into rescue mode), init=/bin/bash (emergency shell) and mem= . On most distributions you change defaults in /etc/default/grub (the GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX line) and regenerate the config with update-grub or grub2-mkconfig . For a one-time test, press e in the boot menu and edit the line; nothing is saved. Keep a known good entry available before changing anything. Kernel messages dmesg -T --level=err,warn | tail journalctl -k -b -1 | tail -50 # kernel messages of the previous boot journalctl -k -p err -b The kernel keeps a ring buffer of messages: hardware detection, driver errors, filesystem problems, out-of-memory kills and network warnings such as nf_conntrack: table full, dropping packet . Read it first when behaviour looks impossible from user space. -T prints readable times; the previous boot&#39;s messages require persistent journals. Messages from before a crash are often the only evidence, so make sure /var/log/journal exists on servers. Predict first. You run sysctl -w to raise net.core.somaxconn and the problem is solved. Next week, after a reboot, it returns. Why? Your guess Answer: The change applied only to the running kernel. It must be saved in /etc/sysctl.d/ and loaded with sysctl --system. sysctl -w is temporary. Incident: Packets dropped, nothing in the application logs Incident A gateway dropped new connections during peak hours while CPU was low. $ dmesg -T | tail -3 [Tue Jan 6 18:03:11 2026] nf_conntrack: nf_conntrack: table full, dropping p"},"linux/processes-memory-and-the-oom-killer":{"html":"\u003cp>Memory problems look mysterious because Linux uses memory differently from how people expect. Free memory near zero is normal, since the kernel uses spare memory as cache. A process can be killed even when free shows gigabytes available, if its cgroup limit was hit. Knowing what the numbers mean, and where the kernel records its decisions, turns an out-of-memory incident from guessing into reading.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Virtual memory, resident memory and the page cache\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Read free, /proc/meminfo and per-process numbers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How the OOM killer chooses a victim and where it logs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Container memory limits and how to prevent kills\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-the-numbers-mean\">What the numbers mean\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"free -h\n              total   used   free  shared  buff/cache  available\nMem:           15Gi   4.1Gi  0.4Gi  0.2Gi       11Gi       10Gi\nSwap:         2.0Gi   0.0Gi  2.0Gi\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">free -h\n              total   used   free  shared  buff/cache  available\nMem:           15Gi   4.1Gi  0.4Gi  0.2Gi       11Gi       10Gi\nSwap:         2.0Gi   0.0Gi  2.0Gi\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The \u003ccode>free\u003c/code> column is memory that nothing uses; \u003ccode>buff/cache\u003c/code> is file data kept in memory to speed up reads, which the kernel gives back when programs need it. The column that matters is \u003cstrong>available\u003c/strong>: an estimate of how much can be given to programs without swapping. Each process has \u003cstrong>virtual\u003c/strong> memory (address space promised to it, often much larger than real use), \u003cstrong>resident\u003c/strong> memory (RSS, pages actually in RAM) and shared pages counted in several processes. Use \u003ccode>ps -eo pid,rss,cmd --sort=-rss | head\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>/proc/PID/smaps_rollup\u003c/code> for accurate per-process values.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"swap-and-pressure\">Swap and pressure\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Swap is disk space the kernel can use to move rarely used pages out of RAM. A little swap use is fine. A system that constantly reads and writes swap is \u003cstrong>thrashing\u003c/strong>: it is slow, with high I/O wait and low CPU use.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"vmstat 1 5            # watch si/so (swap in/out) and wa (I/O wait)\ncat /proc/pressure/memory\nsysctl vm.swappiness\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">vmstat 1 5            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># watch si/so (swap in/out) and wa (I/O wait)\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> /proc/pressure/memory\nsysctl vm.swappiness\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The pressure stall information (PSI) file shows how much time tasks spend waiting for memory: values like \u003ccode>some avg10=12.5\u003c/code> mean tasks were stalled 12.5 percent of the last ten seconds. PSI is a better alarm than free memory. Many container hosts run with no swap on purpose, so that overload shows up as a clear kill rather than as a slow death.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-oom-killer\">The OOM killer\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When the kernel cannot find memory, it picks a process to kill so that the system can survive. It prefers processes that use a lot of memory, adjusted by \u003ccode>oom_score_adj\u003c/code> (range -1000 to 1000; -1000 makes a process unkillable, so use it carefully).\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"dmesg -T | grep -i -E &quot;out of memory|killed process&quot;\njournalctl -k | grep -i oom\ncat /proc/$(pidof myapp)/oom_score\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">dmesg -T | grep -i -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;out of memory|killed process&quot;\u003c/span>\njournalctl -k | grep -i oom\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> /proc/$(pidof myapp)/oom_score\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A typical message reads: \u003ccode>Out of memory: Killed process 4121 (java) total-vm:9876540kB, anon-rss:7340000kB\u003c/code>. The log lists the victim, its sizes and a table of all processes. Check whether the kill came from the whole machine running out, or from a \u003cstrong>cgroup\u003c/strong> limit (the message mentions \u003ccode>Memory cgroup out of memory\u003c/code>).\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"containers-and-prevention\">Containers and prevention\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>In containers and Kubernetes pods, the memory limit becomes a cgroup limit, and exceeding it gets the container killed (status \u003ccode>OOMKilled\u003c/code>, exit code 137) even when the host has plenty. Prevention is design work:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Set requests and limits from measured usage, with headroom.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Make runtimes aware of the limit (for example, set the heap as a percentage of the container memory).\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Fix leaks and unbounded caches or queues.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Alert on memory working set and on PSI, not only on free memory.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep important system daemons protected with a lower \u003ccode>oom_score_adj\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl describe pod web-7d9 | grep -A3 &quot;Last State&quot;\nkubectl top pod web-7d9 --containers\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl describe pod web-7d9 | grep -A3 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Last State&quot;\u003c/span>\nkubectl top pod web-7d9 --containers\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> free shows 300 MB free but 10 GB available. Is the machine in trouble?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> No. Most memory is page cache that the kernel can reclaim. Look at available and at memory pressure, not at the free column.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Free is not the right number.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-pod-killed-with-plenty-of-host-memory\">Incident: The pod killed with plenty of host memory\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A Java service restarted every few hours with exit code 137.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl describe pod orders-6f7 | grep -A4 &quot;Last State&quot;\nkubectl get pod orders-6f7 -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[0].resources.limits.memory}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe pod orders-6f7 | grep -A4 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Last State&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    Last State:   Terminated\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">      Reason:     OOMKilled\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">      Exit Code:  137\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pod orders-6f7 -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.spec.containers[0].resources.limits.memory}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">512Mi\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The JVM was configured with a fixed 1 GB heap while the container limit was 512 MiB, so the kernel killed it inside its cgroup when the heap grew. The fix set the heap as a percentage of the container memory, raised the limit based on measurements, and added an alert on working set above 85 percent of the limit.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reading the free column as the amount of memory left\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Disabling swap or the OOM killer without a plan\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Setting container limits below the real working set\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Giving a process oom_score_adj of -1000 without thought\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-the-numbers-mean","text":"What the numbers mean"},{"id":"swap-and-pressure","text":"Swap and pressure"},{"id":"the-oom-killer","text":"The OOM killer"},{"id":"containers-and-prevention","text":"Containers and prevention"},{"id":"incident-the-pod-killed-with-plenty-of-host-memory","text":"Incident: The pod killed with plenty of host memory"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Linux memory management documentation","u":"https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/mm/index.html"},{"t":"Pressure Stall Information","u":"https://docs.kernel.org/accounting/psi.html"}],"text":"Memory problems look mysterious because Linux uses memory differently from how people expect. Free memory near zero is normal, since the kernel uses spare memory as cache. A process can be killed even when free shows gigabytes available, if its cgroup limit was hit. Knowing what the numbers mean, and where the kernel records its decisions, turns an out-of-memory incident from guessing into reading. What you will learn Virtual memory, resident memory and the page cache Read free, /proc/meminfo and per-process numbers How the OOM killer chooses a victim and where it logs Container memory limits and how to prevent kills What the numbers mean free -h total used free shared buff/cache available Mem: 15Gi 4.1Gi 0.4Gi 0.2Gi 11Gi 10Gi Swap: 2.0Gi 0.0Gi 2.0Gi The free column is memory that nothing uses; buff/cache is file data kept in memory to speed up reads, which the kernel gives back when programs need it. The column that matters is available : an estimate of how much can be given to programs without swapping. Each process has virtual memory (address space promised to it, often much larger than real use), resident memory (RSS, pages actually in RAM) and shared pages counted in several processes. Use ps -eo pid,rss,cmd --sort=-rss | head and /proc/PID/smaps_rollup for accurate per-process values. Swap and pressure Swap is disk space the kernel can use to move rarely used pages out of RAM. A little swap use is fine. A system that constantly reads and writes swap is thrashing : it is slow, with high I/O wait and low CPU use. vmstat 1 5 # watch si/so (swap in/out) and wa (I/O wait) cat /proc/pressure/memory sysctl vm.swappiness The pressure stall information (PSI) file shows how much time tasks spend waiting for memory: values like some avg10=12.5 mean tasks were stalled 12.5 percent of the last ten seconds. PSI is a better alarm than free memory. Many container hosts run with no swap on purpose, so that overload shows up as a clear kill rather than as a slow death. The OOM killer When the kernel cannot find memory, it picks a process to kill so that the system can survive. It prefers processes that use a lot of memory, adjusted by oom_score_adj (range -1000 to 1000; -1000 makes a process unkillable, so use it carefully). dmesg -T | grep -i -E \"out of memory|killed process\" journalctl -k | grep -i oom cat /proc/$(pidof myapp)/oom_score A typical message reads: Out of memory: Killed process 4121 (java) total-vm:9876540kB, anon-rss:7340000kB . The log lists the victim, its sizes and a table of all processes. Check whether the kill came from the whole machine running out, or from a cgroup limit (the message mentions Memory cgroup out of memory ). Containers and prevention In containers and Kubernetes pods, the memory limit becomes a cgroup limit, and exceeding it gets the container killed (status OOMKilled , exit code 137) even when the host has plenty. Prevention is design work: Set requests and limits from measured usage, with headroom. Make runtimes aware of the limit (for example, set the heap as a percentage of the container memory). Fix leaks and unbounded caches or queues. Alert on memory working set and on PSI, not only on free memory. Keep important system daemons protected with a lower oom_score_adj . kubectl describe pod web-7d9 | grep -A3 \"Last State\" kubectl top pod web-7d9 --containers Predict first. free shows 300 MB free but 10 GB available. Is the machine in trouble? Your guess Answer: No. Most memory is page cache that the kernel can reclaim. Look at available and at memory pressure, not at the free column. Free is not the right number. Incident: The pod killed with plenty of host memory Incident A Java service restarted every few hours with exit code 137. $ kubectl describe pod orders-6f7 | grep -A4 \"Last State\" Last State: Terminated Reason: OOMKilled Exit Code: 137 $ kubectl get pod orders-6f7 -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.spec.containers[0].resources.limits.memory}&#x27; 512Mi The JVM was configured with a fixed 1 GB heap whi"},"linux/system-calls-strace-and-perf":{"html":"\u003cp>Every program that touches a file, a socket or the clock must ask the kernel through a system call. When an application hangs, fails with a vague message or burns CPU for no clear reason, watching its system calls shows what it is really doing, regardless of the language it is written in. Two tools do most of the work: strace for the conversation with the kernel and perf for where the time goes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What a system call is and what user and kernel space mean\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Trace a process with strace and read its output\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Find permission, missing file and DNS problems quickly\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Profile CPU use with perf and know its limits\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"user-space-and-kernel-space\">User space and kernel space\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Programs run in \u003cstrong>user space\u003c/strong> with limited rights. To read a file, open a connection or create a process they call into the kernel, which runs in \u003cstrong>kernel space\u003c/strong> with full access: \u003ccode>open\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>read\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>write\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>connect\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>accept\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>clone\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>execve\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>mmap\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>futex\u003c/code> and hundreds more. Libraries such as the C library wrap them. Switching between modes costs time, so a program making millions of tiny calls is slow, and a program stuck in one call (waiting for a read that never returns) appears hung. A list is in \u003ccode>man 2 syscalls\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"using-strace\">Using strace\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"strace -f -tt -T -o /tmp/trace.txt ./myapp --config /etc/myapp.conf\nstrace -p 4121 -f -e trace=network\nstrace -c -p 4121                   # summary table by call, then Ctrl-C\nstrace -e trace=openat,stat ls /nonexistent 2&gt;&amp;1 | tail -3\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">strace -f -tt -T -o /tmp/trace.txt ./myapp --config /etc/myapp.conf\nstrace -p 4121 -f -e trace=network\nstrace -c -p 4121                   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># summary table by call, then Ctrl-C\u003c/span>\nstrace -e trace=openat,\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">stat\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> /nonexistent 2&gt;&amp;1 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -3\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>-f\u003c/code> follows child processes and threads, \u003ccode>-tt\u003c/code> adds timestamps, \u003ccode>-T\u003c/code> shows the time spent inside each call, \u003ccode>-e trace=\u003c/code> filters by call or group, and \u003ccode>-c\u003c/code> prints a summary of counts and time. A line such as \u003ccode>openat(AT_FDCWD, &quot;/etc/myapp.conf&quot;, O_RDONLY) = -1 ENOENT (No such file or directory)\u003c/code> tells you exactly which file was missing. The return value after \u003ccode>=\u003c/code> and the error name are the key.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"typical-findings\">Typical findings\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Wrong file or permissions\u003c/strong>: \u003ccode>ENOENT\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>EACCES\u003c/code> on \u003ccode>openat\u003c/code>; shows the exact path the program really tried.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Hang\u003c/strong>: the last line is a call that has not returned, such as \u003ccode>read(7,\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>futex(0x7f...,\u003c/code> (waiting on a lock) or \u003ccode>connect(\u003c/code> (waiting on a network peer).\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>DNS or config\u003c/strong>: reads of \u003ccode>/etc/resolv.conf\u003c/code> and a \u003ccode>sendto\u003c/code> to port 53 followed by a long wait.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Too many calls\u003c/strong>: a flood of \u003ccode>stat\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>read\u003c/code> of 1 byte shows a bad loop; \u003ccode>strace -c\u003c/code> makes this visible.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>strace slows the target a lot, because it stops it at every call. Use it on a test copy or briefly in production, and consider \u003ccode>perf trace\u003c/code> or eBPF-based tools for low-overhead tracing.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"perf-for-cpu-time\">perf for CPU time\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo perf top\nsudo perf record -F 99 -g -p 4121 -- sleep 30\nsudo perf report --stdio | head -40\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> perf top\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> perf record -F 99 -g -p 4121 -- \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sleep\u003c/span> 30\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> perf report --stdio | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -40\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>perf samples what the CPU is doing 99 times per second and shows which functions use the most time, including kernel code. If a process uses 100 percent CPU and strace shows no calls, it is computing in user space, and perf shows where. Without debug symbols you see addresses instead of names. Flame graphs built from perf data are a common way to present the result. Run profiling during the problem, for a short period, and save the data with the incident notes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A service hangs. strace -p shows the last line is futex(0x7f12..., FUTEX_WAIT, ...) and it never returns. What does that suggest?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The process is waiting on a lock or condition held by another thread, likely a deadlock or a blocked dependency; check other threads and what holds the lock.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Look at all threads with strace -f or a thread dump.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-config-file-nobody-could-find\">Incident: The config file nobody could find\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An application started with default settings, ignoring the settings in its configuration file.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"strace -f -e trace=openat ./myapp 2&gt;&amp;1 | grep -i conf\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>strace -f -e trace=openat ./myapp 2&gt;&amp;1 | grep -i conf\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">openat(AT_FDCWD, &quot;/etc/myapp/config.yaml&quot;, O_RDONLY) = -1 ENOENT (No such file or directory)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">openat(AT_FDCWD, &quot;/home/app/.myapp.yaml&quot;, O_RDONLY) = -1 ENOENT (No such file or directory)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The application looked for /etc/myapp/config.yaml, while the team had placed the file at /etc/myapp.yaml. A path mismatch after a packaging change was the cause. Tracing showed the exact paths in a minute, which saved reading the source code. The packaging was fixed and a startup check was added that fails loudly when no configuration is found.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Running strace on a busy production process for a long time\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting -f and tracing only the parent\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring return values and error names\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Profiling without symbols or for too short a time\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"user-space-and-kernel-space","text":"User space and kernel space"},{"id":"using-strace","text":"Using strace"},{"id":"typical-findings","text":"Typical findings"},{"id":"perf-for-cpu-time","text":"perf for CPU time"},{"id":"incident-the-config-file-nobody-could-find","text":"Incident: The config file nobody could find"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"strace(1) man page","u":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/strace.1.html"},{"t":"perf(1) man page","u":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/perf.1.html"}],"text":"Every program that touches a file, a socket or the clock must ask the kernel through a system call. When an application hangs, fails with a vague message or burns CPU for no clear reason, watching its system calls shows what it is really doing, regardless of the language it is written in. Two tools do most of the work: strace for the conversation with the kernel and perf for where the time goes. What you will learn What a system call is and what user and kernel space mean Trace a process with strace and read its output Find permission, missing file and DNS problems quickly Profile CPU use with perf and know its limits User space and kernel space Programs run in user space with limited rights. To read a file, open a connection or create a process they call into the kernel, which runs in kernel space with full access: open , read , write , connect , accept , clone , execve , mmap , futex and hundreds more. Libraries such as the C library wrap them. Switching between modes costs time, so a program making millions of tiny calls is slow, and a program stuck in one call (waiting for a read that never returns) appears hung. A list is in man 2 syscalls . Using strace strace -f -tt -T -o /tmp/trace.txt ./myapp --config /etc/myapp.conf strace -p 4121 -f -e trace=network strace -c -p 4121 # summary table by call, then Ctrl-C strace -e trace=openat, stat ls /nonexistent 2>&1 | tail -3 -f follows child processes and threads, -tt adds timestamps, -T shows the time spent inside each call, -e trace= filters by call or group, and -c prints a summary of counts and time. A line such as openat(AT_FDCWD, \"/etc/myapp.conf\", O_RDONLY) = -1 ENOENT (No such file or directory) tells you exactly which file was missing. The return value after = and the error name are the key. Typical findings Wrong file or permissions : ENOENT or EACCES on openat ; shows the exact path the program really tried. Hang : the last line is a call that has not returned, such as read(7, or futex(0x7f..., (waiting on a lock) or connect( (waiting on a network peer). DNS or config : reads of /etc/resolv.conf and a sendto to port 53 followed by a long wait. Too many calls : a flood of stat or read of 1 byte shows a bad loop; strace -c makes this visible. strace slows the target a lot, because it stops it at every call. Use it on a test copy or briefly in production, and consider perf trace or eBPF-based tools for low-overhead tracing. perf for CPU time sudo perf top sudo perf record -F 99 -g -p 4121 -- sleep 30 sudo perf report --stdio | head -40 perf samples what the CPU is doing 99 times per second and shows which functions use the most time, including kernel code. If a process uses 100 percent CPU and strace shows no calls, it is computing in user space, and perf shows where. Without debug symbols you see addresses instead of names. Flame graphs built from perf data are a common way to present the result. Run profiling during the problem, for a short period, and save the data with the incident notes. Predict first. A service hangs. strace -p shows the last line is futex(0x7f12..., FUTEX_WAIT, ...) and it never returns. What does that suggest? Your guess Answer: The process is waiting on a lock or condition held by another thread, likely a deadlock or a blocked dependency; check other threads and what holds the lock. Look at all threads with strace -f or a thread dump. Incident: The config file nobody could find Incident An application started with default settings, ignoring the settings in its configuration file. $ strace -f -e trace=openat ./myapp 2>&1 | grep -i conf openat(AT_FDCWD, \"/etc/myapp/config.yaml\", O_RDONLY) = -1 ENOENT (No such file or directory) openat(AT_FDCWD, \"/home/app/.myapp.yaml\", O_RDONLY) = -1 ENOENT (No such file or directory) The application looked for /etc/myapp/config.yaml, while the team had placed the file at /etc/myapp.yaml. A path mismatch after a packaging change was the cause. Tracing showed the exact paths in a minute, which saved reading the sou"},"linux/filesystems-page-cache-and-io":{"html":"\u003cp>Disk behaviour confuses many engineers because the kernel sits in between. A write that returns immediately has not necessarily reached the disk. A read may never touch the disk at all. Understanding the virtual file system, the page cache and writeback explains why databases insist on fsync, why a copy seems instant then stalls, and how to tell a slow disk from a slow application.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>VFS and what happens on read and write\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Dirty pages, writeback and fsync\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Read iostat and find the saturated device\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Common filesystem limits: inodes, descriptors, mount options\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-path-of-a-read-and-a-write\">The path of a read and a write\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Programs call \u003ccode>read\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>write\u003c/code> on a file descriptor. The \u003cstrong>VFS\u003c/strong> (virtual file system) layer gives all filesystems (ext4, XFS, overlayfs, NFS) the same interface. Reads are served from the \u003cstrong>page cache\u003c/strong> if the data is there; otherwise the kernel reads blocks from the device and caches them. Writes go into the cache as \u003cstrong>dirty pages\u003c/strong> and return at once; the kernel writes them to the device later (\u003cstrong>writeback\u003c/strong>). That is fast, but data in dirty pages is lost if power fails before writeback.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"fsync-and-durability\">fsync and durability\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sync                                   # flush all dirty data now\ngrep -E &quot;Dirty|Writeback&quot; /proc/meminfo\nsysctl vm.dirty_ratio vm.dirty_background_ratio\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sync\u003c/span>                                   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># flush all dirty data now\u003c/span>\ngrep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Dirty|Writeback&quot;\u003c/span> /proc/meminfo\nsysctl vm.dirty_ratio vm.dirty_background_ratio\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Applications that must not lose data call \u003ccode>fsync\u003c/code> (or open files with \u003ccode>O_SYNC\u003c/code>) so the kernel writes the file to stable storage before returning. Databases and message queues do this for their logs, which is why their speed depends on disk latency. If too much dirty data builds up, the kernel makes writers wait (\u003ccode>vm.dirty_ratio\u003c/code>), and you see long pauses during large copies. Filesystems also keep a journal so that metadata is consistent after a crash; a journal is not a backup.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"finding-a-slow-disk\">Finding a slow disk\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"iostat -xz 1 3\nDevice  r/s   w/s  rkB/s  wkB/s  await  aqu-sz  %util\nnvme0n1 120  3400  4800  98000   1.2    4.1    62\nsda      15    80   400   2200  85.0    7.5    99\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">iostat -xz 1 3\nDevice  r/s   w/s  rkB/s  wkB/s  await  aqu-sz  %util\nnvme0n1 120  3400  4800  98000   1.2    4.1    62\nsda      15    80   400   2200  85.0    7.5    99\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Columns to watch: \u003ccode>await\u003c/code> (average time per request including queueing, in ms), \u003ccode>aqu-sz\u003c/code> (queue length) and \u003ccode>%util\u003c/code> (how busy the device is; on SSDs and arrays near 100 percent does not always mean saturation, but high await does). In the example, \u003ccode>sda\u003c/code> has an average wait of 85 ms and is nearly fully busy: it is the bottleneck. Find the process responsible with \u003ccode>iotop -oa\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>pidstat -d 1\u003c/code>, and check high \u003ccode>wa\u003c/code> (I/O wait) in \u003ccode>vmstat\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>top\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"limits-that-surprise-people\">Limits that surprise people\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Inodes\u003c/strong>: \u003ccode>df -i\u003c/code> shows inode use; millions of tiny files can exhaust inodes while \u003ccode>df -h\u003c/code> shows free space.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Open files\u003c/strong>: \u003ccode>ulimit -n\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>/proc/PID/limits\u003c/code> limit descriptors per process; \u003ccode>Too many open files\u003c/code> errors point here.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Mount options\u003c/strong>: \u003ccode>noatime\u003c/code> reduces metadata writes; \u003ccode>ro\u003c/code> after errors means the filesystem was remounted read-only, so check \u003ccode>dmesg\u003c/code> for I/O errors.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Full volumes\u003c/strong>: logs and container layers fill disks; \u003ccode>du -xh --max-depth=1 /var | sort -h\u003c/code> finds the biggest directories.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Network filesystems\u003c/strong>: a stuck NFS server leaves processes in uninterruptible sleep (state \u003ccode>D\u003c/code>) that cannot be killed.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A process is in state D and cannot be killed. What is the likely situation?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> It is waiting in the kernel for I/O, often on a failing disk or an unreachable network filesystem. Fix the underlying device or mount; killing signals cannot interrupt it.\u003c/p>\u003cp>D means uninterruptible sleep.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-log-disk-that-went-read-only\">Incident: The log disk that went read-only\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An application started failing with errors writing to its log directory, although df showed free space.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"touch /var/log/app/test\ndmesg -T | tail -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">touch\u003c/span> /var/log/app/test\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">touch: cannot touch '/var/log/app/test': Read-only file system\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>dmesg -T | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">[Wed Jan  7 03:11:09 2026] EXT4-fs error (device sdb1): ext4_find_entry: reading directory lblock 0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">[Wed Jan  7 03:11:09 2026] EXT4-fs (sdb1): Remounting filesystem read-only\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The kernel remounted the filesystem read-only after I/O errors from the failing disk, to protect data. The team moved the service to a healthy volume, replaced the disk, ran fsck on the unmounted filesystem, and added an alert on disk errors in the kernel log.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Assuming a successful write is on disk\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using %util alone to judge SSDs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring inode exhaustion\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Trying to kill processes stuck on a dead network mount\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-path-of-a-read-and-a-write","text":"The path of a read and a write"},{"id":"fsync-and-durability","text":"fsync and durability"},{"id":"finding-a-slow-disk","text":"Finding a slow disk"},{"id":"limits-that-surprise-people","text":"Limits that surprise people"},{"id":"incident-the-log-disk-that-went-read-only","text":"Incident: The log disk that went read-only"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Linux block layer and I/O documentation","u":"https://docs.kernel.org/block/index.html"},{"t":"iostat(1) man page","u":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/iostat.1.html"}],"text":"Disk behaviour confuses many engineers because the kernel sits in between. A write that returns immediately has not necessarily reached the disk. A read may never touch the disk at all. Understanding the virtual file system, the page cache and writeback explains why databases insist on fsync, why a copy seems instant then stalls, and how to tell a slow disk from a slow application. What you will learn VFS and what happens on read and write Dirty pages, writeback and fsync Read iostat and find the saturated device Common filesystem limits: inodes, descriptors, mount options The path of a read and a write Programs call read and write on a file descriptor. The VFS (virtual file system) layer gives all filesystems (ext4, XFS, overlayfs, NFS) the same interface. Reads are served from the page cache if the data is there; otherwise the kernel reads blocks from the device and caches them. Writes go into the cache as dirty pages and return at once; the kernel writes them to the device later ( writeback ). That is fast, but data in dirty pages is lost if power fails before writeback. fsync and durability sync # flush all dirty data now grep -E \"Dirty|Writeback\" /proc/meminfo sysctl vm.dirty_ratio vm.dirty_background_ratio Applications that must not lose data call fsync (or open files with O_SYNC ) so the kernel writes the file to stable storage before returning. Databases and message queues do this for their logs, which is why their speed depends on disk latency. If too much dirty data builds up, the kernel makes writers wait ( vm.dirty_ratio ), and you see long pauses during large copies. Filesystems also keep a journal so that metadata is consistent after a crash; a journal is not a backup. Finding a slow disk iostat -xz 1 3 Device r/s w/s rkB/s wkB/s await aqu-sz %util nvme0n1 120 3400 4800 98000 1.2 4.1 62 sda 15 80 400 2200 85.0 7.5 99 Columns to watch: await (average time per request including queueing, in ms), aqu-sz (queue length) and %util (how busy the device is; on SSDs and arrays near 100 percent does not always mean saturation, but high await does). In the example, sda has an average wait of 85 ms and is nearly fully busy: it is the bottleneck. Find the process responsible with iotop -oa or pidstat -d 1 , and check high wa (I/O wait) in vmstat or top . Limits that surprise people Inodes : df -i shows inode use; millions of tiny files can exhaust inodes while df -h shows free space. Open files : ulimit -n and /proc/PID/limits limit descriptors per process; Too many open files errors point here. Mount options : noatime reduces metadata writes; ro after errors means the filesystem was remounted read-only, so check dmesg for I/O errors. Full volumes : logs and container layers fill disks; du -xh --max-depth=1 /var | sort -h finds the biggest directories. Network filesystems : a stuck NFS server leaves processes in uninterruptible sleep (state D ) that cannot be killed. Predict first. A process is in state D and cannot be killed. What is the likely situation? Your guess Answer: It is waiting in the kernel for I/O, often on a failing disk or an unreachable network filesystem. Fix the underlying device or mount; killing signals cannot interrupt it. D means uninterruptible sleep. Incident: The log disk that went read-only Incident An application started failing with errors writing to its log directory, although df showed free space. $ touch /var/log/app/test touch: cannot touch '/var/log/app/test': Read-only file system $ dmesg -T | tail -3 [Wed Jan 7 03:11:09 2026] EXT4-fs error (device sdb1): ext4_find_entry: reading directory lblock 0 [Wed Jan 7 03:11:09 2026] EXT4-fs (sdb1): Remounting filesystem read-only The kernel remounted the filesystem read-only after I/O errors from the failing disk, to protect data. The team moved the service to a healthy volume, replaced the disk, ran fsck on the unmounted filesystem, and added an alert on disk errors in the kernel log. Common mistakes Assuming a successful write is on disk Using %util"},"linux/networking-in-the-kernel-sockets-and-conntrack":{"html":"\u003cp>When a service drops connections, applications often see only timeouts. The truth is in the kernel: full accept queues, sockets stuck in TIME_WAIT, exhausted ports, or a connection table that is full. A small set of commands shows these structures directly, and a few kernel parameters control them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Sockets, ports and the listen and accept queues\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Read ss output and TCP states\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Port exhaustion, TIME_WAIT and keepalives\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Netfilter and conntrack, and what happens when the table fills\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"sockets-and-queues\">Sockets and queues\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>socket\u003c/strong> is the kernel object behind a connection. A server calls \u003ccode>listen\u003c/code>, and incoming connections wait in a queue until the application calls \u003ccode>accept\u003c/code>. If the application is slow, the queue fills (its size is limited by the backlog argument and \u003ccode>net.core.somaxconn\u003c/code>), and new connections are dropped or delayed, so clients see timeouts although the server appears fine.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ss -ltn\nState   Recv-Q  Send-Q  Local Address:Port\nLISTEN  129     128     0.0.0.0:8080\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ss -ltn\nState   Recv-Q  Send-Q  Local Address:Port\nLISTEN  129     128     0.0.0.0:8080\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>For listening sockets, \u003ccode>Recv-Q\u003c/code> is the current accept queue length and \u003ccode>Send-Q\u003c/code> its maximum. Here 129 is above 128: the queue is overflowing.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tcp-states-with-ss\">TCP states with ss\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ss -s\nss -tan state established | wc -l\nss -tan state time-wait | wc -l\nss -tanp | grep :5432 | head\nss -ti dst 10.0.1.20\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ss -s\nss -tan state established | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\nss -tan state time-wait | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\nss -tanp | grep :5432 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\nss -ti dst 10.0.1.20\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>ss\u003c/code> replaces \u003ccode>netstat\u003c/code>. States to know: \u003ccode>ESTABLISHED\u003c/code> (active), \u003ccode>SYN-SENT\u003c/code> (connecting, no answer yet), \u003ccode>SYN-RECV\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>TIME-WAIT\u003c/code> (closed connection kept briefly to catch late packets; large numbers are normal on busy clients) and \u003ccode>CLOSE-WAIT\u003c/code> (the remote side closed but \u003cstrong>your application has not closed the socket\u003c/strong>: a bug when it piles up). \u003ccode>-p\u003c/code> shows the process, \u003ccode>-i\u003c/code> shows TCP internals such as retransmissions and round-trip time.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ports-and-timeouts\">Ports and timeouts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A client uses one local port per connection to the same destination. The range comes from \u003ccode>net.ipv4.ip_local_port_range\u003c/code> (often about 28,000 ports). A client making many short connections to one server can run out, giving \u003ccode>Cannot assign requested address\u003c/code>. The cure is connection reuse (keep-alive, pooling), not simply a bigger range. Keepalive settings (\u003ccode>net.ipv4.tcp_keepalive_time\u003c/code>) control how long idle connections are probed; load balancers and NAT devices drop idle connections silently, so applications should use their own keepalive or shorter timeouts.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"netfilter-and-conntrack\">Netfilter and conntrack\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Firewall rules and NAT (iptables, nftables, Kubernetes service routing) rely on \u003cstrong>connection tracking\u003c/strong>: the kernel remembers every flow in a table.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo conntrack -L | head\nsudo conntrack -C\nsysctl net.netfilter.nf_conntrack_max\ndmesg -T | grep conntrack\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> conntrack -L | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> conntrack -C\nsysctl net.netfilter.nf_conntrack_max\ndmesg -T | grep conntrack\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>If \u003ccode>conntrack -C\u003c/code> approaches \u003ccode>nf_conntrack_max\u003c/code>, new connections are dropped with \u003ccode>table full\u003c/code> in the kernel log. Fixes: raise the limit (it costs memory), shorten timeouts for UDP and closed flows, avoid tracking traffic that does not need it, and reduce connection churn. On busy NAT gateways and Kubernetes nodes this is a classic cause of mysterious packet loss.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> ss -ltn shows Recv-Q 129 and Send-Q 128 on port 8080. What is happening?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The accept queue is full: the application is not calling accept fast enough, so connections are dropped or delayed. Look at app threads, CPU and the backlog.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Recv-Q above Send-Q on a listener means overflow.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-thousands-of-close-wait\">Incident: Thousands of CLOSE_WAIT\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A service gradually stopped accepting new work and file descriptors ran out.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ss -tan state close-wait | wc -l\nls /proc/$(pidof orders)/fd | wc -l\nss -tanp state close-wait | head -2\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ss -tan state close-wait | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">28450\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> /proc/$(pidof orders)/fd | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">65530\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ss -tanp state close-wait | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">CLOSE-WAIT 1 0 10.0.2.15:41222 10.0.9.3:5432 users:((&quot;orders&quot;,pid=4121,fd=311))\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The application did not close database connections after errors, so the sockets stayed in CLOSE_WAIT until the process ran out of file descriptors. The fix closed connections in a finally block, used a pool with limits and idle timeouts, and added alerts on CLOSE_WAIT count and open descriptors.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Blaming the network for application accept delays\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating TIME_WAIT as a bug\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring CLOSE_WAIT build-up\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Raising conntrack limits without checking memory\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"sockets-and-queues","text":"Sockets and queues"},{"id":"tcp-states-with-ss","text":"TCP states with ss"},{"id":"ports-and-timeouts","text":"Ports and timeouts"},{"id":"netfilter-and-conntrack","text":"Netfilter and conntrack"},{"id":"incident-thousands-of-close-wait","text":"Incident: Thousands of CLOSE_WAIT"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"ss(8) man page","u":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html"},{"t":"Netfilter conntrack sysctl","u":"https://docs.kernel.org/networking/nf_conntrack-sysctl.html"}],"text":"When a service drops connections, applications often see only timeouts. The truth is in the kernel: full accept queues, sockets stuck in TIME_WAIT, exhausted ports, or a connection table that is full. A small set of commands shows these structures directly, and a few kernel parameters control them. What you will learn Sockets, ports and the listen and accept queues Read ss output and TCP states Port exhaustion, TIME_WAIT and keepalives Netfilter and conntrack, and what happens when the table fills Sockets and queues A socket is the kernel object behind a connection. A server calls listen , and incoming connections wait in a queue until the application calls accept . If the application is slow, the queue fills (its size is limited by the backlog argument and net.core.somaxconn ), and new connections are dropped or delayed, so clients see timeouts although the server appears fine. ss -ltn State Recv-Q Send-Q Local Address:Port LISTEN 129 128 0.0.0.0:8080 For listening sockets, Recv-Q is the current accept queue length and Send-Q its maximum. Here 129 is above 128: the queue is overflowing. TCP states with ss ss -s ss -tan state established | wc -l ss -tan state time-wait | wc -l ss -tanp | grep :5432 | head ss -ti dst 10.0.1.20 ss replaces netstat . States to know: ESTABLISHED (active), SYN-SENT (connecting, no answer yet), SYN-RECV , TIME-WAIT (closed connection kept briefly to catch late packets; large numbers are normal on busy clients) and CLOSE-WAIT (the remote side closed but your application has not closed the socket : a bug when it piles up). -p shows the process, -i shows TCP internals such as retransmissions and round-trip time. Ports and timeouts A client uses one local port per connection to the same destination. The range comes from net.ipv4.ip_local_port_range (often about 28,000 ports). A client making many short connections to one server can run out, giving Cannot assign requested address . The cure is connection reuse (keep-alive, pooling), not simply a bigger range. Keepalive settings ( net.ipv4.tcp_keepalive_time ) control how long idle connections are probed; load balancers and NAT devices drop idle connections silently, so applications should use their own keepalive or shorter timeouts. Netfilter and conntrack Firewall rules and NAT (iptables, nftables, Kubernetes service routing) rely on connection tracking : the kernel remembers every flow in a table. sudo conntrack -L | head sudo conntrack -C sysctl net.netfilter.nf_conntrack_max dmesg -T | grep conntrack If conntrack -C approaches nf_conntrack_max , new connections are dropped with table full in the kernel log. Fixes: raise the limit (it costs memory), shorten timeouts for UDP and closed flows, avoid tracking traffic that does not need it, and reduce connection churn. On busy NAT gateways and Kubernetes nodes this is a classic cause of mysterious packet loss. Predict first. ss -ltn shows Recv-Q 129 and Send-Q 128 on port 8080. What is happening? Your guess Answer: The accept queue is full: the application is not calling accept fast enough, so connections are dropped or delayed. Look at app threads, CPU and the backlog. Recv-Q above Send-Q on a listener means overflow. Incident: Thousands of CLOSE_WAIT Incident A service gradually stopped accepting new work and file descriptors ran out. $ ss -tan state close-wait | wc -l 28450 $ ls /proc/$(pidof orders)/fd | wc -l 65530 $ ss -tanp state close-wait | head -2 CLOSE-WAIT 1 0 10.0.2.15:41222 10.0.9.3:5432 users:((\"orders\",pid=4121,fd=311)) The application did not close database connections after errors, so the sockets stayed in CLOSE_WAIT until the process ran out of file descriptors. The fix closed connections in a finally block, used a pool with limits and idle timeouts, and added alerts on CLOSE_WAIT count and open descriptors. Common mistakes Blaming the network for application accept delays Treating TIME_WAIT as a bug Ignoring CLOSE_WAIT build-up Raising conntrack limits without checking memory"},"linux/debugging-a-sick-server":{"html":"\u003cp>A server is &quot;slow&quot;. That word hides a dozen different problems: a runaway process, memory pressure, a full disk, a saturated network link, or a dependency that stopped answering. Guessing wastes time. Experienced engineers walk the same short checklist every time, so that the machine itself tells them where the trouble is.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A repeatable order of checks for a sick server\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The one command that answers each question\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to read load, memory, disk and network numbers without being fooled\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to attach to a misbehaving process and see what it is doing\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"start-with-the-questions-not-the-tools\">Start with the questions, not the tools\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Ask four things, in this order: what changed recently, what do the logs say, which resource is saturated, and which process is responsible. The \u003cstrong>USE method\u003c/strong> helps with the third: for every resource, check Utilisation, Saturation and Errors.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Resource\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Quick check\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Warning sign\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>CPU\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>uptime\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>top\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Load average far above the number of cores\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Memory\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>free -m\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>dmesg -T\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Very little available memory, swap in use, OOM kills\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Disk space\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>df -h\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>df -i\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>100 percent used, or no free inodes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Disk speed\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>iostat -x 1 3\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>High \u003ccode>%util\u003c/code> and long \u003ccode>await\u003c/code> times\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Network\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>ss -s\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>ip -s link\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Many connections in odd states, errors or drops\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-first-two-minutes\">The first two minutes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"uptime\nnproc\ntop -b -n1 | head -12\nfree -m\ndf -h /var; df -i /var\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">uptime\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 14:03:11 up 41 days,  3:12,  2 users,  load average: 9.80, 8.40, 5.10\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">nproc\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>top -b -n1 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -12\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>free -m\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">              total   used   free  shared  buff/cache  available\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Mem:           7822   7410    112      12         300         95\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">df\u003c/span> -h /var; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">df\u003c/span> -i /var\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A load of 9.8 on 4 cores means work is queueing. Load counts processes that want CPU and also those blocked on disk, so look at \u003ccode>top\u003c/code> to see which it is: high \u003ccode>%us\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>%sy\u003c/code> means CPU; high \u003ccode>%wa\u003c/code> means waiting for disk. In \u003ccode>free -m\u003c/code>, trust the \u003cstrong>available\u003c/strong> column, not &quot;free&quot;, because the kernel uses spare memory as cache.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"find-the-process-and-see-what-it-does\">Find the process and see what it does\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ps aux --sort=-%mem | head -5          # biggest memory users\nss -tnp state established | head       # who is connected, and which process owns it\nlsof -p 4321 | head -20                # files and sockets a process has open\nsudo strace -p 4321 -f -tt -e trace=network,file   # live system calls (use briefly, it slows the process)\njournalctl -u shop --since &quot;-15min&quot;    # what the service itself reported\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ps aux --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span>=-%mem | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -5          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># biggest memory users\u003c/span>\nss -tnp state established | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>       \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># who is connected, and which process owns it\u003c/span>\nlsof -p 4321 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -20                \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># files and sockets a process has open\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> strace -p 4321 -f -tt -e trace=network,file   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># live system calls (use briefly, it slows the process)\u003c/span>\njournalctl -u shop --since \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;-15min&quot;\u003c/span>    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># what the service itself reported\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>strace\u003c/code> shows what a process is asking the kernel to do. If it repeats the same failing call, you have found the loop. Remove it as soon as you have the answer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> df -h shows 40 percent free space on /var, yet the application logs &quot;No space left on device&quot;. What should you check next?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Run df -i to see whether the filesystem has run out of inodes.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A filesystem can be out of inodes (file entries) while still having free blocks. Millions of tiny files, such as session files or mail queues, cause this. Find the directory with many files and clean or rotate it.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"ask&gt;logs;ask&gt;use;logs&gt;proc;use&gt;proc;proc&gt;fix\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A fixed order for a sick server\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ask\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">What changed?\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"logs\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Logs and kernel messages\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"use\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">CPU, memory, disk, network\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"proc\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Responsible process\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"fix\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Fix and verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, What changed? to Logs and kernel messages; 2, What changed? to CPU, memory, disk, network; 3, Logs and kernel messages to Responsible process; 4, CPU, memory, disk, network to Responsible process; 5, Responsible process to Fix and verify.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ask\">\u003cdt>What changed? \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Deploys, config edits, package updates and traffic changes in the last hours.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipping this step means hours spent on a cause that was introduced this morning.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"logs\">\u003cdt>Logs and kernel messages \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> journalctl and dmesg show crashes, OOM kills and disk errors with timestamps.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Logs rotated away or never kept leave you guessing.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"use\">\u003cdt>CPU, memory, disk, network \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> uptime, free, df, iostat and ss find the saturated resource.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Reading the wrong column, such as free instead of available memory, misleads you.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"proc\">\u003cdt>Responsible process \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> ps, top, lsof and strace link the saturation to one program.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Killing the wrong process fixes nothing and may hide the evidence.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"fix\">\u003cdt>Fix and verify \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Change one thing, then confirm the metric returns to normal.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Several changes at once leave you unsure what worked.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-server-with-free-space-and-no-space\">Incident: the server with free space and no space\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Uploads fail at 09:40 with &quot;No space left on device&quot;. Disk graphs show 60 percent free.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"df -h /srv\ndf -i /srv\nsudo find /srv/sessions -type f | wc -l\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">df\u003c/span> -h /srv\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">/dev/sdb1   200G  120G   80G  60% /srv\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">df\u003c/span> -i /srv\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Filesystem   Inodes   IUsed IFree IUse% Mounted on\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">/dev/sdb1   13107200 13107200    0  100% /srv\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> find /srv/sessions -\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">type\u003c/span> f | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">12984311\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The session directory held almost 13 million tiny files, and a cleanup job had been failing for weeks. The team removed files older than a day, fixed the job, added an alert on inode use, and moved sessions to a store designed for them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Restarting the service before collecting evidence (logs, a thread dump, \u003ccode>ss\u003c/code> output)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading &quot;free&quot; memory instead of &quot;available&quot;\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Changing several settings at once\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving \u003ccode>strace\u003c/code> or debug logging on after the investigation\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"start-with-the-questions-not-the-tools","text":"Start with the questions, not the tools"},{"id":"the-first-two-minutes","text":"The first two minutes"},{"id":"find-the-process-and-see-what-it-does","text":"Find the process and see what it does"},{"id":"incident-the-server-with-free-space-and-no-space","text":"Incident: the server with free space and no space"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Brendan Gregg: USE Method","u":"https://www.brendangregg.com/usemethod.html"},{"t":"strace(1) manual page","u":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/strace.1.html"}],"text":"A server is \"slow\". That word hides a dozen different problems: a runaway process, memory pressure, a full disk, a saturated network link, or a dependency that stopped answering. Guessing wastes time. Experienced engineers walk the same short checklist every time, so that the machine itself tells them where the trouble is. What you will learn A repeatable order of checks for a sick server The one command that answers each question How to read load, memory, disk and network numbers without being fooled How to attach to a misbehaving process and see what it is doing Start with the questions, not the tools Ask four things, in this order: what changed recently, what do the logs say, which resource is saturated, and which process is responsible. The USE method helps with the third: for every resource, check Utilisation, Saturation and Errors. Resource Quick check Warning sign CPU uptime , top Load average far above the number of cores Memory free -m , dmesg -T Very little available memory, swap in use, OOM kills Disk space df -h and df -i 100 percent used, or no free inodes Disk speed iostat -x 1 3 High %util and long await times Network ss -s , ip -s link Many connections in odd states, errors or drops The first two minutes $ uptime 14:03:11 up 41 days, 3:12, 2 users, load average: 9.80, 8.40, 5.10 $ nproc 4 $ top -b -n1 | head -12 $ free -m total used free shared buff/cache available Mem: 7822 7410 112 12 300 95 $ df -h /var; df -i /var A load of 9.8 on 4 cores means work is queueing. Load counts processes that want CPU and also those blocked on disk, so look at top to see which it is: high %us / %sy means CPU; high %wa means waiting for disk. In free -m , trust the available column, not \"free\", because the kernel uses spare memory as cache. Find the process and see what it does ps aux -- sort =-%mem | head -5 # biggest memory users ss -tnp state established | head # who is connected, and which process owns it lsof -p 4321 | head -20 # files and sockets a process has open sudo strace -p 4321 -f -tt -e trace=network,file # live system calls (use briefly, it slows the process) journalctl -u shop --since \"-15min\" # what the service itself reported strace shows what a process is asking the kernel to do. If it repeats the same failing call, you have found the loop. Remove it as soon as you have the answer. Predict first. df -h shows 40 percent free space on /var, yet the application logs \"No space left on device\". What should you check next? Your guess Answer: Run df -i to see whether the filesystem has run out of inodes. A filesystem can be out of inodes (file entries) while still having free blocks. Millions of tiny files, such as session files or mail queues, cause this. Find the directory with many files and clean or rotate it. A fixed order for a sick server Flow in order: 1, What changed? to Logs and kernel messages; 2, What changed? to CPU, memory, disk, network; 3, Logs and kernel messages to Responsible process; 4, CPU, memory, disk, network to Responsible process; 5, Responsible process to Fix and verify. Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. What changed? Step What it does: Deploys, config edits, package updates and traffic changes in the last hours. If it fails or is weak: Skipping this step means hours spent on a cause that was introduced this morning. Logs and kernel messages Observability What it does: journalctl and dmesg show crashes, OOM kills and disk errors with timestamps. If it fails or is weak: Logs rotated away or never kept leave you guessing. CPU, memory, disk, network Step What it does: uptime, free, df, iostat and ss find the saturated resource. If it fails or is weak: Reading the wrong column, such as free instead of available memory, misleads you. Responsible process Step What it does: ps, top, lsof and strace link the saturation to one program. If it fails or is weak: Killing the wrong process fixes nothing and"}});